{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_014SZ438BvFP9uQFJ2jjrSBT","type":"tool_result","content":"     1\t% Section 1. Written 2026-10-06 from the first author's thesis\n     2\t% (literature/linhares_2001_phd_thesis_industrial_pattern_sequencing.pdf)\n     3\t% and the owner's account of how its Table 2.1 was assembled.\n     4\t\\section{Introduction}\n     5\t\\label{sec:intro}\n     6\t\n     7\tIn the minimization of open stacks problem (MOSP), patterns are cut in some\n     8\torder, every piece type has a stack that opens with the first pattern that\n     9\tcontains it and closes with the last, and the task is to choose the order that\n    10\tkeeps the fewest stacks open at once. The first author's doctoral thesis\n    11\t\\citep{Linhares2001}, supervised by the second, was about this problem and its\n    12\trelatives in industrial pattern sequencing. Its complexity chapter proves two\n    13\tthings. First, MOSP is NP-hard, by a reduction from modified cutwidth: padding\n    14\tthe edge--vertex incidence matrix of a graph so that every column has the same\n    15\tsum $C$ gives an instance whose number of open stacks is the modified cutwidth\n    16\tplus $C$ \\citep[Proposition~2.1 and Lemma~2.2]{Linhares2001}. Second, the number\n    17\tof open stacks equals the number of tracks of a gate matrix layout, ``trivially\n    18\tfrom their definitions'' (Proposition~2.2). From the second it draws, as a\n    19\tcorollary of the fixed-parameter tractability of gate matrix layout\n    20\t\\citep{FellowsLangston1987}, that MOSP is fixed-parameter tractable\n    21\t(Corollary~2.1).\n    22\t\n    23\tBetween those two results the thesis prints a table (Table~2.1, p.~13). Its\n    24\ttwelve rows are problems from operations research, VLSI design and graph theory\n    25\tthat compute, on corresponding inputs, ``a function $f(\\Phi)$ that is either\n    26\tequal to the number of open stacks or closely related to it (plus or minus\n    27\tone)''. The rows were collected over the years of the doctorate, while reading\n    28\tthe literature of each field. Each comes with a reference and nothing else. No\n    29\trow's relation to open stacks was checked, no published proof was restated,\n    30\tand no graph says which problem reduces to which. The table appeared again,\n    31\trow for row and with the same sentence introducing it, as Table~1 of\n    32\t\\citet{LY2002}, and it has been read since as a statement of equivalence.\n    33\t\\citet{GarciaDeLaBandaStuckey2007}, for instance, credit the speed of their\n    34\tfixed-$N$ search in this journal to ``the fact that the problem is fixed\n    35\tparameter tractable (Linhares and Yanasse 2002)''. The thesis's own summary of\n    36\tfindings (\\S7.1) does not mention the table at all.\n    37\t\n    38\tLooking back, the table was the thesis's most important finding. Twelve\n    39\tproblems from three disciplines, each studied independently, are claimed to\n    40\tbe one problem, and if that is true, then every result about any one of them\n    41\ttransfers to all the others. This article does what the thesis did not: it\n    42\tstates each problem precisely, proves what is true and refutes what is not,\n    43\tand then takes the strongest exact algorithm in the family through the same\n    44\tcheck.\n    45\t\n    46\t\\paragraph{The pathwidth complex.} \\citet{LY2002} collected, in their\n    47\tTable~1, twelve problems from operations research, VLSI design and graph theory\n    48\tthat had been studied independently and that compute, on corresponding inputs,\n    49\t``a function $f(\\Pi)$ that is either equal to the number of open stacks or\n    50\tclosely related to it (plus or minus one)'' \\citep[p.~1764]{LY2002}. The table\n    51\tgave references, but no proofs and no graph. Table~\\ref{tab:ly2002} reproduces\n    52\tit as printed. We call this cluster \\emph{the pathwidth complex}. Pathwidth is\n    53\tboth the name the literature uses most (Section~\\ref{sec:names}) and the\n    54\tquantity through which every relation in the table is proved\n    55\t(Section~\\ref{sec:equiv}). Of the twelve rows, one is pathwidth itself, seven\n    56\tare exactly pathwidth plus a constant, two lie within a band of width one or\n    57\ttwo, and two are false as Table~1 states them (Table~\\ref{tab:master}).\n    58\t\n    59\t\\begin{table}[tbp]\n    60\t\\centering\n    61\t\\caption{Display of a family of equivalent problems studied independently on\n    62\tthe literature. Reproduced from \\citet[Table~1, p.~1764]{LY2002}, with the\n    63\tcaption, row order and wording as printed; the references are the ones the\n    64\toriginal cites, given here by author and year.}\n    65\t\\label{tab:ly2002}\n    66\t\\small\n    67\t\\begin{tabular}{@{}lll@{}}\n    68\t\\toprule\n    69\tProblem & Discipline & References \\\\\n    70\t\\midrule\n    71\tMOSP & Operations research & \\citet{Yanasse1997b}; \\citet{LY2002ref4} \\\\\n    72\tGate matrix layout & VLSI design & \\citet{LY2002ref6}; \\citet{LY2002ref8} \\\\\n    73\tOne-dimensional logic & VLSI design & \\citet{LY2002ref7} \\\\\n    74\tPLA folding & VLSI design & \\citet{LY2002ref6} \\\\\n    75\tInterval thickness & Graph theory & \\citet{LY2002ref5} \\\\\n    76\tNode search game & Graph theory & \\citet{LY2002ref9} \\\\\n    77\tEdge search game & Graph theory & \\citet{LY2002ref10} \\\\\n    78\tNarrowness & Graph theory & \\citet{LY2002ref11} \\\\\n    79\tSplit bandwidth & Graph theory & \\citet{LY2002ref12} \\\\\n    80\tGraph path-width & Graph theory & \\citet{LY2002ref13} \\\\\n    81\tEdge separation & Graph theory & \\citet{LY2002ref14} \\\\\n    82\tVertex separation & Graph theory & \\citet{LY2002ref13} \\\\\n    83\t\\bottomrule\n    84\t\\end{tabular}\n    85\t\\end{table}\n    86\t\n    87\t\\paragraph{Contributions.}\n    88\t\\begin{itemize}\n    89\t\\item A census of how much each of the twelve names is used, after removing the\n    90\t  search hits that use the words in other senses; pathwidth has more relevant\n    91\t  works than the other eleven names together, three times over\n    92\t  (Section~\\ref{sec:names}).\n    93\t\\item Each problem of Table~1 stated as an instance and a question, with the\n    94\t  graph it lives on and its exact relation to pathwidth, every relation proved\n    95\t  in Lean; two of Table~1's rows are false as stated (Section~\\ref{sec:equiv}).\n    96\t\\item The exact open-stacks search of \\citet{ChuStuckey2009} read as a\n    97\t  vertex-separation search: two of its published dominance theorems are false,\n    98\t  as are their restatements by \\citet{Chu2011} and \\citet{Fink2012}; the repair,\n    99\t  its cost, and a Lean proof that the repaired search is sound; and a proof\n   100\t  object for its refutations with an independent checker\n   101\t  (Section~\\ref{sec:search}).\n   102\t\\item A dataset of 17,714 isomorphism classes from twenty collections, 16,087 of\n   103\t  them with a certified pathwidth and a witness layout, which through\n   104\t  Section~\\ref{sec:equiv} answers every problem in Table~1's exact core\n   105\t  (Section~\\ref{sec:dataset}).\n   106\t\\end{itemize}\n   107\t\n   108\t\\paragraph{Notation.} Graphs are finite and simple, $G=(V,E)$, $n=|V|$. A\n   109\t\\emph{layout} is a bijection $L:V\\to\\{1,\\dots,n\\}$. For a 0/1 matrix $M$ with\n   110\trows $R$ (customers, nets, piece types) and columns $C$ (patterns, gates,\n   111\tproducts), the \\emph{MOSP graph} $G_M$ has vertex set $R$, with $r\\sim r'$ when\n   112\tsome column has a 1 in both rows; each column becomes a clique on its rows. For\n   113\tan order $\\pi$ of the columns, row $r$ is \\emph{active} at position $j$ if it\n   114\thas 1s in columns $x,y$ with $\\pi(x)\\le j\\le\\pi(y)$.\n     1\t% Section 2. Sources: paper2/popularity.md (\"For the paper\", the method\n     2\t% paragraph, the sensitivity check), numbers regenerated by\n     3\t% `python -m paper2.section2_figures --numbers`, figures by\n     4\t% `python -m paper2.section2_figures`, the table by\n     5\t% `python -m paper2.latex.make_tables`.\n     6\t\\section{One complex, twelve names}\n     7\t\\label{sec:names}\n     8\t\n     9\tTable~1 of \\citet{LY2002} gathers twelve problems from three disciplines. From\n    10\toperations research it takes the minimization of open stacks (MOSP); from VLSI\n    11\tdesign, gate matrix layout, PLA folding and one-dimensional logic; from graph\n    12\ttheory, pathwidth, vertex separation, interval thickness, node search, edge\n    13\tsearch, narrowness, split bandwidth and edge separation. Section~\\ref{sec:equiv}\n    14\tshows which of them are in fact one quantity. This section asks a prior\n    15\tquestion: when the literature studies that quantity, which name does it use?\n    16\tThe answer gives the paper its title. Taken together, the problems are one\n    17\tobject seen from three disciplines, and the name the literature has converged\n    18\ton is pathwidth.\n    19\t\n    20\t\\subsection{Method}\n    21\t\\label{sec:names-method}\n    22\t\n    23\tWe measured how much each name is used with OpenAlex \\citep{Priem2022}, on\n    24\t29 September 2026. For each of the twelve problems we searched titles and\n    25\tabstracts for its name and its common variants, and restricted the search to\n    26\tthe four fields in which the problems are studied: Computer Science,\n    27\tMathematics, Engineering and Decision Sciences. Without that restriction\n    28\tseveral names are ordinary words: ``narrowness'' returns over a million works,\n    29\tand ``edge separation'' returns aerodynamics.\n    30\t\n    31\tInside those fields a search hit is still not a work that uses the name.\n    32\tOpenAlex's search stems words and ignores punctuation, so a quoted phrase\n    33\tmatches more than the phrase. ``Narrowness'' matches ``narrowing'' in term\n    34\trewriting; ``node searching'' matches look-ups in peer-to-peer networks;\n    35\t``split bandwidth'' matches radar interferometry and 5G spectrum assignment;\n    36\t``path-width'' matches the widths of roads, tool paths and datapaths. We\n    37\ttherefore fetched all 2,271 hits with their titles, abstracts, topics and\n    38\tvenues, and kept a work only if it is about the problem in the sense of\n    39\tTable~1. For the eleven smaller names the 700 hits were judged one by one. For\n    40\tpathwidth, the 1,571 hits were filtered by a rule: keep a work that uses the\n    41\tone-word spelling, that OpenAlex places in a theory subfield, or that mentions\n    42\tgraphs, treewidth or minors and not roads, vehicles, trajectories or datapaths.\n    43\tWe checked the rule by reading samples of what it kept and what it dropped.\n    44\t\n    45\tThe check changed the counts and two of the conclusions. Of the 2,271 hits,\n    46\t1,593 are relevant: 1,213 for pathwidth and 380 for the other eleven\n    47\tnames.\\footnote{OpenAlex returned one pathwidth record twice. The counts are of\n    48\trecords, as the figures draw them; there are 1,212 distinct relevant pathwidth\n    49\tworks, 1,592 in all.} Three names have no relevant work at all. The two\n    50\tsearch-game names keep 27\\% and 40\\% of their hits. MOSP moves from seventh\n    51\tplace to fourth.\n    52\t\n    53\tThe counts are lower bounds. A work can study a problem without naming it in\n    54\tits abstract, OpenAlex has few abstracts before about 1990, and its citation\n    55\tcounts run below Google Scholar's. The labels were made in one\n    56\tmodel-assisted session, record a yes or no per work and not the reason, and\n    57\thave not been checked by a second labeller. As a second reading we applied a\n    58\tmechanical rule, the literal phrase with a context term and no exclusion term.\n    59\tIt agrees with the labels on 543 of the 700 labelled works (78\\%). Under both\n    60\treadings pathwidt