{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_013jbDZNGabzpVnLFxCX8gfP","type":"tool_result","content":"     1\t% Section 3. Sources: paper2/problem_transformations.md (problems, statements,\n     2\t% proofs), paper2/equivalences.md (master table, sources, gaps),\n     3\t% paper2/proof_reductions.md (faults in published proofs). Lean:\n     4\t% lean/MOSPFormalization/ and Complex/; axioms printed by paper2/axiom_check.lean.\n     5\t\\section{The equivalences, proved}\n     6\t\\label{sec:equiv}\n     7\t\n     8\t\\citet[p.~1764]{LY2002} introduce Table~1 as ``a set of problems that consist\n     9\tof, given input $\\Pi$, compute a function $f(\\Pi)$ that is either equal to the\n    10\tnumber of open stacks or closely related to it (plus or minus one)''. The table\n    11\tcites where each problem was studied. It gives no proofs and no graph. Two\n    12\treadings of ``plus or minus one'' must be kept apart. Under the \\emph{literal}\n    13\treading, $f$ lies within one of $Z$, the number of open stacks of the\n    14\tcorresponding instance, on every input. Under the \\emph{fixed-offset} reading,\n    15\t$f = Z + c$ for one constant $c\\in\\{-1,0,1\\}$ on every input. Only the second\n    16\tmakes computing one problem the same as computing another.\n    17\t\n    18\tThis section states each problem as an instance and a question, gives the\n    19\trelation to pathwidth that is true, and says where it is proved. Every relation\n    20\tstated below is a theorem of our Lean development unless it says otherwise. All\n    21\tthe theorems named depend only on the axioms \\texttt{propext},\n    22\t\\texttt{Classical.choice} and \\texttt{Quot.sound}.\\footnote{Printed by\n    23\t\\texttt{lake env lean paper2/axiom\\_check.lean}, which covers every theorem\n    24\tnamed in this paper. The development has one\n    25\t\\texttt{sorry}, a conjecture about lower bounds that no result of this paper\n    26\tuses.} Table~\\ref{tab:master} summarises the result and\n    27\tFigure~\\ref{fig:chain} draws it.\n    28\t\n    29\t\\subsection{The problems}\n    30\t\\label{sec:problems}\n    31\t\n    32\tEach problem below is a decision problem with an instance and an integer $k$;\n    33\tits value is the least $k$ for which the answer is yes.\n    34\t\n    35\t\\begin{description}\n    36\t\\item[Pathwidth $\\pw(G)$.] Is there a sequence of bags $X_1,\\dots,X_r\\subseteq V$\n    37\t  covering $V$, with every edge inside some bag, $X_i\\cap X_\\ell\\subseteq X_j$\n    38\t  whenever $i\\le j\\le\\ell$, and $|X_i|\\le k+1$? \\citep[p.~346]{LY2002ref13}.\n    39\t\\item[Vertex separation $\\vs(G)$.] Is there a layout $L$ such that for every\n    40\t  $i<n$ at most $k$ vertices $u$ with $L(u)\\le i$ have a neighbour $v$ with\n    41\t  $L(v)>i$? \\citep[p.~346]{LY2002ref13}.\n    42\t\\item[MOSP $Z(M)$.] Given a 0/1 matrix $M$ of customers by patterns, is there\n    43\t  an order of the patterns with at most $k$ customers active at every position?\n    44\t  \\citep[eqs.~(1)--(2)]{LY2002}.\n    45\t\\item[Gate matrix layout $t(M)$.] Given a net--gate matrix, is there an order of\n    46\t  the gates and an assignment of nets to $k$ tracks such that nets active at a\n    47\t  common position get different tracks? \\citep[p.~18]{LY2002ref6};\n    48\t  \\citep[Problem~1]{LY2002ref8}.\n    49\t\\item[One-dimensional logic.] Given gates, nets and the nets each gate connects,\n    50\t  is there an order of the gates whose net intervals have an interval graph of\n    51\t  clique number at most $k$? Its \\emph{connection graph} $H$ joins two nets that\n    52\t  share a gate. A \\emph{boundary} variant pins two gates to the ends\n    53\t  \\citep[\\S\\S II--IV]{LY2002ref7}.\n    54\t\\item[PLA folding $\\pla(M)$.] As gate matrix layout, with at most two nets per\n    55\t  track. With no such bound (\\emph{multiple folding}) it is gate matrix layout\n    56\t  itself \\citep[pp.~25, 36]{LY2002ref6}.\n    57\t\\item[Interval thickness $\\theta(G)$.] Is there an interval supergraph of $G$ on\n    58\t  the same vertices with clique number at most $k$?\n    59\t  \\citep[p.~182]{LY2002ref9}; \\citep[p.~31]{LY2002ref6}.\n    60\t\\item[Node search $\\ns(G)$.] Can searchers, placed on and removed from vertices,\n    61\t  clear every edge with at most $k$ on the graph at once, where an edge is\n    62\t  cleared when both ends are guarded and a clear edge is recontaminated by a\n    63\t  searcher-free path to a contaminated one? $\\mns(G)$ is the same over strategies\n    64\t  that never recontaminate \\citep[p.~181]{LY2002ref9}; \\citep[\\S2]{LY2002ref10}.\n    65\t\\item[Edge search $\\es(G)$.] The same game with a third move, sliding a searcher\n    66\t  along an edge, which clears that edge; $\\pes(G)$ for progressive strategies\n    67\t  \\citep[p.~208]{LY2002ref10}; \\citep[p.~53]{EllisSudboroughTurner1994}.\n    68\t\\item[Narrowness $\\nu(G)$.] Is there an order $v_1,\\dots,v_n$ such that the\n    69\t  following process never holds more than $k$ vertices: at step $i$ add $v_i$,\n    70\t  then remove every $v_j$, $j\\le i$, with no neighbour $v_\\ell$, $\\ell>i$?\n    71\t  \\citep[\\S2]{LY2002ref11}.\n    72\t\\item[Split bandwidth $\\sbw(G)$.] Is there a graph obtained from $G$ by\n    73\t  repeatedly splitting a vertex into an edge, its neighbours partitioned between\n    74\t  the two ends, with bandwidth at most $k$? \\citep[\\S3.2]{LY2002ref12}.\n    75\t\\item[Edge separation.] Table~1 cites \\citet{LY2002ref14}, which supports three\n    76\t  readings: cutwidth $\\cw(G)$, the least over layouts of the most edges crossing\n    77\t  a gap (p.~468); modified cutwidth $\\mcw(G)$, counting only edges that pass\n    78\t  strictly over a vertex (Definition~6); and Lengauer's vertex separator game\n    79\t  $\\VSG(G)$, the least $K$ such that the vertices can be pebbled in an order that\n    80\t  leaves at most $K$ unpebbled vertices adjacent to pebbled ones (p.~467).\n    81\t\\end{description}\n    82\t\n    83\t\\subsection{What is true}\n    84\t\n    85\t\\begin{table}[t]\n    86\t\\centering\\small\n    87\t\\caption{Table~1 of \\citet{LY2002}, settled. \\emph{Relation}: what is true and\n    88\tproved in Lean, on the graph the problem lives on (the MOSP graph or net graph\n    89\tfor the matrix problems, the input graph otherwise). \\emph{Status}:\n    90\t\\emph{exact}, a fixed offset; \\emph{band}, an inequality whose ends both occur;\n    91\t\\emph{false}, Table~1's claim fails, with a counterexample family proved in\n    92\tLean.}\n    93\t\\label{tab:master}\n    94\t\\begin{tabularx}{\\linewidth}{@{}>{\\raggedright}p{2.7cm}>{\\raggedright}p{3.4cm}>{\\raggedright\\arraybackslash}X l@{}}\n    95\t\\toprule\n    96\tProblem & Proved in & Relation & Status \\\\\n    97\t\\midrule\n    98\tMOSP & \\citet{Yanasse1997a}; \\citet{FellowsLangston1989}; \\citet{LY2002} &\n    99\t  $Z(M)=\\pw(G_M)+1$ ($M$ has a 1) & exact \\\\\n   100\tGate matrix layout & \\citet{LY2002ref6} & $t(M)=Z(M)=\\pw(G_M)+1$ & exact \\\\\n   101\tOne-dim.\\ logic & \\citet{LY2002ref7} & tracks $=\\theta(H)=\\pw(H)+1$; boundary variant $\\ge\\pw(H)+1$ & exact \\\\\n   102\tPLA folding & \\citet{LY2002ref6} & $\\max(\\pw+1,\\lceil|N|/2\\rceil)\\le\\pla$, gap unbounded; multiple folding $=\\pw+1$ & false; variant exact \\\\\n   103\tInterval thickness & \\citet{LY2002ref6}; \\citet{LY2002ref9} & $\\theta=\\pw+1$ ($V\\ne\\emptyset$) & exact \\\\\n   104\tNode search & \\citet{LY2002ref9,LY2002ref10}; \\citet{BienstockSeymour1991} & $\\ns=\\mns=\\vs+1=\\pw+1=\\theta$ ($E\\ne\\emptyset$) & exact \\\\\n   105\tEdge search & \\citet{EllisSudboroughTurner1994}; \\citet{LY2002ref10} & $\\vs\\le\\es\\le\\vs+2$, also for $\\pes$ & band \\\\\n   106\tNarrowness & \\citet{LY2002ref11} & $\\nu=\\pw+1$ ($V\\ne\\emptyset$) & exact \\\\\n   107\tSplit bandwidth & \\citet{LY2002ref12} & $\\pw\\le\\sbw\\le\\pw+1$ & band \\\\\n   108\tPath-width & \\citet{LY2002ref13} & the reference & definition \\\\\n   109\tEdge separation & \\citet{LY2002ref14} & $\\cw,\\mcw$ unbounded against $\\pw$ (stars); $\\VSG=\\max(1,\\vs)$ & false; VSG exact \\\\\n   110\tVertex separation & \\citet{LY2002ref13} & $\\vs=\\pw$ & exact \\\\\n   111\t\\bottomrule\n   112\t\\end{tabularx}\n   113\t\\end{table}\n   114\t\n   115\t\\begin{figure}[t]\n   116\t\\centering\n   117\t\\includegraphics[width=\\linewidth]{sec3_fig1_chain}\n   118\t\\caption{The equivalences of Table~\\ref{tab:master} as a graph around\n   119\tpathwidth. Solid edges are exact relations, dashed edges bands, both proved in\n   120\tLean; the dotted edge is LaPaugh's $\\mathrm{es}=\\mathrm{pes}$, the one relation\n   121\tnot proved, which no row needs. Dark red marks Table~1's two false claims, each\n   122\trefuted by a counterexample family proved in Lean. The four matrix problems (top\n   123\tleft) live on the MOSP graph $G_M$ or the net graph $H$; $\\mathrm{mpb}$ is the minimum\n   124\tprogressive pebbling number of Section~\\ref{sec:thirteenth}. Hypotheses are\n   125\tomitted: $M$ has a 1, $V\\neq\\emptyset$, or $G$ has an edge, as in\n   126\tTable~\\ref{tab:master}. Two edges that follow from the others,\n   127\t$\\mathrm{mns}=\\theta$ and $\\mathrm{ns}=\\mathrm{vs}+1$, are not drawn.}\n   128\t\\label{fig:chain}\n   129\t\\end{figure}\n   130\t\n   131\tTwo lemmas carry most of the exact rows. For a path decomposition and a vertex\n   132\t$v$, let $\\mathrm{first}(v)$ and $\\mathrm{last}(v)$ be the least and greatest\n   133\tindices of bags containing $v$; the decomposition's interval property says\n   134\t$v\\in X_i$ exactly when $\\mathrm{first}(v)\\le i\\le\\mathrm{last}(v)$.\n   135\t\n   136\t\\begin{lemma}[Helly for intervals]\\label{lem:helly}\n   137\tPairwise intersecting intervals of a line have a common point.\n   138\t\\end{lemma}\n   139\t\\begin{proof}\n   140\tLet $a$ be the largest left end. Any two intervals meet, so every right end is\n   141\tat least every left end, and in particular at least $a$. So $a$ lies in all of\n   142\tthem.\n   143\t\\end{proof}\n   144\t\n   145\t\\begin{lemma}[cliques sit in a bag]\\label{lem:clique}\n   146\tIn a path decomposition of $G$, every clique of $G$ lies inside one bag.\\lean{PathDecomposition.exists_bag_of_isClique}\n   147\t\\end{lemma}\n   148\t\\begin{proof}\n   149\tThe bags containing a vertex $u$ of the clique form the interval\n   150\t$[\\mathrm{first}(u),\\mathrm{last}(u)]$. Two vertices of the clique are\n   151\tadjacent, so their edge lies in a bag and their intervals meet. By\n   152\tLemma~\\ref{lem:helly} all the intervals share an index $i$, and the clique lies\n   153\tin $X_i$.\n   154\t\\end{proof}\n   155\t\n   156\t\\begin{theorem}[vertex separation is pathwidth]\\label{thm:vs}\n   157\tFor every graph, $\\vs(G)=\\pw(G)$.\\lean{vertexSeparation_eq_pathwidth}\n   158\t\\end{theorem}\n   159\tThis is Theorem~3.1 of \\citet{LY2002ref13}. From a layout $L$ of separation $k$,\n   160\tthe bags $X_i = V_L(i-1)\\cup\\{L^{-1}(i)\\}$, where $V_L(i)$ is the set the\n   161\tdefinition counts, form a decomposition of width $k$. Conversely, ordering the\n   162\tvertices by $\\mathrm{first}$ gives a layout in which everything counted at a cut\n   163\tlies in the bag where the next vertex first appears, beside that vertex.\n   164\t\n   165\t\\begin{theorem}[MOSP is pathwidth plus one]\\label{thm:mosp}\n   166\tIf $M$ has at least one 1, then $Z(M)=\\pw(G_M)+1$.\\lean{MOSPInstance.mospValue_eq_pathwidth_add_one}\n   167\t\\end{theorem}\n   168\t\\begin{proof}\n   169\t($\\le$) Take a decomposition of $G_M$ of width $k$. The rows of each column form\n   170\ta clique, so by Lemma~\\ref{lem:clique} they lie in a bag $X_{b(c)}$. Order the\n   171\tcolumns by $b(c)$. If row $r$ is active at the position of column $c$, it has\n   172\tcolumns $x,y$ with $b(x)\\le b(c)\\le b(y)$, and $r\\in X_{b(x)}\\cap X_{b(y)}$, so the\n   173\tinterval property gives $r\\in X_{b(c)}$. At most $k+1$ rows are active there.\n   174\t\n   175\t($\\ge$) Take an order with at most $Z$ active rows at every position. The\n   176\tactive sets, with a singleton bag for each row with no 1, form a path\n   177\tdecomposition: rows sharing a column are both active at its position, and a row\n   178\tis active on an interval of positions. Its width is $Z-1$, using $Z\\ge1$.\n   179\t\\end{proof}\n   180\t\n   181\tRead as a cutting problem, Theorem~\\ref{thm:mosp} is Proposition~5 of\n   182\t\\citet{Yanasse1997a}, which turns each pattern into a clique on its piece types;\n   183\tread as a layout problem, it is Theorem~7 of \\citet{FellowsLangston1989}, with\n   184\tthe column-expansion Lemma~4.1 of \\citet{FellowsLangston1987}; and\n   185\t\\citet[Prop.~2]{LY2002} identify MOSP with gate matrix layout. The proof above\n   186\tneeds the Helly property and the interval property, not Hall's theorem. It needs\n   187\tno assumption that the instance is reduced.\n   188\t\n   189\tThe graph matters. The pattern graph, with the patterns as vertices joined when\n   190\tthey share a customer, gives no bound in either direction: one pattern shared by\n   191\tfour customers who each have a private pattern has $Z=4$, while its pattern\n   192\tgraph is the star $K_{1,4}$, of pathwidth~1.\\lean{star_mospValue, star_pathwidth_agreementGraph, star_refutes_pattern_graph_bound} A single\n   193\tcustomer with many patterns errs the other way.\n   194\t\n   195\tThe other exact rows attach to Theorems~\\ref{thm:vs} and~\\ref{thm:mosp}.\n   196\t\n   197\t\\begin{theorem}[the exact core]\\label{thm:core}\n   198\t\\leavevmode\n   199\t\\begin{enumerate}\n   200\t\\item Gate matrix layout is MOSP on the same matrix: if $M$ has a 1,\n   201\t  $t(M)=Z(M)=\\pw(G_M)+1$. For a fixed gate order the fewest tracks equals the\n   202\t  most nets active at one position, by the left-edge algorithm.\\lean{NetGateMatrix.tracks_eq_mospValue, NetGateMatrix.tracks_eq_pathwidth_add_one}\n   203\t\\item Interval thickness: if $V\\ne\\emptyset$, $\\theta(G)=\\pw(G)+1$\n   204\t  \\citep[Prop.~3.5]{LY2002ref6}.\\lean{intervalThickness_eq_pathwidth_add_one}\n   205\t\\item One-dimensional logic: if every net lies on a gate, the fewest tracks is\n   206\t  $\\theta(H)$, and if some gate connects a net it is $\\pw(H)+1$\n   207\t  \\citep[Thm~3]{LY2002ref7}.\\lean{LogicArray.tracks_eq_intervalThickness, LogicArray.tracks_eq_pathwidth_add_one}\n   208\t\\item Narrowness: if $V\\ne\\emptyset$, $\\nu(G)=\\pw(G)+1$, and for each order\n   209\t  $\\sigma$, $\\nu(\\sigma)=\\vs(\\sigma^{\\mathrm{rev}})+1$\n   210\t  \\citep[Prop.~3.1]{LY2002ref11}.\\lean{narrowness_eq_pathwidth_add_one}\n   211\t\\item Node search: for every graph $\\ns(G)=\\mns(G)$, and if $E\\ne\\emptyset$,\n   212\t  $\\ns(G)=\\vs(G)+1=\\pw(G)+1=\\theta(G)$\n   213\t  \\citep[Thm]{LY2002ref9}; \\citep[Thms~2.3, 4.1]{LY2002ref10}.\\lean{nodeSearchMonotonicity, nodeSearch_eq_vertexSeparation_add_one, nodeSearch_chain}\n   214\t\\item Multiple folding: the fewest tracks is $t(M)=\\pw(G_M)+1$\n   215\t  \\citep[Thm~3.14]{LY2002ref6}.\\lean{NetGateMatrix.foldTracks_eq_pathwidth_add_one}\n   216\t\\item Lengauer's vertex separator game: $\\VSG(G)=\\max(1,\\vs(G))$\n   217\t  \\citep[Thm~4]{LY2002ref14}.\\lean{vsg_eq_max}\n   218\t\\end{enumerate}\n   219\t\\end{theorem}\n   220\t\n   221\tThe Lean proof that node search is monotone follows the crusade argument of\n   222\t\\citet{BienstockSeymour1991}, not the reduction to edge search of\n   223\t\\citet{LaPaugh1993} on which \\citet{LY2002ref10} rely.\n   224\t\n   225\t\\begin{theorem}[bands]\\label{thm:bands}\n   226\tFor every graph, $\\pw(G)\\le\\sbw(G)\\le\\pw(G)+1$ \\citep[Thm~8]{LY2002ref12}, and\n   227\t$\\vs(G)\\le\\es(G)\\le\\vs(G)+2$, and the same for $\\pes$\n   228\t\\citep[Thm~2.1]{EllisSudboroughTurner1994}.\\lean{pathwidth_le_splitBandwidth_le_pathwidth_add_one, vertexSeparation_le_edgeSearch_le_add_two, vertexSeparation_le_progressiveEdgeSearch_le_add_two}\n   229\t\\end{theorem}\n   230\tEvery end of both bands occurs. $K_2$ has $\\sbw=\\pw=1$ and $\\es=\\vs=1$; the star\n   231\t$K_{1,3}$ has $\\pw=1$ and $\\sbw=2$, and $\\vs=1$ with $\\es=2$; and $K_{3,3}$ has\n   232\t$\\vs=3$ with $\\es=5$. These values come from a brute-force check of every\n   233\tquantity from its own definition, not from Lean.\\footnote{\\texttt{python -m\n   234\tpaper2.complex\\_check}; $\\sbw(K_{1,3})\\ge2$ is also argued by hand: splits of a\n   235\ttree are trees and keep at least three leaves.} Fomin assumes a connected graph\n   236\twith at least two vertices; the Lean proof needs neither. The equality\n   237\t$\\es=\\pes$ \\citep{LaPaugh1993} is stated in Lean as a named proposition and used\n   238\tby no row.\n   239\t\n   240\t\\begin{theorem}[two rows are false]\\label{thm:false}\n   241\t\\leavevmode\n   242\t\\begin{enumerate}\n   243\t\\item PLA folding: for a matrix with a 1,\n   244\t  $\\max(\\pw(G_M)+1,\\lceil|N|/2\\rceil)\\le\\pla(M)$, and the gap to $\\pw+1$ is\n   245\t  unbounded. The $5\\times5$ identity needs 3 tracks while $\\pw(G_M)+1=1$, and the\n   246\t  path $P_n$ as a matrix, one gate per edge, has $t=2$ and\n   247\t  $\\pla\\ge\\lceil n/2\\rceil$, so the gap is unbounded on connected instances too\n   248\t  \\citep[Prop.~3.15]{LY2002ref6}.\\lean{plaTracks_idMatrix_five, plaTracks_idMatrix_unbounded, plaTracks_pathMatrix_unbounded}\n   249\t\\item Edge separation read as cutwidth: $\\pw\\le\\cw$ always, but\n   250\t  $\\cw(K_{1,n})\\ge n/2$ and $\\mcw(K_{1,n})\\ge n/2-1$ while $\\pw(K_{1,n})=1$; so\n   251\t  neither cutwidth reading is within a constant of pathwidth.\\lean{cutwidth_unbounded, modCutwidth_unbounded}\n   252\t\\end{enumerate}\n   253\t\\end{theorem}\n   254\tTable~1's PLA claim holds for multiple folding (Theorem~\\ref{thm:core}). The\n   255\tedge-separation row is also misattributed: what \\citet{LY2002ref14} proves\n   256\texactly is the vertex separator game, which is Table~1's vertex separation row.\n   257\t\n   258\t\\subsection{Edge cases and a gap in a published proof}\n   259\t\n   260\tThree of the published equalities fail on degenerate inputs, and each failure\n   261\tis proved in Lean. On a nonempty graph with no edges, $\\ns=0$ while\n   262\t$\\vs+1=\\theta=1$, so both equalities of \\citet{LY2002ref9,LY2002ref10} need an\n   263\tedge.\\lean{nodeSearch_ne_intervalThickness_of_edgeless, monotoneNodeSearch_ne_vertexSeparation_add_one_of_edgeless}\n   264\tFor a matrix with no 1s, $Z=0$ while $\\pw+1=1$. Lengauer's Theorem~4 fails at\n   265\t$K=0$ on an edgeless graph.\\lean{isPositiveVSG_triangleGraph_counterexample}\n   266\t\\citet[Thm~3.9]{LY2002ref6}, $t=\\ns$, fails literally on the $1\\times1$ matrix\n   267\t$[1]$.\\lean{tracks_ne_monotoneNodeSearch_one}\n   268\t\n   269\tThe published proof of $\\vs\\le\\ns-1$ \\citep[Thm~4.1]{LY2002ref10} has a gap. It\n   270\torders the vertices by when they first receive a searcher and claims that every\n   271\tvertex counted at the worst cut still holds a searcher, ``since the vertices in\n   272\t$D_{i_0}$ are joined to vertices not yet cleared'' (p.~217). A strategy may place\n   273\ta searcher and remove it again before clearing anything, which recontaminates\n   274\tnothing. On $K_{1,3}$, placing and removing a searcher on each leaf before\n   275\tguarding the centre is an optimal recontamination-free strategy for which the\n   276\tclaim fails.\\lean{kirousisPapadimitriou_claim2_false} The theorem is true. The\n   277\trepair orders the vertices by clearing time, as our Lean proof does, or uses the\n   278\tpaper's own normal form, its Corollary~2.4.\n   279\t\n   280\t\\subsection{A thirteenth member}\\label{sec:thirteenth}\n   281\t\n   282\tProgressive black-white pebbling, which is not in Table~1, belongs to the exact\n   283\tcore. \\citet[Thm~3]{LY2002ref14} gives\n   284\t$\\vs(G)\\le K\\iff\\mathrm{pbw}(G_d)\\le K+2$ on the dag $G_d$ built from $G$; his\n   285\tTheorem~2 gives $\\mathrm{pbw}(D)=\\vs(D_u)+1$ on a dag $D$, where $D_u$ is the\n   286\tMOSP graph of the matrix whose column $v$ is $v$ with its predecessors; and the\n   287\tminimum progressive pebbling number of \\citet[Thm~3.1]{LY2002ref10} equals\n   288\t$\\pw+1$. All three are proved in Lean, each with its hypothesis: Lengauer's\n   289\tTheorem~2 in its instance form fails at $K=1$ on a dag with no arcs, and\n   290\t$\\mathrm{mpb}=\\ns$ fails on edgeless graphs.\\lean{pbw_lengauerD_eq_pathwidth, pbw_eq_mospValue_pebbleMatrix, mpb_eq_pathwidth_add_one}\n   291\t\n   292\t\\paragraph{Summary.} Seven of Table~1's twelve problems are pathwidth, or\n   293\tpathwidth plus one, exactly: MOSP, gate matrix layout, one-dimensional logic,\n   294\tinterval thickness, node search, narrowness and vertex separation, with pathwidth\n   295\titself as the reference. So are two variants, multiple folding and Lengauer's\n   296\tvertex separator game. Split bandwidth and edge search are bands of width one\n   297\tand two, which meet the literal reading of Table~1 and not the fixed offset.\n   298\tSimple PLA folding and edge separation are false as stated. One theorem is\n   299\tstated and not proved, LaPaugh's $\\es=\\pes$, and no row needs it. One of\n   300\tTable~1's references, Kashiwabara and Fujisawa (1979), is not held; its row is\n   301\tproved from \\citet{LY2002ref6} and \\citet{LY2002ref9}.","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"1323e98e-623f-4534-a362-41a379cbd2ab","uuid":"c5432296-cc86-4623-9d8e-4e37ff12f398","timestamp":"2026-10-06T13:48:55.930Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"     1\t% Section 3. Sources: paper2/problem_transformations.md (problems, statements,\n     2\t% proofs), paper2/equivalences.md (master table, sources, gaps),\n     3\t% paper2/proof_reductions.md (faults in published proofs). Lean:\n     4\t% lean/MOSPFormalization/ and Complex/; axioms printed by paper2/axiom_check.lean.\n     5\t\\section{The equivalences, proved}\n     6\t\\label{sec:equiv}\n     7\t\n     8\t\\citet[p.~1764]{LY2002} introduce Table~1 as ``a set of problems that consist\n     9\tof, given input $\\Pi$, compute a function $f(\\Pi)$ that is either equal to the\n    10\tnumber of open stacks or closely related to it (plus or minus one)''. The table\n    11\tcites where each problem was studied. It gives no proofs and no graph. Two\n    12\treadings of ``plus or minus one'' must be kept apart. Under the \\emph{literal}\n    13\treading, $f$ lies within one of $Z$, the number of open stacks of the\n    14\tcorresponding instance, on every input. Under the \\emph{fixed-offset} reading,\n    15\t$f = Z + c$ for one constant $c\\in\\{-1,0,1\\}$ on every input. Only the second\n    16\tmakes computing one problem the same as computing another.\n    17\t\n    18\tThis section states each problem as an instance and a question, gives the\n    19\trelation to pathwidth that is true, and says where it is proved. Every relation\n    20\tstated below is a theorem of our Lean development unless it says otherwise. All\n    21\tthe theorems named depend only on the axioms \\texttt{propext},\n    22\t\\texttt{Classical.choice} and \\texttt{Quot.sound}.\\footnote{Printed by\n    23\t\\texttt{lake env lean paper2/axiom\\_check.lean}, which covers every theorem\n    24\tnamed in this paper. The development has one\n    25\t\\texttt{sorry}, a conjecture about lower bounds that no result of this paper\n    26\tuses.} Table~\\ref{tab:master} summarises the result and\n    27\tFigure~\\ref{fig:chain} draws it.\n    28\t\n    29\t\\subsection{The problems}\n    30\t\\label{sec:problems}\n    31\t\n    32\tEach problem below is a decision problem with an instance and an integer $k$;\n    33\tits value is the least $k$ for which the answer is yes.\n    34\t\n    35\t\\begin{description}\n    36\t\\item[Pathwidth $\\pw(G)$.] Is there a sequence of bags $X_1,\\dots,X_r\\subseteq V$\n    37\t  covering $V$, with every edge inside some bag, $X_i\\cap X_\\ell\\subseteq X_j$\n    38\t  whenever $i\\le j\\le\\ell$, and $|X_i|\\le k+1$? \\citep[p.~346]{LY2002ref13}.\n    39\t\\item[Vertex separation $\\vs(G)$.] Is there a layout $L$ such that for every\n    40\t  $i<n$ at most $k$ vertices $u$ with $L(u)\\le i$ have a neighbour $v$ with\n    41\t  $L(v)>i$? \\citep[p.~346]{LY2002ref13}.\n    42\t\\item[MOSP $Z(M)$.] Given a 0/1 matrix $M$ of customers by patterns, is there\n    43\t  an order of the patterns with at most $k$ customers active at every position?\n    44\t  \\citep[eqs.~(1)--(2)]{LY2002}.\n    45\t\\item[Gate matrix layout $t(M)$.] Given a net--gate matrix, is there an order of\n    46\t  the gates and an assignment of nets to $k$ tracks such that nets active at a\n    47\t  common position get different tracks? \\citep[p.~18]{LY2002ref6};\n    48\t  \\citep[Problem~1]{LY2002ref8}.\n    49\t\\item[One-dimensional logic.] Given gates, nets and the nets each gate connects,\n    50\t  is there an order of the gates whose net intervals have an interval graph of\n    51\t  clique number at most $k$? Its \\emph{connection graph} $H$ joins two nets that\n    52\t  share a gate. A \\emph{boundary} variant pins two gates to the ends\n    53\t  \\citep[\\S\\S II--IV]{LY2002ref7}.\n    54\t\\item[PLA folding $\\pla(M)$.] As gate matrix layout, with at most two nets per\n    55\t  track. With no such bound (\\emph{multiple folding}) it is gate matrix layout\n    56\t  itself \\citep[pp.~25, 36]{LY2002ref6}.\n    57\t\\item[Interval thickness $\\theta(G)$.] Is there an interval supergraph of $G$ on\n    58\t  the same vertices with clique number at most $k$?\n    59\t  \\citep[p.~182]{LY2002ref9}; \\citep[p.~31]{LY2002ref6}.\n    60\t\\item[Node search $\\ns(G)$.] Can searchers, placed on and removed from vertices,\n    61\t  clear every edge with at most $k$ on the graph at once, where an edge is\n    62\t  cleared when both ends are guarded and a clear edge is recontaminated by a\n    63\t  searcher-free path to a contaminated one? $\\mns(G)$ is the same over strategies\n    64\t  that never recontaminate \\citep[p.~181]{LY2002ref9}; \\citep[\\S2]{LY2002ref10}.\n    65\t\\item[Edge search $\\es(G)$.] The same game with a third move, sliding a searcher\n    66\t  along an edge, which clears that edge; $\\pes(G)$ for progressive strategies\n    67\t  \\citep[p.~208]{LY2002ref10}; \\citep[p.~53]{EllisSudboroughTurner1994}.\n    68\t\\item[Narrowness $\\nu(G)$.] Is there an order $v_1,\\dots,v_n$ such that the\n    69\t  following process never holds more than $k$ vertices: at step $i$ add $v_i$,\n    70\t  then remove every $v_j$, $j\\le i$, with no neighbour $v_\\ell$, $\\ell>i$?\n    71\t  \\citep[\\S2]{LY2002ref11}.\n    72\t\\item[Split bandwidth $\\sbw(G)$.] Is there a graph obtained from $G$ by\n    73\t  repeatedly splitting a vertex into an edge, its neighbours partitioned between\n    74\t  the two ends, with bandwidth at most $k$? \\citep[\\S3.2]{LY2002ref12}.\n    75\t\\item[Edge separation.] Table~1 cites \\citet{LY2002ref14}, which supports three\n    76\t  readings: cutwidth $\\cw(G)$, the least over layouts of the most edges crossing\n    77\t  a gap (p.~468); modified cutwidth $\\mcw(G)$, counting only edges that pass\n    78\t  strictly over a vertex (Definition~6); and Lengauer's vertex separator game\n    79\t  $\\VSG(G)$, the least $K$ such that the vertices can be pebbled in an order that\n    80\t  leaves at most $K$ unpebbled vertices adjacent to pebbled ones (p.~467).\n    81\t\\end{description}\n    82\t\n    83\t\\subsection{What is true}\n    84\t\n    85\t\\begin{table}[t]\n    86\t\\centering\\small\n    87\t\\caption{Table~1 of \\citet{LY2002}, settled. \\emph{Relation}: what is true and\n    88\tproved in Lean, on the graph the problem lives on (the MOSP graph or net graph\n    89\tfor the matrix problems, the input graph otherwise). \\emph{Status}:\n    90\t\\emph{exact}, a fixed offset; \\emph{band}, an inequality whose ends both occur;\n    91\t\\emph{false}, Table~1's claim fails, with a counterexample family proved in\n    92\tLean.}\n    93\t\\label{tab:master}\n    94\t\\begin{tabularx}{\\linewidth}{@{}>{\\raggedright}p{2.7cm}>{\\raggedright}p{3.4cm}>{\\raggedright\\arraybackslash}X l@{}}\n    95\t\\toprule\n    96\tProblem & Proved in & Relation & Status \\\\\n    97\t\\midrule\n    98\tMOSP & \\citet{Yanasse1997a}; \\citet{FellowsLangston1989}; \\citet{LY2002} &\n    99\t  $Z(M)=\\pw(G_M)+1$ ($M$ has a 1) & exact \\\\\n   100\tGate matrix layout & \\citet{LY2002ref6} & $t(M)=Z(M)=\\pw(G_M)+1$ & exact \\\\\n   101\tOne-dim.\\ logic & \\citet{LY2002ref7} & tracks $=\\theta(H)=\\pw(H)+1$; boundary variant $\\ge\\pw(H)+1$ & exact \\\\\n   102\tPLA folding & \\citet{LY2002ref6} & $\\max(\\pw+1,\\lceil|N|/2\\rceil)\\le\\pla$, gap unbounded; multiple folding $=\\pw+1$ & false; variant exact \\\\\n   103\tInterval thickness & \\citet{LY2002ref6}; \\citet{LY2002ref9} & $\\theta=\\pw+1$ ($V\\ne\\emptyset$) & exact \\\\\n   104\tNode search & \\citet{LY2002ref9,LY2002ref10}; \\citet{BienstockSeymour1991} & $\\ns=\\mns=\\vs+1=\\pw+1=\\theta$ ($E\\ne\\emptyset$) & exact \\\\\n   105\tEdge search & \\citet{EllisSudboroughTurner1994}; \\citet{LY2002ref10} & $\\vs\\le\\es\\le\\vs+2$, also for $\\pes$ & band \\\\\n   106\tNarrowness & \\citet{LY2002ref11} & $\\nu=\\pw+1$ ($V\\ne\\emptyset$) & exact \\\\\n   107\tSplit bandwidth & \\citet{LY2002ref12} & $\\pw\\le\\sbw\\le\\pw+1$ & band \\\\\n   108\tPath-width & \\citet{LY2002ref13} & the reference & definition \\\\\n   109\tEdge separation & \\citet{LY2002ref14} & $\\cw,\\mcw$ unbounded against $\\pw$ (stars); $\\VSG=\\max(1,\\vs)$ & false; VSG exact \\\\\n   110\tVertex separation & \\citet{LY2002ref13} & $\\vs=\\pw$ & exact \\\\\n   111\t\\bottomrule\n   112\t\\end{tabularx}\n   113\t\\end{table}\n   114\t\n   115\t\\begin{figure}[t]\n   116\t\\centering\n   117\t\\includegraphics[width=\\linewidth]{sec3_fig1_chain}\n   118\t\\caption{The equivalences of Table~\\ref{tab:master} as a graph around\n   119\tpathwidth. Solid edges are exact relations, dashed edges bands, both proved in\n   120\tLean; the dotted edge is LaPaugh's $\\mathrm{es}=\\mathrm{pes}$, the one relation\n   121\tnot proved, which no row needs. Dark red marks Table~1's two false claims, each\n   122\trefuted by a counterexample family proved in Lean. The four matrix problems (top\n   123\tleft) live on the MOSP graph $G_M$ or the net graph $H$; $\\mathrm{mpb}$ is the minimum\n   124\tprogressive pebbling number of Section~\\ref{sec:thirteenth}. Hypotheses are\n   125\tomitted: $M$ has a 1, $V\\neq\\emptyset$, or $G$ has an edge, as in\n   126\tTable~\\ref{tab:master}. Two edges that follow from the others,\n   127\t$\\mathrm{mns}=\\theta$ and $\\mathrm{ns}=\\mathrm{vs}+1$, are not drawn.}\n   128\t\\label{fig:chain}\n   129\t\\end{figure}\n   130\t\n   131\tTwo lemmas carry most of the exact rows. For a path decomposition and a vertex\n   132\t$v$, let $\\mathrm{first}(v)$ and $\\mathrm{last}(v)$ be the least and greatest\n   133\tindices of bags containing $v$; the decomposition's interval property says\n   134\t$v\\in X_i$ exactly when $\\mathrm{first}(v)\\le i\\le\\mathrm{last}(v)$.\n   135\t\n   136\t\\begin{lemma}[Helly for intervals]\\label{lem:helly}\n   137\tPairwise intersecting intervals of a line have a common point.\n   138\t\\end{lemma}\n   139\t\\begin{proof}\n   140\tLet $a$ be the largest left end. Any two intervals meet, so every right end is\n   141\tat least every left end, and in particular at least $a$. So $a$ lies in all of\n   142\tthem.\n   143\t\\end{proof}\n   144\t\n   145\t\\begin{lemma}[cliques sit in a bag]\\label{lem:clique}\n   146\tIn a path decomposition of $G$, every clique of $G$ lies inside one bag.\\lean{PathDecomposition.exists_bag_of_isClique}\n   147\t\\end{lemma}\n   148\t\\begin{proof}\n   149\tThe bags containing a vertex $u$ of the clique form the interval\n   150\t$[\\mathrm{first}(u),\\mathrm{last}(u)]$. Two vertices of the clique are\n   151\tadjacent, so their edge lies in a bag and their intervals meet. By\n   152\tLemma~\\ref{lem:helly} all the intervals share an index $i$, and the clique lies\n   153\tin $X_i$.\n   154\t\\end{proof}\n   155\t\n   156\t\\begin{theorem}[vertex separation is pathwidth]\\label{thm:vs}\n   157\tFor every graph, $\\vs(G)=\\pw(G)$.\\lean{vertexSeparation_eq_pathwidth}\n   158\t\\end{theorem}\n   159\tThis is Theorem~3.1 of \\citet{LY2002ref13}. From a layout $L$ of separation $k$,\n   160\tthe bags $X_i = V_L(i-1)\\cup\\{L^{-1}(i)\\}$, where $V_L(i)$ is the set the\n   161\tdefinition counts, form a decomposition of width $k$. Conversely, ordering the\n   162\tvertices by $\\mathrm{first}$ gives a layout in which everything counted at a cut\n   163\tlies in the bag where the next vertex first appears, beside that vertex.\n   164\t\n   165\t\\begin{theorem}[MOSP is pathwidth plus one]\\label{thm:mosp}\n   166\tIf $M$ has at least one 1, then $Z(M)=\\pw(G_M)+1$.\\lean{MOSPInstance.mospValue_eq_pathwidth_add_one}\n   167\t\\end{theorem}\n   168\t\\begin{proof}\n   169\t($\\le$) Take a decomposition of $G_M$ of width $k$. The rows of each column form\n   170\ta clique, so by Lemma~\\ref{lem:clique} they lie in a bag $X_{b(c)}$. Order the\n   171\tcolumns by $b(c)$. If row $r$ is active at the position of column $c$, it has\n   172\tcolumns $x,y$ with $b(x)\\le b(c)\\le b(y)$, and $r\\in X_{b(x)}\\cap X_{b(y)}$, so the\n   173\tinterval property gives $r\\in X_{b(c)}$. At most $k+1$ rows are active there.\n   174\t\n   175\t($\\ge$) Take an order with at most $Z$ active rows at every position. The\n   176\tactive sets, with a singleton bag for each row with no 1, form a path\n   177\tdecomposition: rows sharing a column are both active at its position, and a row\n   178\tis active on an interval of positions. Its width is $Z-1$, using $Z\\ge1$.\n   179\t\\end{proof}\n   180\t\n   181\tRead as a cutting problem, Theorem~\\ref{thm:mosp} is Proposition~5 of\n   182\t\\citet{Yanasse1997a}, which turns each pattern into a clique on its piece types;\n   183\tread as a layout problem, it is Theorem~7 of \\citet{FellowsLangston1989}, with\n   184\tthe column-expansion Lemma~4.1 of \\citet{FellowsLangston1987}; and\n   185\t\\citet[Prop.~2]{LY2002} identify MOSP with gate matrix layout. The proof above\n   186\tneeds the Helly property and the interval property, not Hall's theorem. It needs\n   187\tno assumption that the instance is reduced.\n   188\t\n   189\tThe graph matters. The pattern graph, with the patterns as vertices joined when\n   190\tthey share a customer, gives no bound in either direction: one pattern shared by\n   191\tfour customers who each have a private pattern has $Z=4$, while its pattern\n   192\tgraph is the star $K_{1,4}$, of pathwidth~1.\\lean{star_mospValue, star_pathwidth_agreementGraph, star_refutes_pattern_graph_bound} A single\n   193\tcustomer with many patterns errs the other way.\n   194\t\n   195\tThe other exact rows attach to Theorems~\\ref{thm:vs} and~\\ref{thm:mosp}.\n   196\t\n   197\t\\begin{theorem}[the exact core]\\label{thm:core}\n   198\t\\leavevmode\n   199\t\\begin{enumerate}\n   200\t\\item Gate matrix layout is MOSP on the same matrix: if $M$ has a 1,\n   201\t  $t(M)=Z(M)=\\pw(G_M)+1$. For a fixed gate order the fewest tracks equals the\n   202\t  most nets active at one position, by the left-edge algorithm.\\lean{NetGateMatrix.tracks_eq_mospValue, NetGateMatrix.tracks_eq_pathwidth_add_one}\n   203\t\\item Interval thickness: if $V\\ne\\emptyset$, $\\theta(G)=\\pw(G)+1$\n   204\t  \\citep[Prop.~3.5]{LY2002ref6}.\\lean{intervalThickness_eq_pathwidth_add_one}\n   205\t\\item One-dimensional logic: if every net lies on a gate, the fewest tracks is\n   206\t  $\\theta(H)$, and if some gate connects a net it is $\\pw(H)+1$\n   207\t  \\citep[Thm~3]{LY2002ref7}.\\lean{LogicArray.tracks_eq_intervalThickness, LogicArray.tracks_eq_pathwidth_add_one}\n   208\t\\item Narrowness: if $V\\ne\\emptyset$, $\\nu(G)=\\pw(G)+1$, and for each order\n   209\t  $\\sigma$, $\\nu(\\sigma)=\\vs(\\sigma^{\\mathrm{rev}})+1$\n   210\t  \\citep[Prop.~3.1]{LY2002ref11}.\\lean{narrowness_eq_pathwidth_add_one}\n   211\t\\item Node search: for every graph $\\ns(G)=\\mns(G)$, and if $E\\ne\\emptyset$,\n   212\t  $\\ns(G)=\\vs(G)+1=\\pw(G)+1=\\theta(G)$\n   213\t  \\citep[Thm]{LY2002ref9}; \\citep[Thms~2.3, 4.1]{LY2002ref10}.\\lean{nodeSearchMonotonicity, nodeSearch_eq_vertexSeparation_add_one, nodeSearch_chain}\n   214\t\\item Multiple folding: the fewest tracks is $t(M)=\\pw(G_M)+1$\n   215\t  \\citep[Thm~3.14]{LY2002ref6}.\\lean{NetGateMatrix.foldTracks_eq_pathwidth_add_one}\n   216\t\\item Lengauer's vertex separator game: $\\VSG(G)=\\max(1,\\vs(G))$\n   217\t  \\citep[Thm~4]{LY2002ref14}.\\lean{vsg_eq_max}\n   218\t\\end{enumerate}\n   219\t\\end{theorem}\n   220\t\n   221\tThe Lean proof that node search is monotone follows the crusade argument of\n   222\t\\citet{BienstockSeymour1991}, not the reduction to edge search of\n   223\t\\citet{LaPaugh1993} on which \\citet{LY2002ref10} rely.\n   224\t\n   225\t\\begin{theorem}[bands]\\label{thm:bands}\n   226\tFor every graph, $\\pw(G)\\le\\sbw(G)\\le\\pw(G)+1$ \\citep[Thm~8]{LY2002ref12}, and\n   227\t$\\vs(G)\\le\\es(G)\\le\\vs(G)+2$, and the same for $\\pes$\n   228\t\\citep[Thm~2.1]{EllisSudboroughTurner1994}.\\lean{pathwidth_le_splitBandwidth_le_pathwidth_add_one, vertexSeparation_le_edgeSearch_le_add_two, vertexSeparation_le_progressiveEdgeSearch_le_add_two}\n   229\t\\end{theorem}\n   230\tEvery end of both bands occurs. $K_2$ has $\\sbw=\\pw=1$ and $\\es=\\vs=1$; the star\n   231\t$K_{1,3}$ has $\\pw=1$ and $\\sbw=2$, and $\\vs=1$ with $\\es=2$; and $K_{3,3}$ has\n   232\t$\\vs=3$ with $\\es=5$. These values come from a brute-force check of every\n   233\tquantity from its own definition, not from Lean.\\footnote{\\texttt{python -m\n   234\tpaper2.complex\\_check}; $\\sbw(K_{1,3})\\ge2$ is also argued by hand: splits of a\n   235\ttree are trees and keep at least three leaves.} Fomin assumes a connected graph\n   236\twith at least two vertices; the Lean proof needs neither. The equality\n   237\t$\\es=\\pes$ \\citep{LaPaugh1993} is stated in Lean as a named proposition and used\n   238\tby no row.\n   239\t\n   240\t\\begin{theorem}[two rows are false]\\label{thm:false}\n   241\t\\leavevmode\n   242\t\\begin{enumerate}\n   243\t\\item PLA folding: for a matrix with a 1,\n   244\t  $\\max(\\pw(G_M)+1,\\lceil|N|/2\\rceil)\\le\\pla(M)$, and the gap to $\\pw+1$ is\n   245\t  unbounded. The $5\\times5$ identity needs 3 tracks while $\\pw(G_M)+1=1$, and the\n   246\t  path $P_n$ as a matrix, one gate per edge, has $t=2$ and\n   247\t  $\\pla\\ge\\lceil n/2\\rceil$, so the gap is unbounded on connected instances too\n   248\t  \\citep[Prop.~3.15]{LY2002ref6}.\\lean{plaTracks_idMatrix_five, plaTracks_idMatrix_unbounded, plaTracks_pathMatrix_unbounded}\n   249\t\\item Edge separation read as cutwidth: $\\pw\\le\\cw$ always, but\n   250\t  $\\cw(K_{1,n})\\ge n/2$ and $\\mcw(K_{1,n})\\ge n/2-1$ while $\\pw(K_{1,n})=1$; so\n   251\t  neither cutwidth reading is within a constant of pathwidth.\\lean{cutwidth_unbounded, modCutwidth_unbounded}\n   252\t\\end{enumerate}\n   253\t\\end{theorem}\n   254\tTable~1's PLA claim holds for multiple folding (Theorem~\\ref{thm:core}). The\n   255\tedge-separation row is also misattributed: what \\citet{LY2002ref14} proves\n   256\texactly is the vertex separator game, which is Table~1's vertex separation row.\n   257\t\n   258\t\\subsection{Edge cases and a gap in a published proof}\n   259\t\n   260\tThree of the published equalities fail on degenerate inputs, and each failure\n   261\tis proved in Lean. On a nonempty graph with no edges, $\\ns=0$ while\n   262\t$\\vs+1=\\theta=1$, so both equalities of \\citet{LY2002ref9,LY2002ref10} need an\n   263\tedge.\\lean{nodeSearch_ne_intervalThickness_of_edgeless, monotoneNodeSearch_ne_vertexSeparation_add_one_of_edgeless}\n   264\tFor a matrix with no 1s, $Z=0$ while $\\pw+1=1$. Lengauer's Theorem~4 fails at\n   265\t$K=0$ on an edgeless graph.\\lean{isPositiveVSG_triangleGraph_counterexample}\n   266\t\\citet[Thm~3.9]{LY2002ref6}, $t=\\ns$, fails literally on the $1\\times1$ matrix\n   267\t$[1]$.\\lean{tracks_ne_monotoneNodeSearch_one}\n   268\t\n   269\tThe published proof of $\\vs\\le\\ns-1$ \\citep[Thm~4.1]{LY2002ref10} has a gap. It\n   270\torders the vertices by when they first receive a searcher and claims that every\n   271\tvertex counted at the worst cut still holds a searcher, ``since the vertices in\n   272\t$D_{i_0}$ are joined to vertices not yet cleared'' (p.~217). A strategy may place\n   273\ta searcher and remove it again before clearing anything, which recontaminates\n   274\tnothing. On $K_{1,3}$, placing and removing a searcher on each leaf before\n   275\tguarding the centre is an optimal recontamination-free strategy for which the\n   276\tclaim fails.\\lean{kirousisPapadimitriou_claim2_false} The theorem is true. The\n   277\trepair orders the vertices by clearing time, as our Lean proof does, or uses the\n   278\tpaper's own normal form, its Corollary~2.4.\n   279\t\n   280\t\\subsection{A thirteenth member}\\label{sec:thirteenth}\n   281\t\n   282\tProgressive black-white pebbling, which is not in Table~1, belongs to the exact\n   283\tcore. \\citet[Thm~3]{LY2002ref14} gives\n   284\t$\\vs(G)\\le K\\iff\\mathrm{pbw}(G_d)\\le K+2$ on the dag $G_d$ built from $G$; his\n   285\tTheorem~2 gives $\\mathrm{pbw}(D)=\\vs(D_u)+1$ on a dag $D$, where $D_u$ is the\n   286\tMOSP graph of the matrix whose column $v$ is $v$ with its predecessors; and the\n   287\tminimum progressive pebbling number of \\citet[Thm~3.1]{LY2002ref10} equals\n   288\t$\\pw+1$. All three are proved in Lean, each with its hypothesis: Lengauer's\n   289\tTheorem~2 in its instance form fails at $K=1$ on a dag with no arcs, and\n   290\t$\\mathrm{mpb}=\\ns$ fails on edgeless graphs.\\lean{pbw_lengauerD_eq_pathwidth, pbw_eq_mospValue_pebbleMatrix, mpb_eq_pathwidth_add_one}\n   291\t\n   292\t\\paragraph{Summary.} Seven of Table~1's twelve problems are pathwidth, or\n   293\tpathwidth plus one, exactly: MOSP, gate matrix layout, one-dimensional logic,\n   294\tinterval thickness, node search, narrowness and vertex separation, with pathwidth\n   295\titself as the reference. So are two variants, multiple folding and Lengauer's\n   296\tvertex separator game. Split bandwidth and edge search are bands of width one\n   297\tand two, which meet the literal reading of Table~1 and not the fixed offset.\n   298\tSimple PLA folding and edge separation are false as stated. One theorem is\n   299\tstated and not proved, LaPaugh's $\\es=\\pes$, and no row needs it. One of\n   300\tTable~1's references, Kashiwabara and Fujisawa (1979), is not held; its row is\n   301\tproved from \\citet{LY2002ref6} and \\citet{LY2002ref9}.","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false}}
