{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_0145EM6dc64i8Z3HQT5byRgh","type":"tool_result","content":"<persisted-output>\nOutput too large (38.3KB). Full output saved to: /home/al/.claude/projects/-home-al-dev-MOSP/1323e98e-623f-4534-a362-41a379cbd2ab/tool-results/bqu7li2ck.txt\n\nPreview (first 2KB):\n     1\t% Section 4. Sources: paper2/revised_algorithm.md (4.1-4.7), search_soundness.md,\n     2\t% solver_fix.md (cost, re-certification), prior_art_counterexample.md (prior\n     3\t% art), certificates.md (proof object), dataset.md (the dataset). Lean:\n     4\t% lean/MOSPFormalization/Search/. Tables from tables/ (make_tables.py).\n     5\t% Page numbers for Chu & Stuckey (2009) are those of the preprint\n     6\t% literature/chu_stuckey_2009.pdf; for Chu (2011) the printed ones.\n     7\t\\section{An exact open-stacks search, read as a pathwidth solver}\n     8\t\\label{sec:search}\n     9\t\n    10\tThe best exact method for MOSP on the standard benchmarks is the \\emph{customer\n    11\tsearch} of \\citet{ChuStuckey2009}. It searches over the orders in which the\n    12\tcustomers' stacks close, not over product sequences, and prunes with dominance\n    13\trules, three of them stated as theorems (their Theorems~1, 2 and~3) and a fourth,\n    14\tthe subset rule, in their \\S2. \\citet{ChuStuckey2009} place MOSP among ``graph\n    15\tpath-width and gate matrix layout'', citing \\citet{LY2002}, but do not use\n    16\tpathwidth in the search. By Theorem~\\ref{thm:mosp} it is nevertheless a\n    17\tpathwidth algorithm: any graph is the MOSP graph of the instance with one\n    18\tcustomer per vertex and one product per edge, whose optimum is pathwidth plus\n    19\tone.\n    20\t\n    21\tThis section reads the search that way, and finds three things. Two of the\n    22\tpublished theorems are false, and so are their restatements in Chu's thesis\n    23\t\\citep{Chu2011} and, with a stronger premise, in \\citet{Fink2012}. The repaired\n    24\tfirst rule is an instance of a known pathwidth theorem, Tamaki's commitment\n    25\tlemma; the published rule is that lemma with its interior condition dropped. And\n    26\tthe whole repaired search is proved sound in Lean. All Lean names in this\n    27\tsection are in \\leanfile{lean/MOSPFormalization/Search/}, which has no\n    28\t\\texttt{sorry} and the standard axioms only.\n...\n</persisted-output>","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"1323e98e-623f-4534-a362-41a379cbd2ab","uuid":"bb4f1142-76ed-4506-8714-84a32e33acfb","timestamp":"2026-10-06T13:48:58.290Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"     1\t% Section 4. Sources: paper2/revised_algorithm.md (4.1-4.7), search_soundness.md,\n     2\t% solver_fix.md (cost, re-certification), prior_art_counterexample.md (prior\n     3\t% art), certificates.md (proof object), dataset.md (the dataset). Lean:\n     4\t% lean/MOSPFormalization/Search/. Tables from tables/ (make_tables.py).\n     5\t% Page numbers for Chu & Stuckey (2009) are those of the preprint\n     6\t% literature/chu_stuckey_2009.pdf; for Chu (2011) the printed ones.\n     7\t\\section{An exact open-stacks search, read as a pathwidth solver}\n     8\t\\label{sec:search}\n     9\t\n    10\tThe best exact method for MOSP on the standard benchmarks is the \\emph{customer\n    11\tsearch} of \\citet{ChuStuckey2009}. It searches over the orders in which the\n    12\tcustomers' stacks close, not over product sequences, and prunes with dominance\n    13\trules, three of them stated as theorems (their Theorems~1, 2 and~3) and a fourth,\n    14\tthe subset rule, in their \\S2. \\citet{ChuStuckey2009} place MOSP among ``graph\n    15\tpath-width and gate matrix layout'', citing \\citet{LY2002}, but do not use\n    16\tpathwidth in the search. By Theorem~\\ref{thm:mosp} it is nevertheless a\n    17\tpathwidth algorithm: any graph is the MOSP graph of the instance with one\n    18\tcustomer per vertex and one product per edge, whose optimum is pathwidth plus\n    19\tone.\n    20\t\n    21\tThis section reads the search that way, and finds three things. Two of the\n    22\tpublished theorems are false, and so are their restatements in Chu's thesis\n    23\t\\citep{Chu2011} and, with a stronger premise, in \\citet{Fink2012}. The repaired\n    24\tfirst rule is an instance of a known pathwidth theorem, Tamaki's commitment\n    25\tlemma; the published rule is that lemma with its interior condition dropped. And\n    26\tthe whole repaired search is proved sound in Lean. All Lean names in this\n    27\tsection are in \\leanfile{lean/MOSPFormalization/Search/}, which has no\n    28\t\\texttt{sorry} and the standard axioms only.\n    29\t\n    30\t\\subsection{The search decides pathwidth}\n    31\t\\label{sec:search-def}\n    32\t\n    33\t\\citet[p.~4]{ChuStuckey2009} observe that the products matter only through the\n    34\tMOSP graph. So fix a graph $G=(C,E)$ on the customers and an integer $k\\ge0$.\n    35\tFor a customer $c$ and a set $T\\subseteq C$ of closed customers, write\n    36\t$N[c]$ for the closed neighbourhood of $c$, and\n    37\t\\begin{align*}\n    38\tO(T) &= \\textstyle\\bigcup_{c\\in T}N[c] && \\text{the stacks opened once $T$ has closed},\\\\\n    39\t\\fin(X) &= \\{c : N[c]\\subseteq X\\} && \\text{the customers free to close when $X$ is open},\\\\\n    40\t\\cl(T) &= \\fin(O(T)) && \\text{the \\emph{free closure} of $T$},\\\\\n    41\tb(T) &= |O(T)\\setminus T| && \\text{the stacks open after $T$ has closed},\\\\\n    42\to(c,T) &= N[c]\\setminus O(T),\\quad \\open(c,T)=|o(c,T)| && \\text{the stacks closing $c$ would newly open.}\n    43\t\\end{align*}\n    44\tA \\emph{move} at $T$ closes one customer $c\\notin T$, at cost\n    45\t$\\cost(T,c)=|O(T\\cup\\{c\\})\\setminus T| = b(T\\cup\\{c\\})+1$, the stacks open at\n    46\tthat moment counting $c$; it is \\emph{playable} when $\\cost(T,c)\\le k$. Write\n    47\t$P_k(T)$ when some order of $C\\setminus T$ has every step playable. On entering\n    48\ta node the search closes every free customer, so it stands only at\n    49\t\\emph{states}, sets with $S=\\cl(S)$, and the \\emph{child} of $S$ by $c$ is\n    50\t$S\\cdot c=\\cl(S\\cup\\{c\\})$. The search's predicate $\\Sol_k(S)$ holds when $S=C$,\n    51\tor when some playable $c$ has $\\Sol_k(S\\cdot c)$.\\lean{stepCost, Solvable, SearchSol}\n    52\tEvery node the search visits satisfies the invariant $b(S)\\le k$.\n    53\t\n    54\t\\begin{lemma}[free moves never hurt]\\label{lem:F}\n    55\tIf $b(T)\\le k$, then $P_k(T)\\iff\\Sol_k(\\cl(T))$.\\lean{solvable_iff_searchSol_cl}\n    56\t\\end{lemma}\n    57\tClosing a customer whose neighbourhood is open opens nothing, never makes a\n    58\tlater step dearer, and leaves one fewer stack open. The hypothesis is needed:\n    59\tclosing the free customers one by one costs $b(T)$ at each step.\n    60\t\n    61\t\\begin{theorem}[the search decides pathwidth]\\label{thm:decides}\n    62\tFor nonempty $C$, $\\Sol_k(\\emptyset)\\iff\\pw(G)+1\\le k$; on the MOSP graph of an\n    63\tinstance $M$ with a requirement, $\\Sol_k(\\emptyset)\\iff Z(M)\\le k$.\\lean{searchSol_empty_iff_pathwidth_add_one_le, searchSol_mospGraph_iff_mospValue_le}\n    64\t\\end{theorem}\n    65\t\\begin{proof}\n    66\tFor a closing order $c_1,\\dots,c_n$ with prefixes $S_i$, the $i$-th step costs\n    67\t$1+|\\partial S_i|$, where $\\partial X$ is the set of vertices outside $X$ with a\n    68\tneighbour in $X$. Read backwards as a layout, the cut separating $C\\setminus S_i$\n    69\tfrom $S_i$ counts exactly $\\partial S_i$. So the cost of the order is the vertex\n    70\tseparation of the reversed layout plus one, and Lemma~\\ref{lem:F} at the root and\n    71\tTheorem~\\ref{thm:vs} finish the proof. The Lean statement goes through\n    72\tnarrowness \\citep{LY2002ref11}, which counts the forward order directly.\n    73\t\\end{proof}\n    74\t\n    75\tSo a refutation, $\\neg\\Sol_k(\\emptyset)$, may only mean $\\pw(G)>k-1$. The rest\n    76\tof the section asks whether the pruning rules preserve that meaning.\n    77\t\n    78\t\\paragraph{What a rule must satisfy.} At a state $S$ the search has a set $Q$ of\n    79\t\\emph{old moves} (Section~\\ref{sec:other-rules}). Its candidates are the\n    80\tcustomers outside $S\\cup Q$; the \\emph{cost cut} keeps the playable ones, $P$,\n    81\tin index order, and a \\emph{filter} keeps a sublist $L\\subseteq P$. The filter\n    82\tis \\emph{node-sound} at $(S,Q)$ if, whenever every $q\\in Q$ has\n    83\t$\\neg\\Sol_k(S\\cdot q)$, $\\Sol_k(S)$ implies $\\Sol_k(S\\cdot c)$ for some $c\\in L$.\\lean{NodeSoundAt}\n    84\tEach rule justifies a discarded move $r$ by a \\emph{covering}, a move $c$ with\n    85\t$\\Sol_k(S\\cdot r)\\Rightarrow\\Sol_k(S\\cdot c)$. A node is sound when every link a\n    86\trule cites is a true covering and every chain of links from a discarded\n    87\tcandidate ends in $L$ or in $Q$; the second condition can fail through a cycle\n    88\teven when every link is true. The filter runs the definite move, then the subset\n    89\trule, then the better move, each once.\n    90\t\n    91\t\\subsection{The definite move is false as published}\n    92\t\\label{sec:definite}\n    93\t\n    94\t\\citet[\\S3.1, p.~6]{ChuStuckey2009} state:\n    95\t\\begin{quote}\n    96\t\\textbf{Theorem 1.} Suppose S ++ [q] is playable and close(q, S) $\\ge$\n    97\topen(q, S), then if U$'$ = S ++ R is a solution, there exists a solution\n    98\tU = S ++ [q] ++ R$'$,\n    99\t\\end{quote}\n   100\twhere $\\close(q,S)$ counts the customers $d$ with $o(d,S)\\subseteq o(q,S)$\n   101\t(p.~4). The rule keeps $q$ alone. We read $d$ as ranging over customers not yet\n   102\tclosed, as the code does; read literally, closed customers count too, which\n   103\tweakens the premise, and both readings are refuted below. \\citet[Thm~6.3.6,\n   104\tp.~142]{Chu2011} restates the theorem with the same premise and proof, reworded\n   105\tfor $k$ stacks. Write $X=S\\cdot q$.\n   106\t\n   107\t\\begin{lemma}[what the premise says]\\label{lem:premise}\n   108\t$\\close(q,S)=|X\\setminus S|$. Hence the premise holds iff $b(X)\\le b(S)$: the\n   109\tchild has no more open stacks than the parent.\\lean{closeCount_eq, isDefinite_iff}\n   110\t\\end{lemma}\n   111\t\n   112\t\\begin{figure}[t]\n   113\t\\centering\n   114\t\\includegraphics[width=0.85\\linewidth]{definite_counterexample.pdf}\n   115\t\\caption{The graph of Counterexample~\\ref{cex:definite} at the state where the\n   116\tdefinite move fails. Customer 2 is closed; 1, 3 and 4 are open. Moving 0 opens\n   117\tthe new stacks 0, 7 and 11 and closes 0, 3 and 4, so open = close = 3 and the\n   118\trule keeps 0 alone. But 3 and 4 have the single new stack 0 between them:\n   119\tclosing them first gains two stacks for the price of one, which a solution\n   120\tstarting with 0 cannot match. Regenerate with \\texttt{python -m\n   121\tpaper2.counterexample\\_figure}, which recomputes every labelled fact.}\n   122\t\\label{fig:cex}\n   123\t\\end{figure}\n   124\t\n   125\t\\begin{counterexample}\\label{cex:definite}\n   126\tLet $G$ be the graph on customers $0,\\dots,13$ with the 26 edges\n   127\t\\begin{gather*}\n   128\t0\\text{--}3,\\ 0\\text{--}4,\\ 0\\text{--}7,\\ 0\\text{--}11,\\ 1\\text{--}2,\\ 1\\text{--}5,\\ 1\\text{--}6,\\ 2\\text{--}3,\\ 2\\text{--}4,\\ 5\\text{--}9,\\ 5\\text{--}11,\\ 5\\text{--}12,\\ 6\\text{--}8,\\\\\n   129\t6\\text{--}13,\\ 7\\text{--}9,\\ 7\\text{--}10,\\ 7\\text{--}13,\\ 8\\text{--}9,\\ 8\\text{--}10,\\ 8\\text{--}11,\\ 9\\text{--}10,\\ 9\\text{--}11,\\ 10\\text{--}11,\\ 10\\text{--}12,\\ 10\\text{--}13,\\ 12\\text{--}13,\n   130\t\\end{gather*}\n   131\twith $S=\\{2\\}$, $k=6$ and $q=0$ (Figure~\\ref{fig:cex}). Then $O(S)=\\{1,2,3,4\\}$,\n   132\t$o(0,S)=\\{0,7,11\\}$ and $\\cost(S,0)=6$, so $0$ is playable; the customers with\n   133\t$o(d,S)\\subseteq\\{0,7,11\\}$ are $0$, $3$ and $4$, so $\\close(0,S)=3\\ge\\open(0,S)$;\n   134\tand $0$ comes first in index order, so the rule keeps only $0$. A solution\n   135\texists from $S$: the order\n   136\t\\[1,3,4,6,12,13,0,5,7,8,9,10,11\\]\n   137\tcosts at most 6. None exists\n   138\tfrom the child $X=\\{0,2,3,4\\}$: only seven sets are reachable from $X$ by moves\n   139\tof cost at most 6, and none is $C$.\\lean{definiteMove_counterexample, cexFamily_closed}\n   140\t\\end{counterexample}\n   141\t\n   142\tThe published implication, as a universal statement, is refuted in Lean under\n   143\tboth readings of $\\close$, and so is the thesis's Theorem~6.3.6.\\lean{chuStuckey_theorem1_false, chuStuckey_theorem1_false_literal, chuThesis_theorem636_false}\n   144\tThe smallest graph on which the rule loses the last solution has 14 customers:\n   145\tthere is none on any graph to 9 vertices, by exhaustion, and none on the 88,802\n   146\tconnected graphs on 10 vertices with at most 14 edges. The counterexample was\n   147\tfound by a search over a family of gadgets built from the failed proof, and\n   148\tminimised by deletion. \\citet[pp.~27--28]{Fink2012} restates the rule with a\n   149\tstronger premise, counting only dominated customers of smaller index than $q$.\n   150\tThe graph above refutes it under no labelling, but a third twin of 3 and 4 gives\n   151\ta 15-customer graph that does.\\lean{fink_theorem1_false}\n   152\t\n   153\t\\paragraph{Why the published proof fails.} The proof moves $q$ to the front of a\n   154\tsolution and claims that after each prefix ``we have at most open(q, S) extra\n   155\tstacks open, but at least close(q, S) extra stacks closed'' (p.~6). At a prefix\n   156\t$T\\supseteq S$ that does not yet contain $q$, moving $q$ forward changes the open\n   157\tstacks by $b(T\\cup X)-b(T) = |N[q]\\setminus O(T)|-|X\\setminus T|$. At $T=S$ this\n   158\tis $\\open-\\close\\le0$. But customers of $X$ that the solution has \\emph{already}\n   159\tclosed are in $T$ and no longer count as extra stacks closed, while the stacks\n   160\t$q$ opens may still be new. In the example, a solution may begin with 3, after\n   161\twhich 4 is free: at $T=\\{2,3,4\\}$, moving 0 forward costs one more stack. A\n   162\tsecond, independent slip: even where the conclusion holds, the literal sequence\n   163\t$S \\mathbin{+\\!\\!+} [q] \\mathbin{+\\!\\!+} R'$ need not be a solution, because a\n   164\tcustomer that $q$ frees stays open until its turn; the argument needs the free\n   165\tmoves.\n   166\t\n   167\t\\paragraph{The repair.} Call $q$ \\emph{hereditarily definite} at $S$ when\n   168\t$b(X)\\le b(B)$ for every $B$ with $S\\subseteq B\\subseteq X$ and $q\\notin B$. By\n   169\tLemma~\\ref{lem:premise}, the published premise is the case $B=S$. In the\n   170\tcounterexample the repair fails at $B=\\{2,3,4\\}$, where $b(B)=2<3=b(X)$.\\lean{IsHereditarilyDefinite, not_isHereditarilyDefinite_cex}\n   171\t\n   172\t\\begin{lemma}[open stacks are submodular]\\label{lem:submod}\n   173\tFor all $A,B\\subseteq C$, $b(A\\cup B)+b(A\\cap B)\\le b(A)+b(B)$.\\lean{openStacks_submodular}\n   174\t\\end{lemma}\n   175\t\\begin{proof}\n   176\t$b(T)=|O(T)|-|T|$, $O(A\\cup B)=O(A)\\cup O(B)$ and $O(A\\cap B)\\subseteq\n   177\tO(A)\\cap O(B)$; the customers themselves balance exactly.\n   178\t\\end{proof}\n   179\t\n   180\t\\begin{theorem}[the repaired definite move is sound]\\label{thm:repaired}\n   181\tIf $q\\notin S$ is hereditarily definite at $S$, then $P_k(S)\\Rightarrow P_k(X)$.\n   182\tAt a state with the invariant, $\\Sol_k(S)\\Rightarrow\\Sol_k(S\\cdot q)$.\\lean{solvable_cl_insert_of_hereditarilyDefinite, searchSol_cl_insert_of_hereditarilyDefinite}\n   183\t\\end{theorem}\n   184\t\\begin{proof}\n   185\tTake an order from $S$ of cost at most $k$ with prefixes $T_i$, in which $q$ is\n   186\tthe $j$-th step, and follow it from $X$, skipping customers already in $X$. A\n   187\tstep $i<j$ closes $c_i$ at $T_{i-1}\\cup X$. With $Z=T_i$ we have\n   188\t$S\\subseteq Z\\cap X\\subseteq X$ and $q\\notin Z$, so the hypothesis gives\n   189\t$b(X)\\le b(Z\\cap X)$, and Lemma~\\ref{lem:submod} gives $b(Z\\cup X)\\le b(Z)$: the\n   190\tstep costs no more than the original. After step $j-1$ the closed set contains\n   191\t$T_j$ with the same opened set, and the rest of the order applies. The search's\n   192\tform follows by Lemma~\\ref{lem:F}.\n   193\t\\end{proof}\n   194\t\n   195\tThis theorem is not new. The hereditary premise is the commitment condition of\n   196\tTamaki, and the proof is the proof of the commitment lemma,\n   197\t\\citet[Lemma~1, p.~142]{Kitsunai2016}; Section~\\ref{sec:layouts} makes the\n   198\tcorrespondence exact. What we did not find in the papers we hold is the\n   199\tfollowing cheap form of the test.\n   200\t\n   201\t\\begin{theorem}[the repair is a matching condition]\\label{thm:matching}\n   202\tLet $Y=X\\setminus(S\\cup\\{q\\})$, and join each $d\\in Y$ to the stacks of\n   203\t$o(d,S)\\subseteq o(q,S)$. Then $q$ is hereditarily definite at $S$ iff this\n   204\tbipartite graph has a matching with at least $\\open(q,S)-1$ edges.\\lean{isHereditarilyDefinite_iff_hasDefiniteMatching}\n   205\t\\end{theorem}\n   206\t\\begin{proof}\n   207\tThe sets of the repair are $B=S\\cup D$, $D\\subseteq Y$, and\n   208\t$b(X)\\le b(S\\cup D)$ iff $|D|-|o(D)|\\le\\delta$ with\n   209\t$\\delta=|Y|+1-\\open(q,S)$, where $o(D)$ is the union of the $o(d,S)$. By the\n   210\tdeficiency form of Hall's theorem the largest matching has size\n   211\t$|Y|-\\max_D(|D|-|o(D)|)$, which is at least $|Y|-\\delta=\\open(q,S)-1$ exactly\n   212\twhen the repair holds. The Lean proof reduces the deficiency form to Mathlib's\n   213\tHall theorem with $\\delta$ dummy stacks.\n   214\t\\end{proof}\n   215\t\n   216\tIn words: the rule is safe exactly when all but one of the new stacks can be\n   217\tpaired with a different freed customer who needed it. In the counterexample,\n   218\tclosing 0 opens three new stacks and frees 3 and 4, both of whom needed only\n   219\tstack 0: one pairing where two are required. Every $q$ with $\\open(q,S)\\le1$\n   220\tpasses at once, so the published rule is right there.\\lean{isHereditarilyDefinite_of_openCount_le_one}\n   221\t\n   222\t\\subsection{The other rules}\n   223\t\\label{sec:other-rules}\n   224\t\n   225\t\\paragraph{The subset rule.} ``If $o(c_i,S)\\subseteq o(c_j,S)$ and $i<j$ \\dots\\\n   226\tmove $j$ can be removed'' \\citep[\\S2, p.~5]{ChuStuckey2009}. As coded, $d$\n   227\tdominates $r$ when $o(d,S)\\subseteq o(r,S)$ and the inclusion is strict or $d<r$;\n   228\tevery dominated playable $r$ is dropped, with a fallback that keeps $P$ if all\n   229\twould go. The covering is sound for every set $S$: if $o(d,S)\\subseteq o(r,S)$\n   230\tand $r$ is playable, then $d$ is playable and $\\Sol_k(S\\cdot r)\\Rightarrow\n   231\t\\Sol_k(S\\cdot d)$, because $d$ is free after $r$ and closing $d$ then $r$ reaches\n   232\tthe same state at no greater cost. With the index tie-break, domination is a\n   233\tstrict order, so an undominated move with a solution survives, and the fallback\n   234\tnever fires when a solution exists.\\lean{searchSol_cl_insert_of_newlyOpened_subset, subsetFilter_sound}\n   235\tThe tie-break is necessary: without it, two customers with equal new stacks\n   236\tdominate each other and both go, which loses a solution on a 7-customer\n   237\tgraph.\\lean{noTieBreak_counterexample}\n   238\t\n   239\t\\paragraph{The better move.} \\citet[\\S3.2, p.~6]{ChuStuckey2009}: ``Suppose\n   240\tS ++ [q] and S ++ [r, q] are playable and close(q, S $\\cup$ \\{r\\}) $\\ge$\n   241\topen(q, S $\\cup$ \\{r\\}) then if U$'$ = S ++ [r] ++ R is a solution there exists a\n   242\tsolution U = S ++ [q] ++ R$'$.'' Its premise is Theorem~1's premise for $q$ at the\n   243\tchild $\\cl(S\\cup\\{r\\})$, together with playability of $r$ then $q$, and its\n   244\tconclusion swaps $q$ and $r$.\\lean{isBetter_iff} The swap is sound; the appeal to\n   245\tTheorem~1 is not. On the graph of Counterexample~\\ref{cex:definite} at the root\n   246\twith $k=6$, the definite move does not fire, customers 0 and 2 both survive the\n   247\tsubset rule, and the premise holds for $r=2$, $q=0$, so the rule drops 2 citing\n   248\t0. But $\\{2\\}$ has a solution and $\\{0\\}$ has none.\\lean{betterMove_counterexample, chuStuckey_theorem2_false}\n   249\tAt that node another kept customer still has a solution, so this is a false link\n   250\tand not a lost node; no node where the better move loses the last solution has\n   251\tbeen found. \\citet[Thm~6.3.8, p.~143]{Chu2011} states the better move with a\n   252\tdifferent premise, $\\close(q,S)\\ge\\open(q,S\\cup\\{r\\})$, and proves it through\n   253\tTheorem~6.3.6. The same graph refutes it at $S=\\{2\\}$, $r=3$, $q=0$.\\lean{chuThesis_theorem638_false, chuThesis_theorem638_false_literal}\n   254\tThe repair cites $q$ for $r$ only if $q$ is hereditarily definite at\n   255\t$\\cl(S\\cup\\{r\\})$, one matching at the child; then the swap argument goes\n   256\tthrough.\\lean{IsRepairedBetter, searchSol_cl_insert_of_repairedBetter}\n   257\t\n   258\tTwo earlier faults of this rule in our own implementation are now proved in\n   259\tLean too: a close count that included the customers $r$ finishes on its own,\n   260\tand an order of the rules in which the subset rule could cite a customer the\n   261\tbetter move had already dropped, which loses a node through a cycle of true\n   262\tlinks.\\lean{bugA_counterexample, bugB_counterexample}\n   263\tThe second is an instance of the general observation of\n   264\t\\citet[Prop.~3, Example~6]{Beck2025} that mutually dominating transitions must\n   265\tbe tie-broken.\n   266\t\n   267\t\\paragraph{The old move.} \\citet[Thm~3, p.~7]{ChuStuckey2009} let a child inherit\n   268\ta sibling $q$ whose subtree failed, provided that inserting $q$ before each\n   269\tlater step keeps that step playable. The rule is sound as coded: if\n   270\t$\\cost(S\\cup\\{q\\},c)\\le k$, then\n   271\t$\\Sol_k(\\cl(\\{q\\}\\cup S\\cdot c))\\Rightarrow\\Sol_k(S\\cdot q)$, so a refuted\n   272\tsibling stays refuted along any path whose steps pass the test.\\lean{searchSol_reinsert, not_searchSol_of_oldMove}\n   273\tThe test is necessary; the smallest graph that shows it is the path on five\n   274\tvertices.\\lean{reinsert_needs_test}\n   275\t\n   276\t\\paragraph{The memo.} A refuted state is recorded and refused when met again.\n   277\tWhether a state with $b(S)\\le k$ has a solution depends on the state alone, not\n   278\ton the path that reached it.\\lean{solvable_iff_of_cl_eq} One implementation of\n   279\tours declined to run the memo beside the old move, on the ground that a\n   280\trefutation obtained with inherited old moves depends on the ancestors. A node\n   281\ttaken on its own, with an old move that is not in fact refuted, can indeed\n   282\trecord a solvable state;\\lean{exec_fake_oldMove} the next theorem shows that this\n   283\tnever happens in a real run.\n   284\t\n   285\t\\subsection{The repaired search is sound}\n   286\t\\label{sec:theorem}\n   287\t\n   288\t\\begin{proposition}\\label{prop:filter}\n   289\tAt every state with the invariant, for every set $Q$ of refuted old moves, the\n   290\tfilter definite $\\to$ subset $\\to$ better with both repairs is node-sound.\\lean{repairedFullFilter_sound}\n   291\t\\end{proposition}\n   292\t\\begin{proof}\n   293\tIf the definite move fires, Theorem~\\ref{thm:repaired} applies. Otherwise the\n   294\tsubset rule keeps a survivor with a solution; let $w$ be the earliest. If the\n   295\tbetter move dropped $w$, it cited an earlier survivor that the repaired\n   296\targument proves solvable, contradicting the choice of $w$.\n   297\t\\end{proof}\n   298\t\n   299\t\\begin{theorem}[runs are sound]\\label{thm:runs}\n   300\tLet the filter be node-sound and return only playable customers. If at the start\n   301\tof a run the invariant holds, every memo entry is a genuine refutation, and\n   302\tevery old move is refuted, then the run leaves only genuine memo entries, and if\n   303\tit answers \\emph{false} at $S$ then $\\neg\\Sol_k(S)$. This holds for any child\n   304\torder and any memo policy, with the old move on or off.\\lean{Exec.sound}\n   305\t\\end{theorem}\n   306\tThe proof is an induction over the run, in the order in which subtrees complete:\n   307\tevery old move a node holds is a sibling subtree that completed with \\emph{false}\n   308\tearlier in the same run, or an inherited one that passed the reinsertion test.\n   309\t\n   310\t\\begin{theorem}[the revised search is sound]\\label{thm:main}\n   311\tRun the customer search with free moves, the memo, old moves and the filter\n   312\tdefinite $\\to$ subset $\\to$ better, the definite move firing only on a\n   313\thereditarily definite customer and the better move citing only under the\n   314\trepaired premise. If it refutes $k$ from the root, then no closing order costs at\n   315\tmost $k$, $\\pw(G)>k-1$, and on the MOSP graph of an instance $M$ with a\n   316\trequirement, $Z(M)>k$.\\lean{exec_repairedFullFilter_sound, exec_repairedFullFilter_pathwidth, exec_repairedFullFilter_mospValue}\n   317\t\\end{theorem}\n   318\t\n   319\tWhat the Lean model covers is the search as specified; that our C and Python\n   320\timplement it is checked, not proved. A transcription of the Lean filter agreed\n   321\twith the production filter on 16,244,090 node checks over graphs on 1 to 17\n   322\tvertices; the matching agreed with the hereditary premise on 36,939,226\n   323\tcandidates; and a search written from the theorems agreed with the C, the Python\n   324\tand an exact oracle on 17,431,232 runs over 52,592 graphs on 1 to 17\n   325\tvertices.\\footnote{\\texttt{paper2/solver\\_fix.md}, items 01--05, and\n   326\t\\texttt{paper2/revised\\_algorithm.md}, section~4.4.4, with the regenerate\n   327\tcommands.} Under the published rules the filter is not\n   328\tnode-sound,\\lean{not_codeFilterSound_cexGraph} and in a check of 570,206 runs with\n   329\tmemo and old move it lost the last solution at a node in 58 runs, all on graphs\n   330\tbuilt around Counterexample~\\ref{cex:definite} and all at $k$ equal to the\n   331\toptimum. Every one found a solution through another branch and answered\n   332\tcorrectly. No whole instance on which the published rules answer \\emph{unsat}\n   333\twrongly is known.\n   334\t\n   335\t\\subsection{The search in pathwidth language}\n   336\t\\label{sec:layouts}\n   337\t\n   338\tFor a set $T$ of vertices write $N(T)$ for its \\emph{border}, the vertices\n   339\toutside $T$ with a neighbour inside, and $d(T)=|N(T)|$, in the notation of\n   340\t\\citet[p.~390]{KobayashiKomuroTamaki2014}. A vertex sequence is $w$-feasible when\n   341\tevery prefix has $d\\le w$, and $\\vs(G)$ is the least $w$ for which some\n   342\tpermutation is $w$-feasible. Table~\\ref{tab:dictionary} translates.\n   343\t\n   344\t\\begin{table}[t]\n   345\t\\centering\\small\n   346\t\\caption{The customer search in the vocabulary of exact pathwidth algorithms.}\n   347\t\\label{tab:dictionary}\n   348\t\\begin{tabularx}{\\linewidth}{@{}>{\\raggedright}p{3.6cm}>{\\raggedright}X>{\\raggedright\\arraybackslash}p{5.2cm}@{}}\n   349\t\\toprule\n   350\tCustomer search & Layout & Lean \\\\\n   351\t\\midrule\n   352\tclosing order from $\\emptyset$ & vertex sequence, read forwards & \\path{orderOfLayout} \\\\\n   353\topen stacks $b(T)$ & border size $d(T)$ & \\path{openStacks_eq_card_boundary} \\\\\n   354\t$\\cost(T,c)$ & $d(T\\cup\\{c\\})+1$ & \\path{stepCost_eq_card_boundary_add_one} \\\\\n   355\t$\\Sol_k(\\emptyset)$ & $\\vs(G)\\le k-1$ & \\path{searchSol_empty_iff_pathwidth_add_one_le} \\\\\n   356\tfree closure $\\cl(T)$ & full set of $T$ \\citep[p.~328]{SuchanVillanger2009} & \\path{mem_cl_iff} \\\\\n   357\tmemo & failure table & -- \\\\\n   358\tdefinite move, repaired & commitment to the child & \\path{isHereditarilyDefinite_iff_isCommittable} \\\\\n   359\t\\bottomrule\n   360\t\\end{tabularx}\n   361\t\\end{table}\n   362\t\n   363\t\\citet[Algorithm~1, p.~393]{KobayashiKomuroTamaki2014} decide whether a vertex\n   364\tset extends to a $w$-feasible permutation: replace the set by a committed\n   365\textension, answer from a failure table or at $V$, recurse on each feasible\n   366\tone-vertex extension, record failures. The customer search is this loop with\n   367\t$w=k-1$: the free closure and the definite move play the part of the commitment,\n   368\tthe memo is the failure table, and the cost cut is the feasibility test.\n   369\t\\citet[\\S3.2]{CoudertMazauricNisse2014} has the same three parts in a branch and\n   370\tbound. Neither line cites the other in the papers we hold: \\citet{ChuStuckey2009}\n   371\tpredates the commitments and both 2014 papers, and none of the pathwidth papers\n   372\tcites it.\n   373\t\n   374\t\\paragraph{Commitments.} \\citet[p.~142]{Kitsunai2016}, stating Tamaki's notion,\n   375\tcall an extension $\\tau$ of $\\sigma$ \\emph{committable} when $d(X')\\ge d(V(\\tau))$\n   376\tfor every $X'$ with $V(\\sigma)\\subseteq X'\\subseteq V(\\tau)$; their Lemma~1 says\n   377\tthat if $\\sigma$ extends to a feasible permutation, so does $\\tau$.\n   378\t\n   379\t\\begin{lemma}\\label{lem:endpoint}\n   380\tFor $q\\notin S$ and $X=\\cl(S\\cup\\{q\\})$: the published premise holds iff\n   381\t$d(X)\\le d(S)$, the commitment condition at the single set $X'=S$; and the\n   382\trepaired premise holds iff $X$ is a commitment from $S$, the condition at every\n   383\t$X'$ between them.\\lean{isDefinite_iff_endpoint, isHereditarilyDefinite_iff_isCommittable}\n   384\t\\end{lemma}\n   385\t\n   386\tSo Theorem~\\ref{thm:repaired} is the commitment lemma at the child, and the\n   387\tpublished Theorem~1 is a commitment whose interior condition was dropped. In\n   388\tCounterexample~\\ref{cex:definite} the endpoint test passes with $d(X)=3=d(S)$,\n   389\twhile the interior set $\\{2,3,4\\}$ has border 2.\\lean{cex_isDefinite_not_isCommittable}\n   390\tThe published premise is not wasted: by \\citet[Lemma~10, Cor.~2,\n   391\tpp.~148--149]{Kitsunai2016} it guarantees a commitment to the least-border set\n   392\tbetween $S$ and $X$, which need not contain $q$.\\lean{exists_isCommittable_of_isDefinite}\n   393\tIn the counterexample that set is $\\{2,3,4\\}$.\\lean{cex_isCommittable_234}\n   394\tCommitting there when the matching fails is a second repair that gives up no\n   395\tpruning; we have not built it.\n   396\t\n   397\tA commitment of depth one, $S$ to $S\\cup\\{q\\}$, holds iff $\\open(q,S)\\le1$.\\lean{isCommittable_insert_iff}\n   398\tThe greedy step of \\citet[Lemma~3, p.~49]{CoudertMazauricNisse2014} is exactly\n   399\tthat condition,\\lean{isGreedyStep_iff} and the push rule of\n   400\t\\citet[Rule~1, p.~330]{SuchanVillanger2009} is a case of it. So every published\n   401\tpathwidth rule of this kind lies in the region $\\open\\le1$, where the published\n   402\tdefinite move is right; \\citet[p.~388, Table~2]{KobayashiKomuroTamaki2014} report\n   403\tdepth-one commitments as ``extremely effective''. Chu and Stuckey's Theorem~1\n   404\treaches into $\\open\\ge2$, and that is where it fails. For the cost of the test,\n   405\t\\citet[Cor.~2]{Kitsunai2016} find the least border between the ends of a given\n   406\textension by a minimum $s$--$t$ separator; Theorem~\\ref{thm:matching} is that\n   407\tcomputation for the single target $X$, where it becomes a bipartite matching.\n   408\t\n   409\tTable~\\ref{tab:known} sums up what is known, limited to the pathwidth papers we\n   410\thold \\citep{SuchanVillanger2009,Bodlaender2012,CoudertMazauricNisse2014,CoudertMazauricNisse2016,KobayashiKomuroTamaki2014,Kitsunai2016}.\n   411\t``None found'' is not a claim of priority.\n   412\t\n   413\t\\begin{table}[t]\n   414\t\\centering\\small\n   415\t\\caption{Each rule of the customer search against the exact-pathwidth literature.}\n   416\t\\label{tab:known}\n   417\t\\begin{tabularx}{\\linewidth}{@{}>{\\raggedright}p{3.2cm}>{\\raggedright}X>{\\raggedright\\arraybackslash}p{4.1cm}@{}}\n   418\t\\toprule\n   419\tRule & Published counterpart & Status \\\\\n   420\t\\midrule\n   421\tfree move & full sets \\citep{SuchanVillanger2009}; \\citet[Prop.~3]{Kitsunai2016} & known \\\\\n   422\tdefinite move, $\\open\\le1$ & depth-one commitments; greedy step; push rule & known \\\\\n   423\tdefinite move, published & -- & false (Cex.~\\ref{cex:definite}) \\\\\n   424\tdefinite move, repaired & commitment lemma \\citep[Lemma~1]{Kitsunai2016} & known; matching test (Thm~\\ref{thm:matching}) not found \\\\\n   425\tsubset rule & none found & proved here \\\\\n   426\tbetter move, published & none found & false \\\\\n   427\tbetter move, repaired & none found & proved here \\\\\n   428\told move & none found & proved here \\\\\n   429\tmemo & subset DP \\citep{Bodlaender2012}; prefix and failure tables & known; with the old move, proved here (Thm~\\ref{thm:runs}) \\\\\n   430\t\\bottomrule\n   431\t\\end{tabularx}\n   432\t\\end{table}\n   433\t\n   434\t\\paragraph{Prior reports.} We looked for any earlier report of the error. We\n   435\tlisted the works citing \\citet{ChuStuckey2009}, Chu's thesis and its IJCAI 2013\n   436\tabstract in OpenAlex, Semantic Scholar and OpenCitations: 141 works. Of the 38\n   437\tthat cite the CP paper we read 24 in full (63\\%), and of the 103 that cite only\n   438\tthe thesis, 32 (31\\%).\\footnote{\\texttt{python3 -m paper2.prior\\_art\\_sweep};\n   439\tthe table of citers is in \\texttt{paper2/prior\\_art\\_counterexample.md}.} No\n   440\twork read gives a counterexample, an erratum or a correction. One,\n   441\t\\citet{Fink2012}, restates the definite move as true with a stronger premise,\n   442\trefuted above. Three use an implementation of the rules and report no failure;\n   443\tamong them \\citet{Frinhani2018} ran the original code to obtain the optima that\n   444\t\\citet","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false,"persistedOutputPath":"/home/al/.claude/projects/-home-al-dev-MOSP/1323e98e-623f-4534-a362-41a379cbd2ab/tool-results/bqu7li2ck.txt","persistedOutputSize":39194}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cfm699PNfh4SJGD3Z1xWd","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01E18DwdG8TKdmqtjyLNkkdT","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/al/.claude/projects/-home-al-dev-MOSP/1323e98e-623f-4534-a362-41a379cbd2ab/tool-results/bqu7li2ck.txt"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1024,"cache_read_input_tokens":112766,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1024,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":16,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"1323e98e-623f-4534-a362-41a379cbd2ab","uuid":"92d1faba-9bb5-4241-8a4c-288c9b274c23","timestamp":"2026-10-06T13:49:01.088Z","request_id":"req_011Cfm698xpnLoSCWPRA2stL","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01E18DwdG8TKdmqtjyLNkkdT":{"file_path":"/home/al/.claude/projects/-home-al-dev-MOSP/1323e98e-623f-4534-a362-41a379cbd2ab/tool-results/bqu7li2ck.txt"}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01E18DwdG8TKdmqtjyLNkkdT","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t     1\t% Section 4. Sources: paper2/revised_algorithm.md (4.1-4.7), search_soundness.md,\n2\t     2\t% solver_fix.md (cost, re-certification), prior_art_counterexample.md (prior\n3\t     3\t% art), certificates.md (proof object), dataset.md (the dataset). Lean:\n4\t     4\t% lean/MOSPFormalization/Search/. Tables from tables/ (make_tables.py).\n5\t     5\t% Page numbers for Chu & Stuckey (2009) are those of the preprint\n6\t     6\t% literature/chu_stuckey_2009.pdf; for Chu (2011) the printed ones.\n7\t     7\t\\section{An exact open-stacks search, read as a pathwidth solver}\n8\t     8\t\\label{sec:search}\n9\t     9\t\n10\t    10\tThe best exact method for MOSP on the standard benchmarks is the \\emph{customer\n11\t    11\tsearch} of \\citet{ChuStuckey2009}. It searches over the orders in which the\n12\t    12\tcustomers' stacks close, not over product sequences, and prunes with dominance\n13\t    13\trules, three of them stated as theorems (their Theorems~1, 2 and~3) and a fourth,\n14\t    14\tthe subset rule, in their \\S2. \\citet{ChuStuckey2009} place MOSP among ``graph\n15\t    15\tpath-width and gate matrix layout'', citing \\citet{LY2002}, but do not use\n16\t    16\tpathwidth in the search. By Theorem~\\ref{thm:mosp} it is nevertheless a\n17\t    17\tpathwidth algorithm: any graph is the MOSP graph of the instance with one\n18\t    18\tcustomer per vertex and one product per edge, whose optimum is pathwidth plus\n19\t    19\tone.\n20\t    20\t\n21\t    21\tThis section reads the search that way, and finds three things. Two of the\n22\t    22\tpublished theorems are false, and so are their restatements in Chu's thesis\n23\t    23\t\\citep{Chu2011} and, with a stronger premise, in \\citet{Fink2012}. The repaired\n24\t    24\tfirst rule is an instance of a known pathwidth theorem, Tamaki's commitment\n25\t    25\tlemma; the published rule is that lemma with its interior condition dropped. And\n26\t    26\tthe whole repaired search is proved sound in Lean. All Lean names in this\n27\t    27\tsection are in \\leanfile{lean/MOSPFormalization/Search/}, which has no\n28\t    28\t\\texttt{sorry} and the standard axioms only.\n29\t    29\t\n30\t    30\t\\subsection{The search decides pathwidth}\n31\t    31\t\\label{sec:search-def}\n32\t    32\t\n33\t    33\t\\citet[p.~4]{ChuStuckey2009} observe that the products matter only through the\n34\t    34\tMOSP graph. So fix a graph $G=(C,E)$ on the customers and an integer $k\\ge0$.\n35\t    35\tFor a customer $c$ and a set $T\\subseteq C$ of closed customers, write\n36\t    36\t$N[c]$ for the closed neighbourhood of $c$, and\n37\t    37\t\\begin{align*}\n38\t    38\tO(T) &= \\textstyle\\bigcup_{c\\in T}N[c] && \\text{the stacks opened once $T$ has closed},\\\\\n39\t    39\t\\fin(X) &= \\{c : N[c]\\subseteq X\\} && \\text{the customers free to close when $X$ is open},\\\\\n40\t    40\t\\cl(T) &= \\fin(O(T)) && \\text{the \\emph{free closure} of $T$},\\\\\n41\t    41\tb(T) &= |O(T)\\setminus T| && \\text{the stacks open after $T$ has closed},\\\\\n42\t    42\to(c,T) &= N[c]\\setminus O(T),\\quad \\open(c,T)=|o(c,T)| && \\text{the stacks closing $c$ would newly open.}\n43\t    43\t\\end{align*}\n44\t    44\tA \\emph{move} at $T$ closes one customer $c\\notin T$, at cost\n45\t    45\t$\\cost(T,c)=|O(T\\cup\\{c\\})\\setminus T| = b(T\\cup\\{c\\})+1$, the stacks open at\n46\t    46\tthat moment counting $c$; it is \\emph{playable} when $\\cost(T,c)\\le k$. Write\n47\t    47\t$P_k(T)$ when some order of $C\\setminus T$ has every step playable. On entering\n48\t    48\ta node the search closes every free customer, so it stands only at\n49\t    49\t\\emph{states}, sets with $S=\\cl(S)$, and the \\emph{child} of $S$ by $c$ is\n50\t    50\t$S\\cdot c=\\cl(S\\cup\\{c\\})$. The search's predicate $\\Sol_k(S)$ holds when $S=C$,\n51\t    51\tor when some playable $c$ has $\\Sol_k(S\\cdot c)$.\\lean{stepCost, Solvable, SearchSol}\n52\t    52\tEvery node the search visits satisfies the invariant $b(S)\\le k$.\n53\t    53\t\n54\t    54\t\\begin{lemma}[free moves never hurt]\\label{lem:F}\n55\t    55\tIf $b(T)\\le k$, then $P_k(T)\\iff\\Sol_k(\\cl(T))$.\\lean{solvable_iff_searchSol_cl}\n56\t    56\t\\end{lemma}\n57\t    57\tClosing a customer whose neighbourhood is open opens nothing, never makes a\n58\t    58\tlater step dearer, and leaves one fewer stack open. The hypothesis is needed:\n59\t    59\tclosing the free customers one by one costs $b(T)$ at each step.\n60\t    60\t\n61\t    61\t\\begin{theorem}[the search decides pathwidth]\\label{thm:decides}\n62\t    62\tFor nonempty $C$, $\\Sol_k(\\emptyset)\\iff\\pw(G)+1\\le k$; on the MOSP graph of an\n63\t    63\tinstance $M$ with a requirement, $\\Sol_k(\\emptyset)\\iff Z(M)\\le k$.\\lean{searchSol_empty_iff_pathwidth_add_one_le, searchSol_mospGraph_iff_mospValue_le}\n64\t    64\t\\end{theorem}\n65\t    65\t\\begin{proof}\n66\t    66\tFor a closing order $c_1,\\dots,c_n$ with prefixes $S_i$, the $i$-th step costs\n67\t    67\t$1+|\\partial S_i|$, where $\\partial X$ is the set of vertices outside $X$ with a\n68\t    68\tneighbour in $X$. Read backwards as a layout, the cut separating $C\\setminus S_i$\n69\t    69\tfrom $S_i$ counts exactly $\\partial S_i$. So the cost of the order is the vertex\n70\t    70\tseparation of the reversed layout plus one, and Lemma~\\ref{lem:F} at the root and\n71\t    71\tTheorem~\\ref{thm:vs} finish the proof. The Lean statement goes through\n72\t    72\tnarrowness \\citep{LY2002ref11}, which counts the forward order directly.\n73\t    73\t\\end{proof}\n74\t    74\t\n75\t    75\tSo a refutation, $\\neg\\Sol_k(\\emptyset)$, may only mean $\\pw(G)>k-1$. The rest\n76\t    76\tof the section asks whether the pruning rules preserve that meaning.\n77\t    77\t\n78\t    78\t\\paragraph{What a rule must satisfy.} At a state $S$ the search has a set $Q$ of\n79\t    79\t\\emph{old moves} (Section~\\ref{sec:other-rules}). Its candidates are the\n80\t    80\tcustomers outside $S\\cup Q$; the \\emph{cost cut} keeps the playable ones, $P$,\n81\t    81\tin index order, and a \\emph{filter} keeps a sublist $L\\subseteq P$. The filter\n82\t    82\tis \\emph{node-sound} at $(S,Q)$ if, whenever every $q\\in Q$ has\n83\t    83\t$\\neg\\Sol_k(S\\cdot q)$, $\\Sol_k(S)$ implies $\\Sol_k(S\\cdot c)$ for some $c\\in L$.\\lean{NodeSoundAt}\n84\t    84\tEach rule justifies a discarded move $r$ by a \\emph{covering}, a move $c$ with\n85\t    85\t$\\Sol_k(S\\cdot r)\\Rightarrow\\Sol_k(S\\cdot c)$. A node is sound when every link a\n86\t    86\trule cites is a true covering and every chain of links from a discarded\n87\t    87\tcandidate ends in $L$ or in $Q$; the second condition can fail through a cycle\n88\t    88\teven when every link is true. The filter runs the definite move, then the subset\n89\t    89\trule, then the better move, each once.\n90\t    90\t\n91\t    91\t\\subsection{The definite move is false as published}\n92\t    92\t\\label{sec:definite}\n93\t    93\t\n94\t    94\t\\citet[\\S3.1, p.~6]{ChuStuckey2009} state:\n95\t    95\t\\begin{quote}\n96\t    96\t\\textbf{Theorem 1.} Suppose S ++ [q] is playable and close(q, S) $\\ge$\n97\t    97\topen(q, S), then if U$'$ = S ++ R is a solution, there exists a solution\n98\t    98\tU = S ++ [q] ++ R$'$,\n99\t    99\t\\end{quote}\n100\t   100\twhere $\\close(q,S)$ counts the customers $d$ with $o(d,S)\\subseteq o(q,S)$\n101\t   101\t(p.~4). The rule keeps $q$ alone. We read $d$ as ranging over customers not yet\n102\t   102\tclosed, as the code does; read literally, closed customers count too, which\n103\t   103\tweakens the premise, and both readings are refuted below. \\citet[Thm~6.3.6,\n104\t   104\tp.~142]{Chu2011} restates the theorem with the same premise and proof, reworded\n105\t   105\tfor $k$ stacks. Write $X=S\\cdot q$.\n106\t   106\t\n107\t   107\t\\begin{lemma}[what the premise says]\\label{lem:premise}\n108\t   108\t$\\close(q,S)=|X\\setminus S|$. Hence the premise holds iff $b(X)\\le b(S)$: the\n109\t   109\tchild has no more open stacks than the parent.\\lean{closeCount_eq, isDefinite_iff}\n110\t   110\t\\end{lemma}\n111\t   111\t\n112\t   112\t\\begin{figure}[t]\n113\t   113\t\\centering\n114\t   114\t\\includegraphics[width=0.85\\linewidth]{definite_counterexample.pdf}\n115\t   115\t\\caption{The graph of Counterexample~\\ref{cex:definite} at the state where the\n116\t   116\tdefinite move fails. Customer 2 is closed; 1, 3 and 4 are open. Moving 0 opens\n117\t   117\tthe new stacks 0, 7 and 11 and closes 0, 3 and 4, so open = close = 3 and the\n118\t   118\trule keeps 0 alone. But 3 and 4 have the single new stack 0 between them:\n119\t   119\tclosing them first gains two stacks for the price of one, which a solution\n120\t   120\tstarting with 0 cannot match. Regenerate with \\texttt{python -m\n121\t   121\tpaper2.counterexample\\_figure}, which recomputes every labelled fact.}\n122\t   122\t\\label{fig:cex}\n123\t   123\t\\end{figure}\n124\t   124\t\n125\t   125\t\\begin{counterexample}\\label{cex:definite}\n126\t   126\tLet $G$ be the graph on customers $0,\\dots,13$ with the 26 edges\n127\t   127\t\\begin{gather*}\n128\t   128\t0\\text{--}3,\\ 0\\text{--}4,\\ 0\\text{--}7,\\ 0\\text{--}11,\\ 1\\text{--}2,\\ 1\\text{--}5,\\ 1\\text{--}6,\\ 2\\text{--}3,\\ 2\\text{--}4,\\ 5\\text{--}9,\\ 5\\text{--}11,\\ 5\\text{--}12,\\ 6\\text{--}8,\\\\\n129\t   129\t6\\text{--}13,\\ 7\\text{--}9,\\ 7\\text{--}10,\\ 7\\text{--}13,\\ 8\\text{--}9,\\ 8\\text{--}10,\\ 8\\text{--}11,\\ 9\\text{--}10,\\ 9\\text{--}11,\\ 10\\text{--}11,\\ 10\\text{--}12,\\ 10\\text{--}13,\\ 12\\text{--}13,\n130\t   130\t\\end{gather*}\n131\t   131\twith $S=\\{2\\}$, $k=6$ and $q=0$ (Figure~\\ref{fig:cex}). Then $O(S)=\\{1,2,3,4\\}$,\n132\t   132\t$o(0,S)=\\{0,7,11\\}$ and $\\cost(S,0)=6$, so $0$ is playable; the customers with\n133\t   133\t$o(d,S)\\subseteq\\{0,7,11\\}$ are $0$, $3$ and $4$, so $\\close(0,S)=3\\ge\\open(0,S)$;\n134\t   134\tand $0$ comes first in index order, so the rule keeps only $0$. A solution\n135\t   135\texists from $S$: the order\n136\t   136\t\\[1,3,4,6,12,13,0,5,7,8,9,10,11\\]\n137\t   137\tcosts at most 6. None exists\n138\t   138\tfrom the child $X=\\{0,2,3,4\\}$: only seven sets are reachable from $X$ by moves\n139\t   139\tof cost at most 6, and none is $C$.\\lean{definiteMove_counterexample, cexFamily_closed}\n140\t   140\t\\end{counterexample}\n141\t   141\t\n142\t   142\tThe published implication, as a universal statement, is refuted in Lean under\n143\t   143\tboth readings of $\\close$, and so is the thesis's Theorem~6.3.6.\\lean{chuStuckey_theorem1_false, chuStuckey_theorem1_false_literal, chuThesis_theorem636_false}\n144\t   144\tThe smallest graph on which the rule loses the last solution has 14 customers:\n145\t   145\tthere is none on any graph to 9 vertices, by exhaustion, and none on the 88,802\n146\t   146\tconnected graphs on 10 vertices with at most 14 edges. The counterexample was\n147\t   147\tfound by a search over a family of gadgets built from the failed proof, and\n148\t   148\tminimised by deletion. \\citet[pp.~27--28]{Fink2012} restates the rule with a\n149\t   149\tstronger premise, counting only dominated customers of smaller index than $q$.\n150\t   150\tThe graph above refutes it under no labelling, but a third twin of 3 and 4 gives\n151\t   151\ta 15-customer graph that does.\\lean{fink_theorem1_false}\n152\t   152\t\n153\t   153\t\\paragraph{Why the published proof fails.} The proof moves $q$ to the front of a\n154\t   154\tsolution and claims that after each prefix ``we have at most open(q, S) extra\n155\t   155\tstacks open, but at least close(q, S) extra stacks closed'' (p.~6). At a prefix\n156\t   156\t$T\\supseteq S$ that does not yet contain $q$, moving $q$ forward changes the open\n157\t   157\tstacks by $b(T\\cup X)-b(T) = |N[q]\\setminus O(T)|-|X\\setminus T|$. At $T=S$ this\n158\t   158\tis $\\open-\\close\\le0$. But customers of $X$ that the solution has \\emph{already}\n159\t   159\tclosed are in $T$ and no longer count as extra stacks closed, while the stacks\n160\t   160\t$q$ opens may still be new. In the example, a solution may begin with 3, after\n161\t   161\twhich 4 is free: at $T=\\{2,3,4\\}$, moving 0 forward costs one more stack. A\n162\t   162\tsecond, independent slip: even where the conclusion holds, the literal sequence\n163\t   163\t$S \\mathbin{+\\!\\!+} [q] \\mathbin{+\\!\\!+} R'$ need not be a solution, because a\n164\t   164\tcustomer that $q$ frees stays open until its turn; the argument needs the free\n165\t   165\tmoves.\n166\t   166\t\n167\t   167\t\\paragraph{The repair.} Call $q$ \\emph{hereditarily definite} at $S$ when\n168\t   168\t$b(X)\\le b(B)$ for every $B$ with $S\\subseteq B\\subseteq X$ and $q\\notin B$. By\n169\t   169\tLemma~\\ref{lem:premise}, the published premise is the case $B=S$. In the\n170\t   170\tcounterexample the repair fails at $B=\\{2,3,4\\}$, where $b(B)=2<3=b(X)$.\\lean{IsHereditarilyDefinite, not_isHereditarilyDefinite_cex}\n171\t   171\t\n172\t   172\t\\begin{lemma}[open stacks are submodular]\\label{lem:submod}\n173\t   173\tFor all $A,B\\subseteq C$, $b(A\\cup B)+b(A\\cap B)\\le b(A)+b(B)$.\\lean{openStacks_submodular}\n174\t   174\t\\end{lemma}\n175\t   175\t\\begin{proof}\n176\t   176\t$b(T)=|O(T)|-|T|$, $O(A\\cup B)=O(A)\\cup O(B)$ and $O(A\\cap B)\\subseteq\n177\t   177\tO(A)\\cap O(B)$; the customers themselves balance exactly.\n178\t   178\t\\end{proof}\n179\t   179\t\n180\t   180\t\\begin{theorem}[the repaired definite move is sound]\\label{thm:repaired}\n181\t   181\tIf $q\\notin S$ is hereditarily definite at $S$, then $P_k(S)\\Rightarrow P_k(X)$.\n182\t   182\tAt a state with the invariant, $\\Sol_k(S)\\Rightarrow\\Sol_k(S\\cdot q)$.\\lean{solvable_cl_insert_of_hereditarilyDefinite, searchSol_cl_insert_of_hereditarilyDefinite}\n183\t   183\t\\end{theorem}\n184\t   184\t\\begin{proof}\n185\t   185\tTake an order from $S$ of cost at most $k$ with prefixes $T_i$, in which $q$ is\n186\t   186\tthe $j$-th step, and follow it from $X$, skipping customers already in $X$. A\n187\t   187\tstep $i<j$ closes $c_i$ at $T_{i-1}\\cup X$. With $Z=T_i$ we have\n188\t   188\t$S\\subseteq Z\\cap X\\subseteq X$ and $q\\notin Z$, so the hypothesis gives\n189\t   189\t$b(X)\\le b(Z\\cap X)$, and Lemma~\\ref{lem:submod} gives $b(Z\\cup X)\\le b(Z)$: the\n190\t   190\tstep costs no more than the original. After step $j-1$ the closed set contains\n191\t   191\t$T_j$ with the same opened set, and the rest of the order applies. The search's\n192\t   192\tform follows by Lemma~\\ref{lem:F}.\n193\t   193\t\\end{proof}\n194\t   194\t\n195\t   195\tThis theorem is not new. The hereditary premise is the commitment condition of\n196\t   196\tTamaki, and the proof is the proof of the commitment lemma,\n197\t   197\t\\citet[Lemma~1, p.~142]{Kitsunai2016}; Section~\\ref{sec:layouts} makes the\n198\t   198\tcorrespondence exact. What we did not find in the papers we hold is the\n199\t   199\tfollowing cheap form of the test.\n200\t   200\t\n201\t   201\t\\begin{theorem}[the repair is a matching condition]\\label{thm:matching}\n202\t   202\tLet $Y=X\\setminus(S\\cup\\{q\\})$, and join each $d\\in Y$ to the stacks of\n203\t   203\t$o(d,S)\\subseteq o(q,S)$. Then $q$ is hereditarily definite at $S$ iff this\n204\t   204\tbipartite graph has a matching with at least $\\open(q,S)-1$ edges.\\lean{isHereditarilyDefinite_iff_hasDefiniteMatching}\n205\t   205\t\\end{theorem}\n206\t   206\t\\begin{proof}\n207\t   207\tThe sets of the repair are $B=S\\cup D$, $D\\subseteq Y$, and\n208\t   208\t$b(X)\\le b(S\\cup D)$ iff $|D|-|o(D)|\\le\\delta$ with\n209\t   209\t$\\delta=|Y|+1-\\open(q,S)$, where $o(D)$ is the union of the $o(d,S)$. By the\n210\t   210\tdeficiency form of Hall's theorem the largest matching has size\n211\t   211\t$|Y|-\\max_D(|D|-|o(D)|)$, which is at least $|Y|-\\delta=\\open(q,S)-1$ exactly\n212\t   212\twhen the repair holds. The Lean proof reduces the deficiency form to Mathlib's\n213\t   213\tHall theorem with $\\delta$ dummy stacks.\n214\t   214\t\\end{proof}\n215\t   215\t\n216\t   216\tIn words: the rule is safe exactly when all but one of the new stacks can be\n217\t   217\tpaired with a different freed customer who needed it. In the counterexample,\n218\t   218\tclosing 0 opens three new stacks and frees 3 and 4, both of whom needed only\n219\t   219\tstack 0: one pairing where two are required. Every $q$ with $\\open(q,S)\\le1$\n220\t   220\tpasses at once, so the published rule is right there.\\lean{isHereditarilyDefinite_of_openCount_le_one}\n221\t   221\t\n222\t   222\t\\subsection{The other rules}\n223\t   223\t\\label{sec:other-rules}\n224\t   224\t\n225\t   225\t\\paragraph{The subset rule.} ``If $o(c_i,S)\\subseteq o(c_j,S)$ and $i<j$ \\dots\\\n226\t   226\tmove $j$ can be removed'' \\citep[\\S2, p.~5]{ChuStuckey2009}. As coded, $d$\n227\t   227\tdominates $r$ when $o(d,S)\\subseteq o(r,S)$ and the inclusion is strict or $d<r$;\n228\t   228\tevery dominated playable $r$ is dropped, with a fallback that keeps $P$ if all\n229\t   229\twould go. The covering is sound for every set $S$: if $o(d,S)\\subseteq o(r,S)$\n230\t   230\tand $r$ is playable, then $d$ is playable and $\\Sol_k(S\\cdot r)\\Rightarrow\n231\t   231\t\\Sol_k(S\\cdot d)$, because $d$ is free after $r$ and closing $d$ then $r$ reaches\n232\t   232\tthe same state at no greater cost. With the index tie-break, domination is a\n233\t   233\tstrict order, so an undominated move with a solution survives, and the fallback\n234\t   234\tnever fires when a solution exists.\\lean{searchSol_cl_insert_of_newlyOpened_subset, subsetFilter_sound}\n235\t   235\tThe tie-break is necessary: without it, two customers with equal new stacks\n236\t   236\tdominate each other and both go, which loses a solution on a 7-customer\n237\t   237\tgraph.\\lean{noTieBreak_counterexample}\n238\t   238\t\n239\t   239\t\\paragraph{The better move.} \\citet[\\S3.2, p.~6]{ChuStuckey2009}: ``Suppose\n240\t   240\tS ++ [q] and S ++ [r, q] are playable and close(q, S $\\cup$ \\{r\\}) $\\ge$\n241\t   241\topen(q, S $\\cup$ \\{r\\}) then if U$'$ = S ++ [r] ++ R is a solution there exists a\n242\t   242\tsolution U = S ++ [q] ++ R$'$.'' Its premise is Theorem~1's premise for $q$ at the\n243\t   243\tchild $\\cl(S\\cup\\{r\\})$, together with playability of $r$ then $q$, and its\n244\t   244\tconclusion swaps $q$ and $r$.\\lean{isBetter_iff} The swap is sound; the appeal to\n245\t   245\tTheorem~1 is not. On the graph of Counterexample~\\ref{cex:definite} at the root\n246\t   246\twith $k=6$, the definite move does not fire, customers 0 and 2 both survive the\n247\t   247\tsubset rule, and the premise holds for $r=2$, $q=0$, so the rule drops 2 citing\n248\t   248\t0. But $\\{2\\}$ has a solution and $\\{0\\}$ has none.\\lean{betterMove_counterexample, chuStuckey_theorem2_false}\n249\t   249\tAt that node another kept customer still has a solution, so this is a false link\n250\t   250\tand not a lost node; no node where the better move loses the last solution has\n251\t   251\tbeen found. \\citet[Thm~6.3.8, p.~143]{Chu2011} states the better move with a\n252\t   252\tdifferent premise, $\\close(q,S)\\ge\\open(q,S\\cup\\{r\\})$, and proves it through\n253\t   253\tTheorem~6.3.6. The same graph refutes it at $S=\\{2\\}$, $r=3$, $q=0$.\\lean{chuThesis_theorem638_false, chuThesis_theorem638_false_literal}\n254\t   254\tThe repair cites $q$ for $r$ only if $q$ is hereditarily definite at\n255\t   255\t$\\cl(S\\cup\\{r\\})$, one matching at the child; then the swap argument goes\n256\t   256\tthrough.\\lean{IsRepairedBetter, searchSol_cl_insert_of_repairedBetter}\n257\t   257\t\n258\t   258\tTwo earlier faults of this rule in our own implementation are now proved in\n259\t   259\tLean too: a close count that included the customers $r$ finishes on its own,\n260\t   260\tand an order of the rules in which the subset rule could cite a customer the\n261\t   261\tbetter move had already dropped, which loses a node through a cycle of true\n262\t   262\tlinks.\\lean{bugA_counterexample, bugB_counterexample}\n263\t   263\tThe second is an instance of the general observation of\n264\t   264\t\\citet[Prop.~3, Example~6]{Beck2025} that mutually dominating transitions must\n265\t   265\tbe tie-broken.\n266\t   266\t\n267\t   267\t\\paragraph{The old move.} \\citet[Thm~3, p.~7]{ChuStuckey2009} let a child inherit\n268\t   268\ta sibling $q$ whose subtree failed, provided that inserting $q$ before each\n269\t   269\tlater step keeps that step playable. The rule is sound as coded: if\n270\t   270\t$\\cost(S\\cup\\{q\\},c)\\le k$, then\n271\t   271\t$\\Sol_k(\\cl(\\{q\\}\\cup S\\cdot c))\\Rightarrow\\Sol_k(S\\cdot q)$, so a refuted\n272\t   272\tsibling stays refuted along any path whose steps pass the test.\\lean{searchSol_reinsert, not_searchSol_of_oldMove}\n273\t   273\tThe test is necessary; the smallest graph that shows it is the path on five\n274\t   274\tvertices.\\lean{reinsert_needs_test}\n275\t   275\t\n276\t   276\t\\paragraph{The memo.} A refuted state is recorded and refused when met again.\n277\t   277\tWhether a state with $b(S)\\le k$ has a solution depends on the state alone, not\n278\t   278\ton the path that reached it.\\lean{solvable_iff_of_cl_eq} One implementation of\n279\t   279\tours declined to run the memo beside the old move, on the ground that a\n280\t   280\trefutation obtained with inherited old moves depends on the ancestors. A node\n281\t   281\ttaken on its own, with an old move that is not in fact refuted, can indeed\n282\t   282\trecord a solvable state;\\lean{exec_fake_oldMove} the next theorem shows that this\n283\t   283\tnever happens in a real run.\n284\t   284\t\n285\t   285\t\\subsection{The repaired search is sound}\n286\t   286\t\\label{sec:theorem}\n287\t   287\t\n288\t   288\t\\begin{proposition}\\label{prop:filter}\n289\t   289\tAt every state with the invariant, for every set $Q$ of refuted old moves, the\n290\t   290\tfilter definite $\\to$ subset $\\to$ better with both repairs is node-sound.\\lean{repairedFullFilter_sound}\n291\t   291\t\\end{proposition}\n292\t   292\t\\begin{proof}\n293\t   293\tIf the definite move fires, Theorem~\\ref{thm:repaired} applies. Otherwise the\n294\t   294\tsubset rule keeps a survivor with a solution; let $w$ be the earliest. If the\n295\t   295\tbetter move dropped $w$, it cited an earlier survivor that the repaired\n296\t   296\targument proves solvable, contradicting the choice of $w$.\n297\t   297\t\\end{proof}\n298\t   298\t\n299\t   299\t\\begin{theorem}[runs are sound]\\label{thm:runs}\n300\t   300\tLet the filter be node-sound and return only playable customers. If at the start\n301\t   301\tof a run the invariant holds, every memo entry is a genuine refutation, and\n302\t   302\tevery old move is refuted, then the run leaves only genuine memo entries, and if\n303\t   303\tit answers \\emph{false} at $S$ then $\\neg\\Sol_k(S)$. This holds for any child\n304\t   304\torder and any memo policy, with the old move on or off.\\lean{Exec.sound}\n305\t   305\t\\end{theorem}\n306\t   306\tThe proof is an induction over the run, in the order in which subtrees complete:\n307\t   307\tevery old move a node holds is a sibling subtree that completed with \\emph{false}\n308\t   308\tearlier in the same run, or an inherited one that passed the reinsertion test.\n309\t   309\t\n310\t   310\t\\begin{theorem}[the revised search is sound]\\label{thm:main}\n311\t   311\tRun the customer search with free moves, the memo, old moves and the filter\n312\t   312\tdefinite $\\to$ subset $\\to$ better, the definite move firing only on a\n313\t   313\thereditarily definite customer and the better move citing only under the\n314\t   314\trepaired premise. If it refutes $k$ from the root, then no closing order costs at\n315\t   315\tmost $k$, $\\pw(G)>k-1$, and on the MOSP graph of an instance $M$ with a\n316\t   316\trequirement, $Z(M)>k$.\\lean{exec_repairedFullFilter_sound, exec_repairedFullFilter_pathwidth, exec_repairedFullFilter_mospValue}\n317\t   317\t\\end{theorem}\n318\t   318\t\n319\t   319\tWhat the Lean model covers is the search as specified; that our C and Python\n320\t   320\timplement it is checked, not proved. A transcription of the Lean filter agreed\n321\t   321\twith the production filter on 16,244,090 node checks over graphs on 1 to 17\n322\t   322\tvertices; the matching agreed with the hereditary premise on 36,939,226\n323\t   323\tcandidates; and a search written from the theorems agreed with the C, the Python\n324\t   324\tand an exact oracle on 17,431,232 runs over 52,592 graphs on 1 to 17\n325\t   325\tvertices.\\footnote{\\texttt{paper2/solver\\_fix.md}, items 01--05, and\n326\t   326\t\\texttt{paper2/revised\\_algorithm.md}, section~4.4.4, with the regenerate\n327\t   327\tcommands.} Under the published rules the filter is not\n328\t   328\tnode-sound,\\lean{not_codeFilterSound_cexGraph} and in a check of 570,206 runs with\n329\t   329\tmemo and old move it lost the last solution at a node in 58 runs, all on graphs\n330\t   330\tbuilt around Counterexample~\\ref{cex:definite} and all at $k$ equal to the\n331\t   331\toptimum. Every one found a solution through another branch and answered\n332\t   332\tcorrectly. No whole instance on which the published rules answer \\emph{unsat}\n333\t   333\twrongly is known.\n334\t   334\t\n335\t   335\t\\subsection{The search in pathwidth language}\n336\t   336\t\\label{sec:layouts}\n337\t   337\t\n338\t   338\tFor a set $T$ of vertices write $N(T)$ for its \\emph{border}, the vertices\n339\t   339\toutside $T$ with a neighbour inside, and $d(T)=|N(T)|$, in the notation of\n340\t   340\t\\citet[p.~390]{KobayashiKomuroTamaki2014}. A vertex sequence is $w$-feasible when\n341\t   341\tevery prefix has $d\\le w$, and $\\vs(G)$ is the least $w$ for which some\n342\t   342\tpermutation is $w$-feasible. Table~\\ref{tab:dictionary} translates.\n343\t   343\t\n344\t   344\t\\begin{table}[t]\n345\t   345\t\\centering\\small\n346\t   346\t\\caption{The customer search in the vocabulary of exact pathwidth algorithms.}\n347\t   347\t\\label{tab:dictionary}\n348\t   348\t\\begin{tabularx}{\\linewidth}{@{}>{\\raggedright}p{3.6cm}>{\\raggedright}X>{\\raggedright\\arraybackslash}p{5.2cm}@{}}\n349\t   349\t\\toprule\n350\t   350\tCustomer search & Layout & Lean \\\\\n351\t   351\t\\midrule\n352\t   352\tclosing order from $\\emptyset$ & vertex sequence, read forwards & \\path{orderOfLayout} \\\\\n353\t   353\topen stacks $b(T)$ & border size $d(T)$ & \\path{openStacks_eq_card_boundary} \\\\\n354\t   354\t$\\cost(T,c)$ & $d(T\\cup\\{c\\})+1$ & \\path{stepCost_eq_card_boundary_add_one} \\\\\n355\t   355\t$\\Sol_k(\\emptyset)$ & $\\vs(G)\\le k-1$ & \\path{searchSol_empty_iff_pathwidth_add_one_le} \\\\\n356\t   356\tfree closure $\\cl(T)$ & full set of $T$ \\citep[p.~328]{SuchanVillanger2009} & \\path{mem_cl_iff} \\\\\n357\t   357\tmemo & failure table & -- \\\\\n358\t   358\tdefinite move, repaired & commitment to the child & \\path{isHereditarilyDefinite_iff_isCommittable} \\\\\n359\t   359\t\\bottomrule\n360\t   360\t\\end{tabularx}\n361\t   361\t\\end{table}\n362\t   362\t\n363\t   363\t\\citet[Algorithm~1, p.~393]{KobayashiKomuroTamaki2014} decide whether a vertex\n364\t   364\tset extends to a $w$-feasible permutation: replace the set by a committed\n365\t   365\textension, answer from a failure table or at $V$, recurse on each feasible\n366\t   366\tone-vertex extension, record failures. The customer search is this loop with\n367\t   367\t$w=k-1$: the free closure and the definite move play the part of the commitment,\n368\t   368\tthe memo is the failure table, and the cost cut is the feasibility test.\n369\t   369\t\\citet[\\S3.2]{CoudertMazauricNisse2014} has the same three parts in a branch and\n370\t   370\tbound. Neither line cites the other in the papers we hold: \\citet{ChuStuckey2009}\n371\t   371\tpredates the commitments and both 2014 papers, and none of the pathwidth papers\n372\t   372\tcites it.\n373\t   373\t\n374\t   374\t\\paragraph{Commitments.} \\citet[p.~142]{Kitsunai2016}, stating Tamaki's notion,\n375\t   375\tcall an extension $\\tau$ of $\\sigma$ \\emph{committable} when $d(X')\\ge d(V(\\tau))$\n376\t   376\tfor every $X'$ with $V(\\sigma)\\subseteq X'\\subseteq V(\\tau)$; their Lemma~1 says\n377\t   377\tthat if $\\sigma$ extends to a feasible permutation, so does $\\tau$.\n378\t   378\t\n379\t   379\t\\begin{lemma}\\label{lem:endpoint}\n380\t   380\tFor $q\\notin S$ and $X=\\cl(S\\cup\\{q\\})$: the published premise holds iff\n381\t   381\t$d(X)\\le d(S)$, the commitment condition at the single set $X'=S$; and the\n382\t   382\trepaired premise holds iff $X$ is a commitment from $S$, the condition at every\n383\t   383\t$X'$ between them.\\lean{isDefinite_iff_endpoint, isHereditarilyDefinite_iff_isCommittable}\n384\t   384\t\\end{lemma}\n385\t   385\t\n386\t   386\tSo Theorem~\\ref{thm:repaired} is the commitment lemma at the child, and the\n387\t   387\tpublished Theorem~1 is a commitment whose interior condition was dropped. In\n388\t   388\tCounterexample~\\ref{cex:definite} the endpoint test passes with $d(X)=3=d(S)$,\n389\t   389\twhile the interior set $\\{2,3,4\\}$ has border 2.\\lean{cex_isDefinite_not_isCommittable}\n390\t   390\tThe published premise is not wasted: by \\citet[Lemma~10, Cor.~2,\n391\t   391\tpp.~148--149]{Kitsunai2016} it guarantees a commitment to the least-border set\n392\t   392\tbetween $S$ and $X$, which need not contain $q$.\\lean{exists_isCommittable_of_isDefinite}\n393\t   393\tIn the counterexample that set is $\\{2,3,4\\}$.\\lean{cex_isCommittable_234}\n394\t   394\tCommitting there when the matching fails is a second repair that gives up no\n395\t   395\tpruning; we have not built it.\n396\t   396\t\n397\t   397\tA commitment of depth one, $S$ to $S\\cup\\{q\\}$, holds iff $\\open(q,S)\\le1$.\\lean{isCommittable_insert_iff}\n398\t   398\tThe greedy step of \\citet[Lemma~3, p.~49]{CoudertMazauricNisse2014} is exactly\n399\t   399\tthat condition,\\lean{isGreedyStep_iff} and the push rule of\n400\t   400\t\\citet[Rule~1, p.~330]{SuchanVillanger2009} is a case of it. So every published\n401\t   401\tpathwidth rule of this kind lies in the region $\\open\\le1$, where the published\n402\t   402\tdefinite move is right; \\citet[p.~388, Table~2]{KobayashiKomuroTamaki2014} report\n403\t   403\tdepth-one commitments as ``extremely effective''. Chu and Stuckey's Theorem~1\n404\t   404\treaches into $\\open\\ge2$, and that is where it fails. For the cost of the test,\n405\t   405\t\\citet[Cor.~2]{Kitsunai2016} find the least border between the ends of a given\n406\t   406\textension by a minimum $s$--$t$ separator; Theorem~\\ref{thm:matching} is that\n407\t   407\tcomputation for the single target $X$, where it becomes a bipartite matching.\n408\t   408\t\n409\t   409\tTable~\\ref{tab:known} sums up what is known, limited to the pathwidth papers we\n410\t   410\thold \\citep{SuchanVillanger2009,Bodlaender2012,CoudertMazauricNisse2014,CoudertMazauricNisse2016,KobayashiKomuroTamaki2014,Kitsunai2016}.\n411\t   411\t``None found'' is not a claim of priority.\n412\t   412\t\n413\t   413\t\\begin{table}[t]\n414\t   414\t\\centering\\small\n415\t   415\t\\caption{Each rule of the customer search against the exact-pathwidth literature.}\n416\t   416\t\\label{tab:known}\n417\t   417\t\\begin{tabularx}{\\linewidth}{@{}>{\\raggedright}p{3.2cm}>{\\raggedright}X>{\\raggedright\\arraybackslash}p{4.1cm}@{}}\n418\t   418\t\\toprule\n419\t   419\tRule & Published counterpart & Status \\\\\n420\t   420\t\\midrule\n421\t   421\tfree move & full sets \\citep{SuchanVillanger2009}; \\citet[Prop.~3]{Kitsunai2016} & known \\\\\n422\t   422\tdefinite move, $\\open\\le1$ & depth-one commitments; greedy step; push rule & known \\\\\n423\t   423\tdefinite move, published & -- & false (Cex.~\\ref{cex:definite}) \\\\\n424\t   424\tdefinite move, repaired & commitment lemma \\citep[Lemma~1]{Kitsunai2016} & known; matching test (Thm~\\ref{thm:matching}) not found \\\\\n425\t   425\tsubset rule & none found & proved here \\\\\n426\t   426\tbetter move, published & none found & false \\\\\n427\t   427\tbetter move, repaired & none found & proved here \\\\\n428\t   428\told move & none found & proved here \\\\\n429\t   429\tmemo & subset DP \\citep{Bodlaender2012}; prefix and failure tables & known; with the old move, proved here (Thm~\\ref{thm:runs}) \\\\\n430\t   430\t\\bottomrule\n431\t   431\t\\end{tabularx}\n432\t   432\t\\end{table}\n433\t   433\t\n434\t   434\t\\paragraph{Prior reports.} We looked for any earlier report of the error. We\n435\t   435\tlisted the works citing \\citet{ChuStuckey2009}, Chu's thesis and its IJCAI 2013\n436\t   436\tabstract in OpenAlex, Semantic Scholar and OpenCitations: 141 works. Of the 38\n437\t   437\tthat cite the CP paper we read 24 in full (63\\%), and of the 103 that cite only\n438\t   438\tthe thesis, 32 (31\\%).\\footnote{\\texttt{python3 -m paper2.prior\\_art\\_sweep};\n439\t   439\tthe table of citers is in \\texttt{paper2/prior\\_art\\_counterexample.md}.} No\n440\t   440\twork read gives a counterexample, an erratum or a correction. One,\n441\t   441\t\\citet{Fink2012}, restates the definite move as true with a stronger premise,\n442\t   442\trefuted above. Three use an implementation of the rules and report no failure;\n443\t   443\tamong them \\citet{Frinhani2018} ran the original code to obtain the optima that\n444\t   444\t\\citet{MartinYanassePinto2022} reuse. We found no prior report of the error.\n445\t   445\tThe pathwidth literature has the correct form of the rule, as a commitment, and\n446\t   446\tnone of the pathwidth papers we hold mentions MOSP.\n447\t   447\t\n448\t   448\t\\subsection{The revised algorithm in practice}\n449\t   449\t\\label{sec:practice}\n450\t   450\t\n451\t   451\tThe revised search changes two tests. The definite move fires on the first\n452\t   452\tplayable $q$ that passes the published test and then the matching of\n453\t   453\tTheorem~\\ref{thm:matching}; a $q$ that fails the matching is passed over. The\n454\t   454\tbetter move cites $q$ for $r$ only if the same matching succeeds at\n455\t   455\t$\\cl(S\\cup\\{r\\})$. The matching joins at most $\\close-1$ customers to $\\open$\n456\t   456\tstacks and stops at $\\open-1$ edges. Since 1 October 2026 these are the default\n457\t   457\tin both of our solvers, the MOSP customer search and the graph pathwidth solver,\n458\t   458\tin C and in Python; the published rules remain behind a flag.\n459\t   459\t\n460\t   460\t\\begin{table}[t]\n461\t   461\t\\centering\\small\n462\t   462\t\\caption{The cost of the repair: nodes under the published and the repaired\n463\t   463\trules, in paired runs of the same decision in one worker. Totals over the pairs\n464\t   464\tthat finished under both settings; a censored pair says nothing about cost.\n465\t   465\tMOSP rows refute the optimum minus one; the graph row is whole descents at\n466\t   466\t120\\,s each.}\n467\t   467\t\\label{tab:cost}\n468\t   468\t\\resizebox{\\linewidth}{!}{\\input{tables/cost}}\n469\t   469\t\\end{table}\n470\t   470\t\n471\t   471\tTable~\\ref{tab:cost} gives the cost in nodes. The repair changes tree sizes by\n472\t   472\tat most a few percent and never changed an answer. Per node it costs about 2\\% in\n473\t   473\tthe MOSP C engine, measured on six hard instances at 75--125 customers. The\n474\t   474\tpublished test passes and the matching then fails on 0.07--0.27\\% of\n475\t   475\tdefinite-move candidates, a rate that rises with size.\n476\t   476\t\n477\t   477\t\\paragraph{Re-certification.} Of the 6,374 certified optima in our MOSP corpus,\n478\t   478\t6,259 do not depend on the customer search: they rest on an exact subset-lattice\n479\t   479\tcomputation, a checked DRAT refutation, an earlier SAT search, or a lower bound\n480\t   480\tequal to the value. The other 115 rested on the customer search under the\n481\t   481\tpublished rules. The repaired solver has re-refuted the value minus one on 112\n482\t   482\tof them, and no value changed. Table~\\ref{tab:split} gives the seven hardest,\n483\t   483\t125-customer instances at densities 2 and 4, which needed a split of the root\n484\t   484\tover many cores; three are still running at the time of writing, and keep their\n485\t   485\tvalues as verified upper bounds until they finish.\\footnote{\\texttt{python -m\n486\t   486\tpaper2.solver\\_fix\\_split -{}-summary}; the split is proved sound in\n487\t   487\t\\leanfile{Search/Split.lean}.}\n488\t   488\t\n489\t   489\t\\begin{table}[t]\n490\t   490\t\\centering\\small\n491\t   491\t\\caption{Re-certification of the hardest corpus values under the repaired rules,\n492\t   492\tby a split of the root into independent tasks. \\emph{$k$}: the value minus one,\n493\t   493\twhich must be refuted. For an instance in flight, the nodes and core-hours are\n494\t   494\tthose of the finished tasks so far.}\n495\t   495\t\\label{tab:split}\n496\t   496\t\\input{tables/split}\n497\t   497\t\\end{table}\n498\t   498\t\n499\t   499\t\\paragraph{Certificates.} A refutation by the search is a claim about a tree of\n500\t   500\t$10^{11}$ nodes at 125 customers, and nothing in Theorem~\\ref{thm:main} lets a\n501\t   501\treader check that the code ran the search it models. So the search emits a\n502\t   502\tcertificate: the tree, every pruning step with its rule and witness, and for\n503\t   503\teach definite and better step the matching of Theorem~\\ref{thm:matching}. An\n504\t   504\tindependent checker, which shares no code with the search, recomputes every\n505\t   505\tpremise from the matrix, verifies each matching (distinct customers, distinct\n506\t   506\tstacks, each stack newly opened by its customer, at least $\\open-1$ edges),\n507\t   507\trequires every chain of covers to end at a child or an old move with no cycle,\n508\t   508\tand replays the tree. Each accepted link is a Lean theorem; that an acyclic\n509\t   509\tcovering by such links is sound is a short paper argument, not yet formal.\n510\t   510\tTable~\\ref{tab:certs} gives the results on the corpus at 9--75 customers.\n511\t   511\tEvery refutation emitted within 60\\,s verifies, 6,275 of 6,286 under the\n512\t   512\t\\texttt{csearch} configuration, and none is rejected; the 35.5\\,MB of\n513\t   513\tcertificates check in 147\\,s. On the graph of Counterexample~\\ref{cex:definite}\n514\t   514\trooted at $\\{2\\}$, the published rules emit an exhaustive tree that claims no\n515\t   515\tsolution; a checker of the published premises accepts it, and ours rejects it,\n516\t   516\teven with a largest matching attached (one edge where two are needed).\n517\t   517\t\n518\t   518\t\\begin{table}[t]\n519\t   519\t\\centering\\small\n520\t   520\t\\caption{Certificates for the repaired rules on the certified corpus at 9--75\n521\t   521\tcustomers, refuting the optimum minus one, 60\\,s emission budget.\n522\t   522\t\\emph{Unknown}: emission did not finish. Below 41 customers the\n523\t   523\t\\texttt{default} rows match \\texttt{csearch} and are omitted.}\n524\t   524\t\\label{tab:certs}\n525\t   525\t\\resizebox{\\linewidth}{!}{\\input{tables/certificates}}\n526\t   526\t\\end{table}\n527\t   527\t\n528\t   528\t\\subsection{A dataset with certified pathwidth}\n529\t   529\t\\label{sec:dataset}\n530\t   530\t\n531\t   531\tSection~\\ref{sec:equiv} makes one certified pathwidth an exact answer to every\n532\t   532\tproblem in Table~1's exact core: pathwidth, vertex separation and the vertex\n533\t   533\tseparator game take the width $w$; MOSP, gate matrix layout with multiple\n534\t   534\tfolding, one-dimensional logic, interval thickness, narrowness and node search\n535\t   535\ttake $w+1$. We therefore built one dataset from every benchmark collection of\n536\t   536\tthose problems that we could obtain, and the MOSP corpus. The unit is the\n537\t   537\tisomorphism class of the graph, the MOSP graph for a matrix instance: two\n538\t   538\tinstances with isomorphic graphs have the same value under every problem, so the\n539\t   539\tdataset holds one record per class, with its members and their isomorphism maps.\n540\t   540\tEach record carries the graph, the width, its provenance (\\emph{refutation},\n541\t   541\t\\emph{bound} or \\emph{upper bound}), a witness layout of exactly that width and,\n542\t   542\tfor a bound, a minor certificate that replays.\n543\t   543\t\n544\t   544\t\\begin{table}[t]\n545\t   545\t\\centering\\small\n546\t   546\t\\caption{The dataset by collection. \\emph{Classes}: isomorphism classes within\n547\t   547\tthe collection (nauty certificates; 78 graphs above 5,000 vertices by file\n548\t   548\tcontent only). \\emph{Owned}: classes whose first member is in this collection,\n549\t   549\tso the column sums to the dataset's class count. The provenance columns count\n550\t   550\towned classes with a witness layout.}\n551\t   551\t\\label{tab:dataset}\n552\t   552\t\\resizebox{\\linewidth}{!}{\\input{tables/dataset}}\n553\t   553\t\\end{table}\n554\t   554\t\n555\t   555\tTable~\\ref{tab:dataset} gives the counts. Twenty collections, 21,754 instance\n556\t   556\tfiles, reduce to 17,714 classes: the MOSP collections\n557\t   557\t\\citep{SmithGent2005,FaggioliBentivoglio1998,ChuStuckey2009,CarvalhoSoma2015,Frinhani2018},\n558\t   558\televen VLSI gate matrix circuits \\citep{OliveiraLorena2002}, the VSPLIB sets\n559\t   559\t\\citep{Duarte2012} and the Small graphs \\citep{MartiCamposPinana2008}, the Rome\n560\t   560\tgraphs \\citep{DiBattista1997}, and treewidth collections used as pathwidth\n561\t   561\tbenchmarks \\citep{TreewidthLIB,freetdi,PACE2016,PACE2017}. Of the classes, 16,087\n562\t   562\tcarry a certified pathwidth with a witness layout and 862 a witnessed upper\n563\t   563\tbound. An independent checker, sharing no code with the solvers, re-reads all\n564\t   564\t20,987 member files with its own readers, maps each onto its record by\n565\t   565\tisomorphism, re-measures every witness and replays every bound certificate, and\n566\t   566\treports no failure on the 16,949 records.\\footnote{\\texttt{python\n567\t   567\tpaper2/dataset\\_check.py -{}-sources}; builder \\texttt{python -m paper2.dataset}.}\n568\t   568\t\n569\t   569\tAgainst published values: all 84 Small graphs agree with the pathwidths of\n570\t   570\t\\citet[Tables~4--5]{Mallach2018}, and ten of the eleven VLSI circuits are\n571\t   571\tcertified at their published best-known track counts \\citep[Table~I]{OliveiraLorena2002};\n572\t   572\tthe eleventh, W4 with 202 nets, is open at 28 against 27. One caution about MOSP\n573\t   573\tbenchmark files emerged: the same public repository ships the Carvalho and Soma\n574\t   574\tinstances as customers by patterns and the Chu and Stuckey instances beside them\n575\t   575\tas patterns by customers, and only published densities and values tell them\n576\t   576\tapart. Read the wrong way round, an instance certifies at a different value.\n577\t   577\t\n578\t   578\tWhat is left is priced. One more budget step for every open class that fits our\n579\t   579\tengine (1,024 vertices per component) costs at most 500 core-hours, and on the\n580\t   580\tRome graphs each such step has closed about 40\\% of what it attempted. The\n581\t   581\tlargest MOSP instances, 400 to 1,000 customers, and the 154 classes above 1,024\n582\t   582\tvertices are beyond an exact search. Refutation-certified records rest on the\n583\t   583\trepaired search; for those within reach of the certificate emitter, the proof\n584\t   584\tobject above is the route by which a third party can check them without our code.\n585\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"1323e98e-623f-4534-a362-41a379cbd2ab","uuid":"1ed6b470-c9e4-4936-aa0f-06fd3d71d842","timestamp":"2026-10-06T13:49:01.338Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/al/.claude/projects/-home-al-dev-MOSP/1323e98e-623f-4534-a362-41a379cbd2ab/tool-results/bqu7li2ck.txt","content":"     1\t% Section 4. Sources: paper2/revised_algorithm.md (4.1-4.7), search_soundness.md,\n     2\t% solver_fix.md (cost, re-certification), prior_art_counterexample.md (prior\n     3\t% art), certificates.md (proof object), dataset.md (the dataset). Lean:\n     4\t% lean/MOSPFormalization/Search/. Tables from tables/ (make_tables.py).\n     5\t% Page numbers for Chu & Stuckey (2009) are those of the preprint\n     6\t% literature/chu_stuckey_2009.pdf; for Chu (2011) the printed ones.\n     7\t\\section{An exact open-stacks search, read as a pathwidth solver}\n     8\t\\label{sec:search}\n     9\t\n    10\tThe best exact method for MOSP on the standard benchmarks is the \\emph{customer\n    11\tsearch} of \\citet{ChuStuckey2009}. It searches over the orders in which the\n    12\tcustomers' stacks close, not over product sequences, and prunes with dominance\n    13\trules, three of them stated as theorems (their Theorems~1, 2 and~3) and a fourth,\n    14\tthe subset rule, in their \\S2. \\citet{ChuStuckey2009} place MOSP among ``graph\n    15\tpath-width and gate matrix layout'', citing \\citet{LY2002}, but do not use\n    16\tpathwidth in the search. By Theorem~\\ref{thm:mosp} it is nevertheless a\n    17\tpathwidth algorithm: any graph is the MOSP graph of the instance with one\n    18\tcustomer per vertex and one product per edge, whose optimum is pathwidth plus\n    19\tone.\n    20\t\n    21\tThis section reads the search that way, and finds three things. Two of the\n    22\tpublished theorems are false, and so are their restatements in Chu's thesis\n    23\t\\citep{Chu2011} and, with a stronger premise, in \\citet{Fink2012}. The repaired\n    24\tfirst rule is an instance of a known pathwidth theorem, Tamaki's commitment\n    25\tlemma; the published rule is that lemma with its interior condition dropped. And\n    26\tthe whole repaired search is proved sound in Lean. All Lean names in this\n    27\tsection are in \\leanfile{lean/MOSPFormalization/Search/}, which has no\n    28\t\\texttt{sorry} and the standard axioms only.\n    29\t\n    30\t\\subsection{The search decides pathwidth}\n    31\t\\label{sec:search-def}\n    32\t\n    33\t\\citet[p.~4]{ChuStuckey2009} observe that the products matter only through the\n    34\tMOSP graph. So fix a graph $G=(C,E)$ on the customers and an integer $k\\ge0$.\n    35\tFor a customer $c$ and a set $T\\subseteq C$ of closed customers, write\n    36\t$N[c]$ for the closed neighbourhood of $c$, and\n    37\t\\begin{align*}\n    38\tO(T) &= \\textstyle\\bigcup_{c\\in T}N[c] && \\text{the stacks opened once $T$ has closed},\\\\\n    39\t\\fin(X) &= \\{c : N[c]\\subseteq X\\} && \\text{the customers free to close when $X$ is open},\\\\\n    40\t\\cl(T) &= \\fin(O(T)) && \\text{the \\emph{free closure} of $T$},\\\\\n    41\tb(T) &= |O(T)\\setminus T| && \\text{the stacks open after $T$ has closed},\\\\\n    42\to(c,T) &= N[c]\\setminus O(T),\\quad \\open(c,T)=|o(c,T)| && \\text{the stacks closing $c$ would newly open.}\n    43\t\\end{align*}\n    44\tA \\emph{move} at $T$ closes one customer $c\\notin T$, at cost\n    45\t$\\cost(T,c)=|O(T\\cup\\{c\\})\\setminus T| = b(T\\cup\\{c\\})+1$, the stacks open at\n    46\tthat moment counting $c$; it is \\emph{playable} when $\\cost(T,c)\\le k$. Write\n    47\t$P_k(T)$ when some order of $C\\setminus T$ has every step playable. On entering\n    48\ta node the search closes every free customer, so it stands only at\n    49\t\\emph{states}, sets with $S=\\cl(S)$, and the \\emph{child} of $S$ by $c$ is\n    50\t$S\\cdot c=\\cl(S\\cup\\{c\\})$. The search's predicate $\\Sol_k(S)$ holds when $S=C$,\n    51\tor when some playable $c$ has $\\Sol_k(S\\cdot c)$.\\lean{stepCost, Solvable, SearchSol}\n    52\tEvery node the search visits satisfies the invariant $b(S)\\le k$.\n    53\t\n    54\t\\begin{lemma}[free moves never hurt]\\label{lem:F}\n    55\tIf $b(T)\\le k$, then $P_k(T)\\iff\\Sol_k(\\cl(T))$.\\lean{solvable_iff_searchSol_cl}\n    56\t\\end{lemma}\n    57\tClosing a customer whose neighbourhood is open opens nothing, never makes a\n    58\tlater step dearer, and leaves one fewer stack open. The hypothesis is needed:\n    59\tclosing the free customers one by one costs $b(T)$ at each step.\n    60\t\n    61\t\\begin{theorem}[the search decides pathwidth]\\label{thm:decides}\n    62\tFor nonempty $C$, $\\Sol_k(\\emptyset)\\iff\\pw(G)+1\\le k$; on the MOSP graph of an\n    63\tinstance $M$ with a requirement, $\\Sol_k(\\emptyset)\\iff Z(M)\\le k$.\\lean{searchSol_empty_iff_pathwidth_add_one_le, searchSol_mospGraph_iff_mospValue_le}\n    64\t\\end{theorem}\n    65\t\\begin{proof}\n    66\tFor a closing order $c_1,\\dots,c_n$ with prefixes $S_i$, the $i$-th step costs\n    67\t$1+|\\partial S_i|$, where $\\partial X$ is the set of vertices outside $X$ with a\n    68\tneighbour in $X$. Read backwards as a layout, the cut separating $C\\setminus S_i$\n    69\tfrom $S_i$ counts exactly $\\partial S_i$. So the cost of the order is the vertex\n    70\tseparation of the reversed layout plus one, and Lemma~\\ref{lem:F} at the root and\n    71\tTheorem~\\ref{thm:vs} finish the proof. The Lean statement goes through\n    72\tnarrowness \\citep{LY2002ref11}, which counts the forward order directly.\n    73\t\\end{proof}\n    74\t\n    75\tSo a refutation, $\\neg\\Sol_k(\\emptyset)$, may only mean $\\pw(G)>k-1$. The rest\n    76\tof the section asks whether the pruning rules preserve that meaning.\n    77\t\n    78\t\\paragraph{What a rule must satisfy.} At a state $S$ the search has a set $Q$ of\n    79\t\\emph{old moves} (Section~\\ref{sec:other-rules}). Its candidates are the\n    80\tcustomers outside $S\\cup Q$; the \\emph{cost cut} keeps the playable ones, $P$,\n    81\tin index order, and a \\emph{filter} keeps a sublist $L\\subseteq P$. The filter\n    82\tis \\emph{node-sound} at $(S,Q)$ if, whenever every $q\\in Q$ has\n    83\t$\\neg\\Sol_k(S\\cdot q)$, $\\Sol_k(S)$ implies $\\Sol_k(S\\cdot c)$ for some $c\\in L$.\\lean{NodeSoundAt}\n    84\tEach rule justifies a discarded move $r$ by a \\emph{covering}, a move $c$ with\n    85\t$\\Sol_k(S\\cdot r)\\Rightarrow\\Sol_k(S\\cdot c)$. A node is sound when every link a\n    86\trule cites is a true covering and every chain of links from a discarded\n    87\tcandidate ends in $L$ or in $Q$; the second condition can fail through a cycle\n    88\teven when every link is true. The filter runs the definite move, then the subset\n    89\trule, then the better move, each once.\n    90\t\n    91\t\\subsection{The definite move is false as published}\n    92\t\\label{sec:definite}\n    93\t\n    94\t\\citet[\\S3.1, p.~6]{ChuStuckey2009} state:\n    95\t\\begin{quote}\n    96\t\\textbf{Theorem 1.} Suppose S ++ [q] is playable and close(q, S) $\\ge$\n    97\topen(q, S), then if U$'$ = S ++ R is a solution, there exists a solution\n    98\tU = S ++ [q] ++ R$'$,\n    99\t\\end{quote}\n   100\twhere $\\close(q,S)$ counts the customers $d$ with $o(d,S)\\subseteq o(q,S)$\n   101\t(p.~4). The rule keeps $q$ alone. We read $d$ as ranging over customers not yet\n   102\tclosed, as the code does; read literally, closed customers count too, which\n   103\tweakens the premise, and both readings are refuted below. \\citet[Thm~6.3.6,\n   104\tp.~142]{Chu2011} restates the theorem with the same premise and proof, reworded\n   105\tfor $k$ stacks. Write $X=S\\cdot q$.\n   106\t\n   107\t\\begin{lemma}[what the premise says]\\label{lem:premise}\n   108\t$\\close(q,S)=|X\\setminus S|$. Hence the premise holds iff $b(X)\\le b(S)$: the\n   109\tchild has no more open stacks than the parent.\\lean{closeCount_eq, isDefinite_iff}\n   110\t\\end{lemma}\n   111\t\n   112\t\\begin{figure}[t]\n   113\t\\centering\n   114\t\\includegraphics[width=0.85\\linewidth]{definite_counterexample.pdf}\n   115\t\\caption{The graph of Counterexample~\\ref{cex:definite} at the state where the\n   116\tdefinite move fails. Customer 2 is closed; 1, 3 and 4 are open. Moving 0 opens\n   117\tthe new stacks 0, 7 and 11 and closes 0, 3 and 4, so open = close = 3 and the\n   118\trule keeps 0 alone. But 3 and 4 have the single new stack 0 between them:\n   119\tclosing them first gains two stacks for the price of one, which a solution\n   120\tstarting with 0 cannot match. Regenerate with \\texttt{python -m\n   121\tpaper2.counterexample\\_figure}, which recomputes every labelled fact.}\n   122\t\\label{fig:cex}\n   123\t\\end{figure}\n   124\t\n   125\t\\begin{counterexample}\\label{cex:definite}\n   126\tLet $G$ be the graph on customers $0,\\dots,13$ with the 26 edges\n   127\t\\begin{gather*}\n   128\t0\\text{--}3,\\ 0\\text{--}4,\\ 0\\text{--}7,\\ 0\\text{--}11,\\ 1\\text{--}2,\\ 1\\text{--}5,\\ 1\\text{--}6,\\ 2\\text{--}3,\\ 2\\text{--}4,\\ 5\\text{--}9,\\ 5\\text{--}11,\\ 5\\text{--}12,\\ 6\\text{--}8,\\\\\n   129\t6\\text{--}13,\\ 7\\text{--}9,\\ 7\\text{--}10,\\ 7\\text{--}13,\\ 8\\text{--}9,\\ 8\\text{--}10,\\ 8\\text{--}11,\\ 9\\text{--}10,\\ 9\\text{--}11,\\ 10\\text{--}11,\\ 10\\text{--}12,\\ 10\\text{--}13,\\ 12\\text{--}13,\n   130\t\\end{gather*}\n   131\twith $S=\\{2\\}$, $k=6$ and $q=0$ (Figure~\\ref{fig:cex}). Then $O(S)=\\{1,2,3,4\\}$,\n   132\t$o(0,S)=\\{0,7,11\\}$ and $\\cost(S,0)=6$, so $0$ is playable; the customers with\n   133\t$o(d,S)\\subseteq\\{0,7,11\\}$ are $0$, $3$ and $4$, so $\\close(0,S)=3\\ge\\open(0,S)$;\n   134\tand $0$ comes first in index order, so the rule keeps only $0$. A solution\n   135\texists from $S$: the order\n   136\t\\[1,3,4,6,12,13,0,5,7,8,9,10,11\\]\n   137\tcosts at most 6. None exists\n   138\tfrom the child $X=\\{0,2,3,4\\}$: only seven sets are reachable from $X$ by moves\n   139\tof cost at most 6, and none is $C$.\\lean{definiteMove_counterexample, cexFamily_closed}\n   140\t\\end{counterexample}\n   141\t\n   142\tThe published implication, as a universal statement, is refuted in Lean under\n   143\tboth readings of $\\close$, and so is the thesis's Theorem~6.3.6.\\lean{chuStuckey_theorem1_false, chuStuckey_theorem1_false_literal, chuThesis_theorem636_false}\n   144\tThe smallest graph on which the rule loses the last solution has 14 customers:\n   145\tthere is none on any graph to 9 vertices, by exhaustion, and none on the 88,802\n   146\tconnected graphs on 10 vertices with at most 14 edges. The counterexample was\n   147\tfound by a search over a family of gadgets built from the failed proof, and\n   148\tminimised by deletion. \\citet[pp.~27--28]{Fink2012} restates the rule with a\n   149\tstronger premise, counting only dominated customers of smaller index than $q$.\n   150\tThe graph above refutes it under no labelling, but a third twin of 3 and 4 gives\n   151\ta 15-customer graph that does.\\lean{fink_theorem1_false}\n   152\t\n   153\t\\paragraph{Why the published proof fails.} The proof moves $q$ to the front of a\n   154\tsolution and claims that after each prefix ``we have at most open(q, S) extra\n   155\tstacks open, but at least close(q, S) extra stacks closed'' (p.~6). At a prefix\n   156\t$T\\supseteq S$ that does not yet contain $q$, moving $q$ forward changes the open\n   157\tstacks by $b(T\\cup X)-b(T) = |N[q]\\setminus O(T)|-|X\\setminus T|$. At $T=S$ this\n   158\tis $\\open-\\close\\le0$. But customers of $X$ that the solution has \\emph{already}\n   159\tclosed are in $T$ and no longer count as extra stacks closed, while the stacks\n   160\t$q$ opens may still be new. In the example, a solution may begin with 3, after\n   161\twhich 4 is free: at $T=\\{2,3,4\\}$, moving 0 forward costs one more stack. A\n   162\tsecond, independent slip: even where the conclusion holds, the literal sequence\n   163\t$S \\mathbin{+\\!\\!+} [q] \\mathbin{+\\!\\!+} R'$ need not be a solution, because a\n   164\tcustomer that $q$ frees stays open until its turn; the argument needs the free\n   165\tmoves.\n   166\t\n   167\t\\paragraph{The repair.} Call $q$ \\emph{hereditarily definite} at $S$ when\n   168\t$b(X)\\le b(B)$ for every $B$ with $S\\subseteq B\\subseteq X$ and $q\\notin B$. By\n   169\tLemma~\\ref{lem:premise}, the published premise is the case $B=S$. In the\n   170\tcounterexample the repair fails at $B=\\{2,3,4\\}$, where $b(B)=2<3=b(X)$.\\lean{IsHereditarilyDefinite, not_isHereditarilyDefinite_cex}\n   171\t\n   172\t\\begin{lemma}[open stacks are submodular]\\label{lem:submod}\n   173\tFor all $A,B\\subseteq C$, $b(A\\cup B)+b(A\\cap B)\\le b(A)+b(B)$.\\lean{openStacks_submodular}\n   174\t\\end{lemma}\n   175\t\\begin{proof}\n   176\t$b(T)=|O(T)|-|T|$, $O(A\\cup B)=O(A)\\cup O(B)$ and $O(A\\cap B)\\subseteq\n   177\tO(A)\\cap O(B)$; the customers themselves balance exactly.\n   178\t\\end{proof}\n   179\t\n   180\t\\begin{theorem}[the repaired definite move is sound]\\label{thm:repaired}\n   181\tIf $q\\notin S$ is hereditarily definite at $S$, then $P_k(S)\\Rightarrow P_k(X)$.\n   182\tAt a state with the invariant, $\\Sol_k(S)\\Rightarrow\\Sol_k(S\\cdot q)$.\\lean{solvable_cl_insert_of_hereditarilyDefinite, searchSol_cl_insert_of_hereditarilyDefinite}\n   183\t\\end{theorem}\n   184\t\\begin{proof}\n   185\tTake an order from $S$ of cost at most $k$ with prefixes $T_i$, in which $q$ is\n   186\tthe $j$-th step, and follow it from $X$, skipping customers already in $X$. A\n   187\tstep $i<j$ closes $c_i$ at $T_{i-1}\\cup X$. With $Z=T_i$ we have\n   188\t$S\\subseteq Z\\cap X\\subseteq X$ and $q\\notin Z$, so the hypothesis gives\n   189\t$b(X)\\le b(Z\\cap X)$, and Lemma~\\ref{lem:submod} gives $b(Z\\cup X)\\le b(Z)$: the\n   190\tstep costs no more than the original. After step $j-1$ the closed set contains\n   191\t$T_j$ with the same opened set, and the rest of the order applies. The search's\n   192\tform follows by Lemma~\\ref{lem:F}.\n   193\t\\end{proof}\n   194\t\n   195\tThis theorem is not new. The hereditary premise is the commitment condition of\n   196\tTamaki, and the proof is the proof of the commitment lemma,\n   197\t\\citet[Lemma~1, p.~142]{Kitsunai2016}; Section~\\ref{sec:layouts} makes the\n   198\tcorrespondence exact. What we did not find in the papers we hold is the\n   199\tfollowing cheap form of the test.\n   200\t\n   201\t\\begin{theorem}[the repair is a matching condition]\\label{thm:matching}\n   202\tLet $Y=X\\setminus(S\\cup\\{q\\})$, and join each $d\\in Y$ to the stacks of\n   203\t$o(d,S)\\subseteq o(q,S)$. Then $q$ is hereditarily definite at $S$ iff this\n   204\tbipartite graph has a matching with at least $\\open(q,S)-1$ edges.\\lean{isHereditarilyDefinite_iff_hasDefiniteMatching}\n   205\t\\end{theorem}\n   206\t\\begin{proof}\n   207\tThe sets of the repair are $B=S\\cup D$, $D\\subseteq Y$, and\n   208\t$b(X)\\le b(S\\cup D)$ iff $|D|-|o(D)|\\le\\delta$ with\n   209\t$\\delta=|Y|+1-\\open(q,S)$, where $o(D)$ is the union of the $o(d,S)$. By the\n   210\tdeficiency form of Hall's theorem the largest matching has size\n   211\t$|Y|-\\max_D(|D|-|o(D)|)$, which is at least $|Y|-\\delta=\\open(q,S)-1$ exactly\n   212\twhen the repair holds. The Lean proof reduces the deficiency form to Mathlib's\n   213\tHall theorem with $\\delta$ dummy stacks.\n   214\t\\end{proof}\n   215\t\n   216\tIn words: the rule is safe exactly when all but one of the new stacks can be\n   217\tpaired with a different freed customer who needed it. In the counterexample,\n   218\tclosing 0 opens three new stacks and frees 3 and 4, both of whom needed only\n   219\tstack 0: one pairing where two are required. Every $q$ with $\\open(q,S)\\le1$\n   220\tpasses at once, so the published rule is right there.\\lean{isHereditarilyDefinite_of_openCount_le_one}\n   221\t\n   222\t\\subsection{The other rules}\n   223\t\\label{sec:other-rules}\n   224\t\n   225\t\\paragraph{The subset rule.} ``If $o(c_i,S)\\subseteq o(c_j,S)$ and $i<j$ \\dots\\\n   226\tmove $j$ can be removed'' \\citep[\\S2, p.~5]{ChuStuckey2009}. As coded, $d$\n   227\tdominates $r$ when $o(d,S)\\subseteq o(r,S)$ and the inclusion is strict or $d<r$;\n   228\tevery dominated playable $r$ is dropped, with a fallback that keeps $P$ if all\n   229\twould go. The covering is sound for every set $S$: if $o(d,S)\\subseteq o(r,S)$\n   230\tand $r$ is playable, then $d$ is playable and $\\Sol_k(S\\cdot r)\\Rightarrow\n   231\t\\Sol_k(S\\cdot d)$, because $d$ is free after $r$ and closing $d$ then $r$ reaches\n   232\tthe same state at no greater cost. With the index tie-break, domination is a\n   233\tstrict order, so an undominated move with a solution survives, and the fallback\n   234\tnever fires when a solution exists.\\lean{searchSol_cl_insert_of_newlyOpened_subset, subsetFilter_sound}\n   235\tThe tie-break is necessary: without it, two customers with equal new stacks\n   236\tdominate each other and both go, which loses a solution on a 7-customer\n   237\tgraph.\\lean{noTieBreak_counterexample}\n   238\t\n   239\t\\paragraph{The better move.} \\citet[\\S3.2, p.~6]{ChuStuckey2009}: ``Suppose\n   240\tS ++ [q] and S ++ [r, q] are playable and close(q, S $\\cup$ \\{r\\}) $\\ge$\n   241\topen(q, S $\\cup$ \\{r\\}) then if U$'$ = S ++ [r] ++ R is a solution there exists a\n   242\tsolution U = S ++ [q] ++ R$'$.'' Its premise is Theorem~1's premise for $q$ at the\n   243\tchild $\\cl(S\\cup\\{r\\})$, together with playability of $r$ then $q$, and its\n   244\tconclusion swaps $q$ and $r$.\\lean{isBetter_iff} The swap is sound; the appeal to\n   245\tTheorem~1 is not. On the graph of Counterexample~\\ref{cex:definite} at the root\n   246\twith $k=6$, the definite move does not fire, customers 0 and 2 both survive the\n   247\tsubset rule, and the premise holds for $r=2$, $q=0$, so the rule drops 2 citing\n   248\t0. But $\\{2\\}$ has a solution and $\\{0\\}$ has none.\\lean{betterMove_counterexample, chuStuckey_theorem2_false}\n   249\tAt that node another kept customer still has a solution, so this is a false link\n   250\tand not a lost node; no node where the better move loses the last solution has\n   251\tbeen found. \\citet[Thm~6.3.8, p.~143]{Chu2011} states the better move with a\n   252\tdifferent premise, $\\close(q,S)\\ge\\open(q,S\\cup\\{r\\})$, and proves it through\n   253\tTheorem~6.3.6. The same graph refutes it at $S=\\{2\\}$, $r=3$, $q=0$.\\lean{chuThesis_theorem638_false, chuThesis_theorem638_false_literal}\n   254\tThe repair cites $q$ for $r$ only if $q$ is hereditarily definite at\n   255\t$\\cl(S\\cup\\{r\\})$, one matching at the child; then the swap argument goes\n   256\tthrough.\\lean{IsRepairedBetter, searchSol_cl_insert_of_repairedBetter}\n   257\t\n   258\tTwo earlier faults of this rule in our own implementation are now proved in\n   259\tLean too: a close count that included the customers $r$ finishes on its own,\n   260\tand an order of the rules in which the subset rule could cite a customer the\n   261\tbetter move had already dropped, which loses a node through a cycle of true\n   262\tlinks.\\lean{bugA_counterexample, bugB_counterexample}\n   263\tThe second is an instance of the general observation of\n   264\t\\citet[Prop.~3, Example~6]{Beck2025} that mutually dominating transitions must\n   265\tbe tie-broken.\n   266\t\n   267\t\\paragraph{The old move.} \\citet[Thm~3, p.~7]{ChuStuckey2009} let a child inherit\n   268\ta sibling $q$ whose subtree failed, provided that inserting $q$ before each\n   269\tlater step keeps that step playable. The rule is sound as coded: if\n   270\t$\\cost(S\\cup\\{q\\},c)\\le k$, then\n   271\t$\\Sol_k(\\cl(\\{q\\}\\cup S\\cdot c))\\Rightarrow\\Sol_k(S\\cdot q)$, so a refuted\n   272\tsibling stays refuted along any path whose steps pass the test.\\lean{searchSol_reinsert, not_searchSol_of_oldMove}\n   273\tThe test is necessary; the smallest graph that shows it is the path on five\n   274\tvertices.\\lean{reinsert_needs_test}\n   275\t\n   276\t\\paragraph{The memo.} A refuted state is recorded and refused when met again.\n   277\tWhether a state with $b(S)\\le k$ has a solution depends on the state alone, not\n   278\ton the path that reached it.\\lean{solvable_iff_of_cl_eq} One implementation of\n   279\tours declined to run the memo beside the old move, on the ground that a\n   280\trefutation obtained with inherited old moves depends on the ancestors. A node\n   281\ttaken on its own, with an old move that is not in fact refuted, can indeed\n   282\trecord a solvable state;\\lean{exec_fake_oldMove} the next theorem shows that this\n   283\tnever happens in a real run.\n   284\t\n   285\t\\subsection{The repaired search is sound}\n   286\t\\label{sec:theorem}\n   287\t\n   288\t\\begin{proposition}\\label{prop:filter}\n   289\tAt every state with the invariant, for every set $Q$ of refuted old moves, the\n   290\tfilter definite $\\to$ subset $\\to$ better with both repairs is node-sound.\\lean{repairedFullFilter_sound}\n   291\t\\end{proposition}\n   292\t\\begin{proof}\n   293\tIf the definite move fires, Theorem~\\ref{thm:repaired} applies. Otherwise the\n   294\tsubset rule keeps a survivor with a solution; let $w$ be the earliest. If the\n   295\tbetter move dropped $w$, it cited an earlier survivor that the repaired\n   296\targument proves solvable, contradicting the choice of $w$.\n   297\t\\end{proof}\n   298\t\n   299\t\\begin{theorem}[runs are sound]\\label{thm:runs}\n   300\tLet the filter be node-sound and return only playable customers. If at the start\n   301\tof a run the invariant holds, every memo entry is a genuine refutation, and\n   302\tevery old move is refuted, then the run leaves only genuine memo entries, and if\n   303\tit answers \\emph{false} at $S$ then $\\neg\\Sol_k(S)$. This holds for any child\n   304\torder and any memo policy, with the old move on or off.\\lean{Exec.sound}\n   305\t\\end{theorem}\n   306\tThe proof is an induction over the run, in the order in which subtrees complete:\n   307\tevery old move a node holds is a sibling subtree that completed with \\emph{false}\n   308\tearlier in the same run, or an inherited one that passed the reinsertion test.\n   309\t\n   310\t\\begin{theorem}[the revised search is sound]\\label{thm:main}\n   311\tRun the customer search with free moves, the memo, old moves and the filter\n   312\tdefinite $\\to$ subset $\\to$ better, the definite move firing only on a\n   313\thereditarily definite customer and the better move citing only under the\n   314\trepaired premise. If it refutes $k$ from the root, then no closing order costs at\n   315\tmost $k$, $\\pw(G)>k-1$, and on the MOSP graph of an instance $M$ with a\n   316\trequirement, $Z(M)>k$.\\lean{exec_repairedFullFilter_sound, exec_repairedFullFilter_pathwidth, exec_repairedFullFilter_mospValue}\n   317\t\\end{theorem}\n   318\t\n   319\tWhat the Lean model covers is the search as specified; that our C and Python\n   320\timplement it is checked, not proved. A transcription of the Lean filter agreed\n   321\twith the production filter on 16,244,090 node checks over graphs on 1 to 17\n   322\tvertices; the matching agreed with the hereditary premise on 36,939,226\n   323\tcandidates; and a search written from the theorems agreed with the C, the Python\n   324\tand an exact oracle on 17,431,232 runs over 52,592 graphs on 1 to 17\n   325\tvertices.\\footnote{\\texttt{paper2/solver\\_fix.md}, items 01--05, and\n   326\t\\texttt{paper2/revised\\_algorithm.md}, section~4.4.4, with the regenerate\n   327\tcommands.} Under the published rules the filter is not\n   328\tnode-sound,\\lean{not_codeFilterSound_cexGraph} and in a check of 570,206 runs with\n   329\tmemo and old move it lost the last solution at a node in 58 runs, all on graphs\n   330\tbuilt around Counterexample~\\ref{cex:definite} and all at $k$ equal to the\n   331\toptimum. Every one found a solution through another branch and answered\n   332\tcorrectly. No whole instance on which the published rules answer \\emph{unsat}\n   333\twrongly is known.\n   334\t\n   335\t\\subsection{The search in pathwidth language}\n   336\t\\label{sec:layouts}\n   337\t\n   338\tFor a set $T$ of vertices write $N(T)$ for its \\emph{border}, the vertices\n   339\toutside $T$ with a neighbour inside, and $d(T)=|N(T)|$, in the notation of\n   340\t\\citet[p.~390]{KobayashiKomuroTamaki2014}. A vertex sequence is $w$-feasible when\n   341\tevery prefix has $d\\le w$, and $\\vs(G)$ is the least $w$ for which some\n   342\tpermutation is $w$-feasible. Table~\\ref{tab:dictionary} translates.\n   343\t\n   344\t\\begin{table}[t]\n   345\t\\centering\\small\n   346\t\\caption{The customer search in the vocabulary of exact pathwidth algorithms.}\n   347\t\\label{tab:dictionary}\n   348\t\\begin{tabularx}{\\linewidth}{@{}>{\\raggedright}p{3.6cm}>{\\raggedright}X>{\\raggedright\\arraybackslash}p{5.2cm}@{}}\n   349\t\\toprule\n   350\tCustomer search & Layout & Lean \\\\\n   351\t\\midrule\n   352\tclosing order from $\\emptyset$ & vertex sequence, read forwards & \\path{orderOfLayout} \\\\\n   353\topen stacks $b(T)$ & border size $d(T)$ & \\path{openStacks_eq_card_boundary} \\\\\n   354\t$\\cost(T,c)$ & $d(T\\cup\\{c\\})+1$ & \\path{stepCost_eq_card_boundary_add_one} \\\\\n   355\t$\\Sol_k(\\emptyset)$ & $\\vs(G)\\le k-1$ & \\path{searchSol_empty_iff_pathwidth_add_one_le} \\\\\n   356\tfree closure $\\cl(T)$ & full set of $T$ \\citep[p.~328]{SuchanVillanger2009} & \\path{mem_cl_iff} \\\\\n   357\tmemo & failure table & -- \\\\\n   358\tdefinite move, repaired & commitment to the child & \\path{isHereditarilyDefinite_iff_isCommittable} \\\\\n   359\t\\bottomrule\n   360\t\\end{tabularx}\n   361\t\\end{table}\n   362\t\n   363\t\\citet[Algorithm~1, p.~393]{KobayashiKomuroTamaki2014} decide whether a vertex\n   364\tset extends to a $w$-feasible permutation: replace the set by a committed\n   365\textension, answer from a failure table or at $V$, recurse on each feasible\n   366\tone-vertex extension, record failures. The customer search is this loop with\n   367\t$w=k-1$: the free closure and the definite move play the part of the commitment,\n   368\tthe memo is the failure table, and the cost cut is the feasibility test.\n   369\t\\citet[\\S3.2]{CoudertMazauricNisse2014} has the same three parts in a branch and\n   370\tbound. Neither line cites the other in the papers we hold: \\citet{ChuStuckey2009}\n   371\tpredates the commitments and both 2014 papers, and none of the pathwidth papers\n   372\tcites it.\n   373\t\n   374\t\\paragraph{Commitments.} \\citet[p.~142]{Kitsunai2016}, stating Tamaki's notion,\n   375\tcall an extension $\\tau$ of $\\sigma$ \\emph{committable} when $d(X')\\ge d(V(\\tau))$\n   376\tfor every $X'$ with $V(\\sigma)\\subseteq X'\\subseteq V(\\tau)$; their Lemma~1 says\n   377\tthat if $\\sigma$ extends to a feasible permutation, so does $\\tau$.\n   378\t\n   379\t\\begin{lemma}\\label{lem:endpoint}\n   380\tFor $q\\notin S$ and $X=\\cl(S\\cup\\{q\\})$: the published premise holds iff\n   381\t$d(X)\\le d(S)$, the commitment condition at the single set $X'=S$; and the\n   382\trepaired premise holds iff $X$ is a commitment from $S$, the condition at every\n   383\t$X'$ between them.\\lean{isDefinite_iff_endpoint, isHereditarilyDefinite_iff_isCommittable}\n   384\t\\end{lemma}\n   385\t\n   386\tSo Theorem~\\ref{thm:repaired} is the commitment lemma at the child, and the\n   387\tpublished Theorem~1 is a commitment whose interior condition was dropped. In\n   388\tCounterexample~\\ref{cex:definite} the endpoint test passes with $d(X)=3=d(S)$,\n   389\twhile the interior set $\\{2,3,4\\}$ has border 2.\\lean{cex_isDefinite_not_isCommittable}\n   390\tThe published premise is not wasted: by \\citet[Lemma~10, Cor.~2,\n   391\tpp.~148--149]{Kitsunai2016} it guarantees a commitment to the least-border set\n   392\tbetween $S$ and $X$, which need not contain $q$.\\lean{exists_isCommittable_of_isDefinite}\n   393\tIn the counterexample that set is $\\{2,3,4\\}$.\\lean{cex_isCommittable_234}\n   394\tCommitting there when the matching fails is a second repair that gives up no\n   395\tpruning; we have not built it.\n   396\t\n   397\tA commitment of depth one, $S$ to $S\\cup\\{q\\}$, holds iff $\\open(q,S)\\le1$.\\lean{isCommittable_insert_iff}\n   398\tThe greedy step of \\citet[Lemma~3, p.~49]{CoudertMazauricNisse2014} is exactly\n   399\tthat condition,\\lean{isGreedyStep_iff} and the push rule of\n   400\t\\citet[Rule~1, p.~330]{SuchanVillanger2009} is a case of it. So every published\n   401\tpathwidth rule of this kind lies in the region $\\open\\le1$, where the published\n   402\tdefinite move is right; \\citet[p.~388, Table~2]{KobayashiKomuroTamaki2014} report\n   403\tdepth-one commitments as ``extremely effective''. Chu and Stuckey's Theorem~1\n   404\treaches into $\\open\\ge2$, and that is where it fails. For the cost of the test,\n   405\t\\citet[Cor.~2]{Kitsunai2016} find the least border between the ends of a given\n   406\textension by a minimum $s$--$t$ separator; Theorem~\\ref{thm:matching} is that\n   407\tcomputation for the single target $X$, where it becomes a bipartite matching.\n   408\t\n   409\tTable~\\ref{tab:known} sums up what is known, limited to the pathwidth papers we\n   410\thold \\citep{SuchanVillanger2009,Bodlaender2012,CoudertMazauricNisse2014,CoudertMazauricNisse2016,KobayashiKomuroTamaki2014,Kitsunai2016}.\n   411\t``None found'' is not a claim of priority.\n   412\t\n   413\t\\begin{table}[t]\n   414\t\\centering\\small\n   415\t\\caption{Each rule of the customer search against the exact-pathwidth literature.}\n   416\t\\label{tab:known}\n   417\t\\begin{tabularx}{\\linewidth}{@{}>{\\raggedright}p{3.2cm}>{\\raggedright}X>{\\raggedright\\arraybackslash}p{4.1cm}@{}}\n   418\t\\toprule\n   419\tRule & Published counterpart & Status \\\\\n   420\t\\midrule\n   421\tfree move & full sets \\citep{SuchanVillanger2009}; \\citet[Prop.~3]{Kitsunai2016} & known \\\\\n   422\tdefinite move, $\\open\\le1$ & depth-one commitments; greedy step; push rule & known \\\\\n   423\tdefinite move, published & -- & false (Cex.~\\ref{cex:definite}) \\\\\n   424\tdefinite move, repaired & commitment lemma \\citep[Lemma~1]{Kitsunai2016} & known; matching test (Thm~\\ref{thm:matching}) not found \\\\\n   425\tsubset rule & none found & proved here \\\\\n   426\tbetter move, published & none found & false \\\\\n   427\tbetter move, repaired & none found & proved here \\\\\n   428\told move & none found & proved here \\\\\n   429\tmemo & subset DP \\citep{Bodlaender2012}; prefix and failure tables & known; with the old move, proved here (Thm~\\ref{thm:runs}) \\\\\n   430\t\\bottomrule\n   431\t\\end{tabularx}\n   432\t\\end{table}\n   433\t\n   434\t\\paragraph{Prior reports.} We looked for any earlier report of the error. We\n   435\tlisted the works citing \\citet{ChuStuckey2009}, Chu's thesis and its IJCAI 2013\n   436\tabstract in OpenAlex, Semantic Scholar and OpenCitations: 141 works. Of the 38\n   437\tthat cite the CP paper we read 24 in full (63\\%), and of the 103 that cite only\n   438\tthe thesis, 32 (31\\%).\\footnote{\\texttt{python3 -m paper2.prior\\_art\\_sweep};\n   439\tthe table of citers is in \\texttt{paper2/prior\\_art\\_counterexample.md}.} No\n   440\twork read gives a counterexample, an erratum or a correction. One,\n   441\t\\citet{Fink2012}, restates the definite move as true with a stronger premise,\n   442\trefuted above. Three use an implementation of the rules and report no failure;\n   443\tamong them \\citet{Frinhani2018} ran the original code to obtain the optima that\n   444\t\\citet{MartinYanassePinto2022} reuse. We found no prior report of the error.\n   445\tThe pathwidth literature has the correct form of the rule, as a commitment, and\n   446\tnone of the pathwidth papers we hold mentions MOSP.\n   447\t\n   448\t\\subsection{The revised algorithm in practice}\n   449\t\\label{sec:practice}\n   450\t\n   451\tThe revised search changes two tests. The definite move fires on the first\n   452\tplayable $q$ that passes the published test and then the matching of\n   453\tTheorem~\\ref{thm:matching}; a $q$ that fails the matching is passed over. The\n   454\tbetter move cites $q$ for $r$ only if the same matching succeeds at\n   455\t$\\cl(S\\cup\\{r\\})$. The matching joins at most $\\close-1$ customers to $\\open$\n   456\tstacks and stops at $\\open-1$ edges. Since 1 October 2026 these are the default\n   457\tin both of our solvers, the MOSP customer search and the graph pathwidth solver,\n   458\tin C and in Python; the published rules remain behind a flag.\n   459\t\n   460\t\\begin{table}[t]\n   461\t\\centering\\small\n   462\t\\caption{The cost of the repair: nodes under the published and the repaired\n   463\trules, in paired runs of the same decision in one worker. Totals over the pairs\n   464\tthat finished under both settings; a censored pair says nothing about cost.\n   465\tMOSP rows refute the optimum minus one; the graph row is whole descents at\n   466\t120\\,s each.}\n   467\t\\label{tab:cost}\n   468\t\\resizebox{\\linewidth}{!}{\\input{tables/cost}}\n   469\t\\end{table}\n   470\t\n   471\tTable~\\ref{tab:cost} gives the cost in nodes. The repair changes tree sizes by\n   472\tat most a few percent and never changed an answer. Per node it costs about 2\\% in\n   473\tthe MOSP C engine, measured on six hard instances at 75--125 customers. The\n   474\tpublished test passes and the matching then fails on 0.07--0.27\\% of\n   475\tdefinite-move candidates, a rate that rises with size.\n   476\t\n   477\t\\paragraph{Re-certification.} Of the 6,374 certified optima in our MOSP corpus,\n   478\t6,259 do not depend on the customer search: they rest on an exact subset-lattice\n   479\tcomputation, a checked DRAT refutation, an earlier SAT search, or a lower bound\n   480\tequal to the value. The other 115 rested on the customer search under the\n   481\tpublished rules. The repaired solver has re-refuted the value minus one on 112\n   482\tof them, and no value changed. Table~\\ref{tab:split} gives the seven hardest,\n   483\t125-customer instances at densities 2 and 4, which needed a split of the root\n   484\tover many cores; three are still running at the time of writing, and keep their\n   485\tvalues as verified upper bounds until they finish.\\footnote{\\texttt{python -m\n   486\tpaper2.solver\\_fix\\_split -{}-summary}; the split is proved sound in\n   487\t\\leanfile{Search/Split.lean}.}\n   488\t\n   489\t\\begin{table}[t]\n   490\t\\centering\\small\n   491\t\\caption{Re-certification of the hardest corpus values under the repaired rules,\n   492\tby a split of the root into independent tasks. \\emph{$k$}: the value minus one,\n   493\twhich must be refuted. For an instance in flight, the nodes and core-hours are\n   494\tthose of the finished tasks so far.}\n   495\t\\label{tab:split}\n   496\t\\input{tables/split}\n   497\t\\end{table}\n   498\t\n   499\t\\paragraph{Certificates.} A refutation by the search is a claim about a tree of\n   500\t$10^{11}$ nodes at 125 customers, and nothing in Theorem~\\ref{thm:main} lets a\n   501\treader check that the code ran the search it models. So the search emits a\n   502\tcertificate: the tree, every pruning step with its rule and witness, and for\n   503\teach definite and better step the matching of Theorem~\\ref{thm:matching}. An\n   504\tindependent checker, which shares no code with the search, recomputes every\n   505\tpremise from the matrix, verifies each matching (distinct customers, distinct\n   506\tstacks, each stack newly opened by its customer, at least $\\open-1$ edges),\n   507\trequires every chain of covers to end at a child or an old move with no cycle,\n   508\tand replays the tree. Each accepted link is a Lean theorem; that an acyclic\n   509\tcovering by such links is sound is a short paper argument, not yet formal.\n   510\tTable~\\ref{tab:certs} gives the results on the corpus at 9--75 customers.\n   511\tEvery refutation emitted within 60\\,s verifies, 6,275 of 6,286 under the\n   512\t\\texttt{csearch} configuration, and none is rejected; the 35.5\\,MB of\n   513\tcertificates check in 147\\,s. On the graph of Counterexample~\\ref{cex:definite}\n   514\trooted at $\\{2\\}$, the published rules emit an exhaustive tree that claims no\n   515\tsolution; a checker of the published premises accepts it, and ours rejects it,\n   516\teven with a largest matching attached (one edge where two are needed).\n   517\t\n   518\t\\begin{table}[t]\n   519\t\\centering\\small\n   520\t\\caption{Certificates for the repaired rules on the certified corpus at 9--75\n   521\tcustomers, refuting the optimum minus one, 60\\,s emission budget.\n   522\t\\emph{Unknown}: emission did not finish. Below 41 customers the\n   523\t\\texttt{default} rows match \\texttt{csearch} and are omitted.}\n   524\t\\label{tab:certs}\n   525\t\\resizebox{\\linewidth}{!}{\\input{tables/certificates}}\n   526\t\\end{table}\n   527\t\n   528\t\\subsection{A dataset with certified pathwidth}\n   529\t\\label{sec:dataset}\n   530\t\n   531\tSection~\\ref{sec:equiv} makes one certified pathwidth an exact answer to every\n   532\tproblem in Table~1's exact core: pathwidth, vertex separation and the vertex\n   533\tseparator game take the width $w$; MOSP, gate matrix layout with multiple\n   534\tfolding, one-dimensional logic, interval thickness, narrowness and node search\n   535\ttake $w+1$. We therefore built one dataset from every benchmark collection of\n   536\tthose problems that we could obtain, and the MOSP corpus. The unit is the\n   537\tisomorphism class of the graph, the MOSP graph for a matrix instance: two\n   538\tinstances with isomorphic graphs have the same value under every problem, so the\n   539\tdataset holds one record per class, with its members and their isomorphism maps.\n   540\tEach record carries the graph, the width, its provenance (\\emph{refutation},\n   541\t\\emph{bound} or \\emph{upper bound}), a witness layout of exactly that width and,\n   542\tfor a bound, a minor certificate that replays.\n   543\t\n   544\t\\begin{table}[t]\n   545\t\\centering\\small\n   546\t\\caption{The dataset by collection. \\emph{Classes}: isomorphism classes within\n   547\tthe collection (nauty certificates; 78 graphs above 5,000 vertices by file\n   548\tcontent only). \\emph{Owned}: classes whose first member is in this collection,\n   549\tso the column sums to the dataset's class count. The provenance columns count\n   550\towned classes with a witness layout.}\n   551\t\\label{tab:dataset}\n   552\t\\resizebox{\\linewidth}{!}{\\input{tables/dataset}}\n   553\t\\end{table}\n   554\t\n   555\tTable~\\ref{tab:dataset} gives the counts. Twenty collections, 21,754 instance\n   556\tfiles, reduce to 17,714 classes: the MOSP collections\n   557\t\\citep{SmithGent2005,FaggioliBentivoglio1998,ChuStuckey2009,CarvalhoSoma2015,Frinhani2018},\n   558\televen VLSI gate matrix circuits \\citep{OliveiraLorena2002}, the VSPLIB sets\n   559\t\\citep{Duarte2012} and the Small graphs \\citep{MartiCamposPinana2008}, the Rome\n   560\tgraphs \\citep{DiBattista1997}, and treewidth collections used as pathwidth\n   561\tbenchmarks \\citep{TreewidthLIB,freetdi,PACE2016,PACE2017}. Of the classes, 16,087\n   562\tcarry a certified pathwidth with a witness layout and 862 a witnessed upper\n   563\tbound. An independent checker, sharing no code with the solvers, re-reads all\n   564\t20,987 member files with its own readers, maps each onto its record by\n   565\tisomorphism, re-measures every witness and replays every bound certificate, and\n   566\treports no failure on the 16,949 records.\\footnote{\\texttt{python\n   567\tpaper2/dataset\\_check.py -{}-sources}; builder \\texttt{python -m paper2.dataset}.}\n   568\t\n   569\tAgainst published values: all 84 Small graphs agree with the pathwidths of\n   570\t\\citet[Tables~4--5]{Mallach2018}, and ten of the eleven VLSI circuits are\n   571\tcertified at their published best-known track counts \\citep[Table~I]{OliveiraLorena2002};\n   572\tthe eleventh, W4 with 202 nets, is open at 28 against 27. One caution about MOSP\n   573\tbenchmark files emerged: the same public repository ships the Carvalho and Soma\n   574\tinstances as customers by patterns and the Chu and Stuckey instances beside them\n   575\tas patterns by customers, and only published densities and values tell them\n   576\tapart. Read the wrong way round, an instance certifies at a different value.\n   577\t\n   578\tWhat is left is priced. One more budget step for every open class that fits our\n   579\tengine (1,024 vertices per component) costs at most 500 core-hours, and on the\n   580\tRome graphs each such step has closed about 40\\% of what it attempted. The\n   581\tlargest MOSP instances, 400 to 1,000 customers, and the 154 classes above 1,024\n   582\tvertices are beyond an exact search. Refutation-certified records rest on the\n   583\trepaired search; for those within reach of the certificate emitter, the proof\n   584\tobject above is the route by which a third party can check them without our code.\n","numLines":585,"startLine":1,"totalLines":585}}}
