{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_017Xdoo95nhvaUn5GPo7enot","type":"tool_result","content":"......                                                                   [100%]\n6 passed in 0.38s\n         Random-125-125-2-4_0                    24    23    refuted               1,686    4.98 × 1011            154.1\n         Random-125-125-4-5_0                    46    45    refuted                 616    7.96 × 1010             26.1\n         Random-125-125-4-2_0                    57    56    refuted                 842    7.64 × 1010             30.2\n         Random-125-125-2-1_0                    24    23    in flight               197    8.24 × 1010             51.0\n         Random-125-125-2-5_0                    20    19    in flight               168    6.97 × 1010             45.6\n         Random-125-125-4-4_0                    51    50    in flight               745    1.28 × 1011             67.1\n\n\n5     Computational results and a dataset\n5.1     The revised algorithm in practice\nThe revised search changes two tests. The definite move fires on the first playable q that passes the published\ntest and then the matching of Theorem 4.7; a q that fails the matching is passed over. The better move cites\nq for r only if the same matching succeeds at cl(S ∪ {r}). The matching joins at most close − 1 customers to\nopen stacks and stops at open − 1 edges. Since 1 October 2026 these are the default in both of our solvers,\nthe MOSP customer search and the graph pathwidth solver, in C and in Python; the published rules remain\nbehind a flag.\n     Table 5.1 gives the cost in nodes. The repair changes tree sizes by at most a few percent and never\nchanged an answer. Per node it costs about 2% in the MOSP C engine, measured on six hard instances at\n75–125 customers. The published test passes and the matching then fails on 0.07–0.27% of definite-move\ncandidates, a rate that rises with size.\n\nwithout our code.\n\n\n6     Conclusion\nPlaceholder. Section 6 is written last, once Sections 1–5 are final. It is to say what the complex is, what\nis proved and where, and what remains open: the missing Table 1 reference (Kashiwabara and Fujisawa\n1979); the Table 1 claims that turned out false or only bands; LaPaugh’s es = pes, stated and not formalised;\nthe three re-certifications still running; the certificate’s acceptance condition, argued on paper and not in\nLean; and the gap between pathwidth and treewidth on trees of cliques, where no degree or clique bound\ncan close it.\n\nData, code and proofs\nEverything this paper reports can be checked from its repository, URL to be added on release . The repos-\nitory holds:\n\n\n\n                                                          21\n\f    • the Lean 4 development, with every theorem the paper names (Appendix A) and an axiom check that\n      covers all of them;\n\n    • the source of both solvers, the open-stacks customer search and the graph pathwidth solver, in C and\n      in Python, with the published and the repaired rules;\n\n    • the dataset of Section 5.2, the certificates of Section 5.1 and the independent checkers of both;\n\n    • the bibliometric data of Section 3: the cached OpenAlex responses, the relevance labels and the\n      mechanical rule;\n\n    • a script for every table, figure and number in the paper, each of which regenerates it from the recorded\n      data.","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"1323e98e-623f-4534-a362-41a379cbd2ab","uuid":"2525bd76-fc9f-455d-a6c0-35589e07dc6f","timestamp":"2026-10-06T13:54:14.592Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"......                                                                   [100%]\n6 passed in 0.38s\n         Random-125-125-2-4_0                    24    23    refuted               1,686    4.98 × 1011            154.1\n         Random-125-125-4-5_0                    46    45    refuted                 616    7.96 × 1010             26.1\n         Random-125-125-4-2_0                    57    56    refuted                 842    7.64 × 1010             30.2\n         Random-125-125-2-1_0                    24    23    in flight               197    8.24 × 1010             51.0\n         Random-125-125-2-5_0                    20    19    in flight               168    6.97 × 1010             45.6\n         Random-125-125-4-4_0                    51    50    in flight               745    1.28 × 1011             67.1\n\n\n5     Computational results and a dataset\n5.1     The revised algorithm in practice\nThe revised search changes two tests. The definite move fires on the first playable q that passes the published\ntest and then the matching of Theorem 4.7; a q that fails the matching is passed over. The better move cites\nq for r only if the same matching succeeds at cl(S ∪ {r}). The matching joins at most close − 1 customers to\nopen stacks and stops at open − 1 edges. Since 1 October 2026 these are the default in both of our solvers,\nthe MOSP customer search and the graph pathwidth solver, in C and in Python; the published rules remain\nbehind a flag.\n     Table 5.1 gives the cost in nodes. The repair changes tree sizes by at most a few percent and never\nchanged an answer. Per node it costs about 2% in the MOSP C engine, measured on six hard instances at\n75–125 customers. The published test passes and the matching then fails on 0.07–0.27% of definite-move\ncandidates, a rate that rises with size.\n\nwithout our code.\n\n\n6     Conclusion\nPlaceholder. Section 6 is written last, once Sections 1–5 are final. It is to say what the complex is, what\nis proved and where, and what remains open: the missing Table 1 reference (Kashiwabara and Fujisawa\n1979); the Table 1 claims that turned out false or only bands; LaPaugh’s es = pes, stated and not formalised;\nthe three re-certifications still running; the certificate’s acceptance condition, argued on paper and not in\nLean; and the gap between pathwidth and treewidth on trees of cliques, where no degree or clique bound\ncan close it.\n\nData, code and proofs\nEverything this paper reports can be checked from its repository, URL to be added on release . The repos-\nitory holds:\n\n\n\n                                                          21\n\f    • the Lean 4 development, with every theorem the paper names (Appendix A) and an axiom check that\n      covers all of them;\n\n    • the source of both solvers, the open-stacks customer search and the graph pathwidth solver, in C and\n      in Python, with the published and the repaired rules;\n\n    • the dataset of Section 5.2, the certificates of Section 5.1 and the independent checkers of both;\n\n    • the bibliometric data of Section 3: the cached OpenAlex responses, the relevance labels and the\n      mechanical rule;\n\n    • a script for every table, figure and number in the paper, each of which regenerates it from the recorded\n      data.","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false}}
